   "id": "Q_GEN_ANAES_CPR_002",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "topic": "CPR / BLS-ACLS basics",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Allowing complete chest recoil between compressions during CPR is emphasised primarily because it:",
   "options": [
    "Prevents rib fractures during compressions",
    "Lowers intrathoracic pressure so blood returns to the heart, improving cardiac output and coronary perfusion",
    "Reduces the number of ventilations required",
    "Increases intrathoracic pressure to push blood forward"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Complete chest recoil lets the thorax re-expand, dropping intrathoracic pressure so blood returns to the heart (venous return); this refills the ventricles and sustains the coronary perfusion pressure that drives ROSC. Leaning on the chest prevents recoil, raises intrathoracic pressure, cuts venous return and worsens outcomes. It does not primarily prevent fractures or reduce ventilation need.",
   "reference": "AHA Guidelines for CPR and ECC 2020, High-Quality CPR Components",
   "verified": "Y",
