  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_ANAES_LA_011",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "topic": "Local anaesthetics (max doses, toxicity)",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Local anaesthetics produce conduction block mainly by:",
   "options": [
    "Opening voltage-gated potassium channels",
    "Enhancing GABA-A receptor activity",
    "Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels from the intracellular side, preferentially in the open/inactivated state",
    "Blocking voltage-gated calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Local anaesthetics bind the alpha-subunit of the voltage-gated sodium channel from its inner (cytoplasmic) aspect, preventing the sodium influx that generates and propagates the action potential. They act preferentially on channels in the open and inactivated states (state-dependent block). The drug must first cross the membrane in its un-ionized form and then act as the ionized cation inside the axon. They do not work through potassium channels, GABA receptors, or calcium channels as the primary mechanism.",
   "reference": "Miller's Anesthesia 9e, Ch. Pharmacology of Local Anesthetics",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
