   "concept": "Trivalent arsenicals (arsenite) bind covalently to the vicinal dithiol (two adjacent -SH) groups of reduced lipoic acid (dihydrolipoamide), inactivating both the pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complexes; pyruvate and lactate therefore accumulate. This is why dimercaprol (BAL), itself a dithiol, is the antidote — it competes with the tissue dithiols for the arsenic. Arsenite is not a TPP competitor, and neither NAD+ nor coenzyme A is its direct molecular target.",
   "reference": "Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry 8e, Ch. Chapter 9: TCA Cycle & Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (Arsenite/lipoic acid)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
