   "concept": "Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency (often X-linked E1-alpha) blocks the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, so pyruvate builds up and is shunted to lactate, producing congenital lactic acidosis with neurological damage (the brain depends heavily on glucose oxidation). Because the block is BEFORE the electron-transport-dependent steps, both pyruvate and lactate rise and the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio stays normal (a high ratio suggests a respiratory-chain defect). Von Gierke disease also causes lactic acidosis but with hepatomegaly and hypoglycaemia; MCAD is a fatty-acid oxidation defect; OTC deficiency causes hyperammonaemia.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Chapter 18: Oxidation of Pyruvate (PDH deficiency, lactic acidosis)",
