   "concept": "To use ketone bodies, tissues must first re-activate acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA using thiophorase (SCOT), which transfers CoA from succinyl-CoA. The liver lacks thiophorase, so it cannot re-activate acetoacetate and therefore exports ketones rather than consuming them, ensuring fuel reaches the brain, heart and muscle during fasting. Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase and thiolase are present in the liver; HMG-CoA synthase is actually the ketogenic (synthetic) enzyme, so its presence, not absence, is what makes the liver ketogenic.",
