  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_FMT_ASPHYXIA_010",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Asphyxial deaths (hanging, drowning)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Compared with the mark of hanging, the ligature mark of ligature strangulation is typically:",
   "options": [
    "Oblique, incomplete and situated high on the neck above the thyroid cartilage",
    "Horizontal (transverse), continuous and completely encircling the neck, and low at or below the thyroid cartilage",
    "Always completely absent",
    "V-shaped with its apex behind the ear"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "In ligature strangulation the constricting force is applied horizontally by an assailant, so the mark is transverse, usually continuous and completely encircling the neck, and lies low — at or below the level of the thyroid cartilage. In hanging, by contrast, the mark is oblique, incomplete (interrupted at the site of the knot) and high up above the thyroid, directed toward the point of suspension. Direction, completeness and level thus reliably separate strangulation from hanging.",
   "reference": "Modi's A Textbook of Medical Jurisprudence and Toxicology 26e, Ch. Strangulation",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
