  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_FMT_ASPHYXIA_014",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Asphyxial deaths (hanging, drowning)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In fresh-water drowning, absorption of the hypotonic aspirated water from the alveoli into the circulation classically produces:",
   "options": [
    "Haemoconcentration with hypernatraemia",
    "Haemodilution, hypervolaemia, haemolysis with hyperkalaemia, and ventricular fibrillation",
    "No appreciable change in circulating blood volume",
    "Marked pulmonary oedema from fluid drawn osmotically into the alveoli"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Fresh water is hypotonic relative to blood, so water aspirated into the alveoli is rapidly absorbed across the alveolar-capillary membrane into the circulation, causing haemodilution and hypervolaemia within a couple of minutes. Dilution plus haemolysis of red cells releases potassium, and the resulting hyperkalaemia together with anoxia precipitates ventricular fibrillation, so death may be very rapid. This contrasts with salt-water drowning, where hypertonic water draws plasma into the alveoli producing haemoconcentration and pulmonary oedema.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Drowning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_FMT_ASPHYXIA_015",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Asphyxial deaths (hanging, drowning)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Relative to fresh-water drowning, salt-water (sea-water) drowning characteristically produces:",
   "options": [
    "Haemoconcentration, hypovolaemia, hypernatraemia and marked pulmonary oedema",
    "Haemodilution with hyperkalaemia",
    "Immediate ventricular fibrillation as the usual mode of death",
    "A fall in the serum sodium and chloride concentrations"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Sea water is hypertonic (about 3% salt). Water aspirated into the alveoli therefore draws plasma osmotically OUT of the pulmonary capillaries into the alveolar spaces, producing intense pulmonary oedema together with haemoconcentration, hypovolaemia and hypernatraemia. Death tends to be somewhat slower than in fresh-water drowning, resulting from hypoxia and pulmonary oedema over several minutes rather than the rapid ventricular fibrillation of fresh-water drowning. This osmotic contrast is the basis of the Gettler chloride test.",
   "reference": "Parikh's Textbook of Medical Jurisprudence, Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, Ch. Drowning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
