  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_FMT_ASPHYXIA_018",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Asphyxial deaths (hanging, drowning)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The diatom (acid-digestion) test for antemortem drowning depends chiefly on which property of diatoms, and how is a positive result in the marrow of a closed long bone interpreted?",
   "options": [
    "Their calcium-carbonate shells dissolve in acid, so any diatoms recovered must be postmortem contaminants",
    "Their silica frustules resist strong-acid digestion; diatoms in the marrow of a closed bone imply they were carried there by the circulation during active (antemortem) drowning",
    "Diatoms are proteinaceous and are destroyed by acid digestion, so their ABSENCE proves drowning",
    "Diatoms enter closed bones equally by passive diffusion after death, so the test cannot indicate antemortem drowning"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Diatoms are microscopic algae whose cell wall (frustule) is made of silica and resists the strong nitric/sulphuric acid digestion used to destroy the surrounding soft tissue in the test. For diatoms to reach the marrow of a 'closed' bone such as the femur they must have been inhaled with water and distributed by a beating heart — so their presence there indicates antemortem drowning. Reliable interpretation requires matching the diatom species and counts with those of the drowning medium and rigorous laboratory controls, since contamination and mere presence in the lungs are far less conclusive.",
   "reference": "Modi's A Textbook of Medical Jurisprudence and Toxicology 26e, Ch. Drowning (Diatom Test)",
   "verified": "Y",
