   "id": "Q_GEN_FMT_ASPHYXIA_019",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Asphyxial deaths (hanging, drowning)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A labourer is pinned across the chest under a collapsed wall for several minutes and dies. The face, neck and upper chest are deeply blue-purple with florid petechiae and marked subconjunctival haemorrhage, sharply demarcated at the level of compression. This 'masque ecchymotique' is characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Carbon monoxide poisoning",
    "Manual strangulation (throttling)",
    "Traumatic (crush/compression) asphyxia",
    "Fresh-water drowning"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Traumatic (crush or compression) asphyxia occurs when the chest, or chest and upper abdomen, is forcibly compressed — crowd crushes, vehicle or wall collapse, cave-ins — preventing respiratory movement. The abrupt rise in intrathoracic and cranial venous pressure drives blood backward into the valveless veins of the head and neck, producing intense congestion, cyanosis, countless petechiae over the face, neck and upper chest, and subconjunctival haemorrhage — the 'ecchymotic mask' (masque ecchymotique of Morestin), sharply bordered at the compression line. Death is from inability to breathe, not from vascular occlusion.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Traumatic Asphyxia",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
