   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "topic": "Death, postmortem changes (rigor/livor/algor)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The muscular stiffening of rigor mortis is produced by which underlying biochemical change?",
   "options": [
    "Depletion of ATP, so that actin and myosin remain locked together as inextensible actomyosin",
    "Excess ATP that drives continuous active muscle contraction after death",
    "Coagulation of muscle proteins by lactic-acid-induced heat",
    "Bacterial gas production that mechanically splints the muscles"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "During life ATP keeps the actin and myosin filaments dissociated, allowing muscle to relax. After death glycogen is consumed and ATP can no longer be regenerated; once ATP falls below roughly 85 percent of normal, actin and myosin bind irreversibly into rigid actomyosin, and the muscle becomes stiff and slightly shortened. Rigor passes off only when autolytic and putrefactive enzymes break down the actomyosin. It is thus an ATP-depletion phenomenon, not active contraction or bacterial gas.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Rigor Mortis",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
