   "topic": "Death, postmortem changes (rigor/livor/algor)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A body recovered from a house fire is heavily charred and lies in a flexed 'pugilistic (boxer's) attitude'. What is the correct interpretation of this posture?",
   "options": [
    "Heat-induced coagulation and shortening of muscles, with flexors overpowering the extensors",
    "Cadaveric spasm proving the victim was fighting the flames while conscious",
    "Ordinary rigor mortis that developed after the fire was extinguished",
    "Deliberate positioning of the body by an assailant before ignition"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Exposure to intense heat (above roughly 65 degrees Celsius) coagulates and denatures muscle proteins, producing heat stiffening. Because the bulkier flexor muscles shorten more than the extensors, the limbs assume the flexed 'pugilistic' or boxer's attitude. Crucially, this posture develops whether the person was alive or already dead when burnt, so it has NO value in deciding whether the victim was alive in the fire. It is not cadaveric spasm and not ordinary rigor mortis.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Injuries from Heat and Thanatology",
