   "topic": "Death, postmortem changes (rigor/livor/algor)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In which of the following circumstances would rigor mortis be expected to appear UNUSUALLY early and also pass off quickly?",
   "options": [
    "Death from massive haemorrhage in a person kept in a cold room",
    "Death from strychnine poisoning after prolonged convulsions",
    "Death of a healthy, well-rested young adult in a cool environment",
    "Death of a well-nourished person immersed in cold water"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The onset and duration of rigor depend largely on how much ATP and glycogen remain in the muscle at death and on the temperature. When muscles are exhausted before death — as after strychnine or tetanus convulsions, violent exertion, high fever or an exhausting illness — ATP is already depleted, so rigor develops rapidly and also passes off quickly. Cold environments and a rested, well-nourished state delay its onset and prolong it. Hence strychnine convulsions produce early, short-lasting rigor.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Rigor Mortis",
