   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The colour of postmortem staining can hint at the cause of death. Methaemoglobin-forming poisons — nitrites, nitrates, potassium chlorate, aniline, nitrobenzene and acetanilide — turn the blood, and hence the lividity, dark brown (chocolate). Carbon monoxide and cyanide give a cherry-red or bright pink hue, hydrogen sulphide and early putrefaction give a greenish discolouration, and simple asphyxia gives deep bluish-purple lividity. Recognising the brown tint points toward a methaemoglobinaemia-producing agent.",
   "reference": "Modi's A Textbook of Medical Jurisprudence and Toxicology 26e, Ch. Postmortem Lividity",
