   "topic": "Death, postmortem changes (rigor/livor/algor)",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "At autopsy an incision is made into a purple discolouration of the skin. Which finding indicates postmortem lividity rather than a bruise (contusion)?",
   "options": [
    "Blood lies free within the vessels and washes away, leaving the tissues uninfiltrated",
    "Blood is clotted, infiltrates the subcutaneous tissues, and cannot be washed off",
    "There is swelling and elevation of the overlying skin",
    "The colour changes progressively through green and yellow over several days"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Lividity is intravascular: on incision the blood remains inside the vessels and can be wiped or washed away, the cut surface is clean, the tissues are not infiltrated and there is no swelling. A bruise is extravascular: blood has escaped into and coagulated within the tissues, so on incision it infiltrates the subcutaneous tissue, cannot be washed off, is often swollen or elevated, and its colour evolves over days if the person survived. These features reliably separate hypostasis from a true contusion.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Postmortem Lividity and Bruises",
