   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Livor mortis develops as gravity draws blood into the dependent capillaries and small vessels. Wherever the body presses firmly against a supporting surface, or where tight clothing or a ligature compresses the skin, the underlying capillaries are squeezed shut and blood cannot pool there, so those areas stay pale — 'contact pallor' or contact flattening. The pattern of these pale pressure marks helps indicate the position of the body after death and the presence of constricting objects.",
