   "concept": "Blowflies and flesh flies are attracted to a body within minutes to hours and lay eggs in the natural orifices and wounds. The eggs hatch into first-instar larvae (maggots), which grow through successive instars, pupate, and emerge as adults on a known, temperature-dependent timetable. By identifying the fly species and the developmental stage of the oldest and largest larvae or pupae, and correcting for temperature, an entomologist estimates the minimum postmortem interval. The mere number of adult flies, or the ambient humidity alone, cannot date the death.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Estimation of Time Since Death",
   "verified": "Y",
