    "A progressive rise in the potassium concentration",
    "A steady rise in the glucose concentration",
    "A rapid rise in the sodium concentration"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "After death potassium leaks out of the retinal and other ocular cells into the vitreous humour, so its concentration rises steadily and fairly predictably with the postmortem interval, usefully up to about 100 hours. Because the vitreous is anatomically isolated and resists putrefaction, it gives more reliable postmortem chemistry than blood. In contrast, vitreous sodium and chloride tend to fall and glucose falls after death; it is the RISE in potassium that is applied to estimate the time since death.",
   "reference": "Modi's A Textbook of Medical Jurisprudence and Toxicology 26e, Ch. Estimation of Time Since Death",
   "verified": "Y",
