   "stem": "After HIV enters a CD4 cell, which molecular event is directly catalysed by reverse transcriptase and forms the target of nucleoside analogues such as zidovudine?",
   "options": [
    "Cleavage of the gag-pol polyprotein into mature enzymes",
    "Insertion of proviral DNA into the host chromosome",
    "Synthesis of double-stranded DNA from the viral single-stranded RNA template",
    "Budding of assembled virions through the plasma membrane"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Reverse transcriptase is an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase that copies the (+)ssRNA genome into complementary DNA and then double-stranded DNA, while its RNase-H activity degrades the original RNA template; this step is inhibited by NRTIs such as zidovudine and by NNRTIs. Polyprotein cleavage is the job of protease, chromosomal insertion is performed by integrase, and budding is a late assembly event — none of these is catalysed by reverse transcriptase.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. HIV replication cycle",
   "verified": "Y",
