   "id": "Q_GEN_OPHTH_GLAUCOMA_003",
   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "topic": "Glaucoma",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "In the normal human eye, the majority of aqueous humour drains from the anterior chamber via which pathway?",
   "options": [
    "Uveoscleral (ciliary body to suprachoroidal) route",
    "Trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm",
    "Diffusion across the corneal stroma",
    "Active pumping by the retinal pigment epithelium"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The conventional (trabecular) pathway accounts for roughly 80-90% of aqueous outflow: aqueous passes through the trabecular meshwork into Schlemm's canal, then via collector channels and aqueous veins to the episcleral veins. The uveoscleral (unconventional) route drains the remaining 10-20%, is relatively pressure-independent, and is the pathway enhanced by prostaglandin analogues; the cornea and RPE play no drainage role.",
   "reference": "AK Khurana Comprehensive Ophthalmology 8e, Ch. Glaucoma (Aqueous outflow)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
