   "stem": "A man sustained blunt trauma to one eye from a cricket ball several years ago; that eye now has raised IOP, and gonioscopy shows an abnormally deep, widened ciliary-body band with a torn iris root over part of the angle. The diagnosis is:",
   "options": [
    "Neovascular glaucoma",
    "Phacolytic glaucoma",
    "Angle-recession glaucoma",
    "Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Blunt ocular trauma can tear the face of the ciliary body between the longitudinal and circular muscle fibres (angle recession), seen on gonioscopy as a segment of abnormally widened and deepened ciliary-body band with a torn iris root. Associated scarring of the trabecular meshwork reduces outflow, and glaucoma may develop months to years later as an open-angle disease, so long-term IOP follow-up after blunt trauma is essential. Involvement of more than 180-270 degrees of the angle increases the risk; the mechanism is distinct from neovascular, phacolytic and pseudoexfoliative glaucomas.",
   "reference": "AK Khurana Comprehensive Ophthalmology 8e, Ch. Post-traumatic (angle-recession) glaucoma",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
