   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "topic": "Refractive errors",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "During plane-mirror streak retinoscopy performed at 1 metre, the retinal reflex moves in the SAME direction as the streak (a \"with\" movement). Before applying the working-distance correction, this indicates the eye is:",
   "options": [
    "Myopic greater than 1 D — neutralise with minus lenses",
    "Exactly 1 D myopic, at the point of reversal",
    "Irregularly astigmatic and cannot be neutralised",
    "Hypermetropic, emmetropic, or myopic less than 1 D — neutralise with plus lenses"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A \"with\" movement means the far point lies behind the retinoscope peephole, so the eye is hypermetropic, emmetropic, or myopic by less than the working-distance dioptre, and plus lenses are added to neutralise it. An \"against\" movement (myopia greater than the working distance) needs minus lenses. At neutralisation the far point lies at the peephole; the working-distance value (1 D at 1 m) is then subtracted to give the true error.",
   "reference": "AK Khurana Comprehensive Ophthalmology 8e, Ch. Retinoscopy",
   "verified": "Y",
