   "subject": "Pathology",
   "topic": "Acute & chronic inflammation, healing/repair",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Alternatively activated (M2) macrophages, induced by interleukins 4 and 13, differ from classically activated (M1) macrophages chiefly in that M2 macrophages predominantly:",
   "options": [
    "Produce nitric oxide and reactive oxygen species to kill microbes",
    "Promote tissue repair, angiogenesis and fibrosis with anti-inflammatory IL-10 and TGF-beta",
    "Are activated by interferon-gamma and microbial lipopolysaccharide",
    "Secrete IL-12 to drive Th1 differentiation"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "M1 (classically activated) macrophages are induced by interferon-gamma from Th1 and NK cells and by microbial products such as LPS acting through Toll-like receptors; they secrete IL-1, IL-12 and TNF, generate reactive oxygen species and nitric oxide, and are microbicidal and pro-inflammatory. M2 (alternatively activated) macrophages are induced by IL-4 and IL-13 from Th2 cells; they secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta, dampen inflammation, and promote tissue repair, angiogenesis and fibrosis. The M1-to-M2 shift helps terminate inflammation and begin healing.",
   "reference": "Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 10e, Ch. Chapter 3: Inflammation and Repair",
