   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A hypoalbuminemic patient with chronic kidney disease on phenytoin has a total phenytoin level reported as 6 mcg/mL (reference 10-20) but shows clear signs of phenytoin toxicity. What is the best interpretation?",
   "options": [
    "Total phenytoin reliably reflects free drug even in hypoalbuminemia, so toxicity is unrelated to phenytoin",
    "The dose should be increased because the total level is subtherapeutic",
    "Phenytoin is not protein-bound, so albumin is irrelevant to interpretation",
    "In hypoalbuminemia/uraemia the unbound (free) fraction rises, so a low total level can correspond to a therapeutic or toxic free concentration; measure free phenytoin or apply a correction"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Phenytoin is about 90% albumin-bound, and only the free fraction is pharmacologically active; in hypoalbuminemia and uraemia (which also displaces the drug), the free fraction rises so a low measured total level can hide a therapeutic or toxic free concentration. Therefore option D is correct: measure free phenytoin or use a correction (e.g. Sheiner-Tozer) rather than raising the dose. Options A and C are false about protein binding, and option B would dangerously worsen toxicity.",
   "reference": "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics 14e, Ch. Phenytoin protein binding and therapeutic drug monitoring",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
