   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_001",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Minimum Alveolar Concentration (MAC) is the alveolar concentration of an inhalational anaesthetic at 1 atmosphere that abolishes movement to a standard surgical (skin) incision in 50% of patients. Clinically, MAC is used as an index of the agent's:",
   "options": [
    "Speed of induction",
    "Potency",
    "Blood solubility",
    "Rate of hepatic metabolism"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "MAC is inversely proportional to potency — a low MAC (halothane ~0.75%) marks a highly potent agent, whereas a high MAC (nitrous oxide >100%) marks a weak one. MAC correlates with the oil:gas partition coefficient (Meyer-Overton lipid-solubility rule), NOT with the blood:gas coefficient, which instead governs speed of induction and recovery. Metabolism of modern agents is minimal and does not define MAC. Hence potency (B) is the correct answer, and the other options describe unrelated properties.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_002",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Assertion (A): The MAC of an inhalational anaesthetic is lower in an elderly patient than in an infant. Reason (R): MAC rises progressively from infancy through to old age. Select the correct option.",
   "options": [
    "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A",
    "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A",
    "A is true but R is false",
    "A is false but R is true"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "MAC is highest in infancy (around 6 months) and declines steadily with advancing age, so the elderly need less anaesthetic — the Assertion is TRUE. The Reason is FALSE because it states the opposite trend; MAC does not increase toward old age, it decreases. MAC is also lowered by hypothermia, pregnancy, opioids, benzodiazepines, alpha-2 agonists and acute alcohol intake, and is raised by hyperthermia, chronic alcoholism and hypernatraemia. Since A is true but R is false, the answer is C.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics (MAC and factors affecting it)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_003",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "An inhalational anaesthetic with a LOW blood:gas partition coefficient (e.g. desflurane, nitrous oxide) is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "High potency",
    "Rapid induction and rapid recovery",
    "Extensive hepatic metabolism",
    "Marked skeletal muscle relaxation"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "A low blood:gas coefficient means the agent is poorly soluble in blood, so blood is quickly 'saturated' and the alveolar (hence brain) partial pressure rises fast — giving rapid induction and, because wash-out is equally quick, rapid recovery. This coefficient controls SPEED, not potency (that is the oil:gas coefficient / MAC). Highly soluble agents like methoxyflurane and ether give slow induction. Metabolism and muscle relaxation are unrelated to blood solubility, so only B is correct.",
   "reference": "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology 15e, Ch. General Anesthetics (uptake and distribution)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_004",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "During induction, delivering a high concentration of nitrous oxide together with a volatile agent such as sevoflurane accelerates the rise of the volatile agent's alveolar concentration. This phenomenon is termed the:",
   "options": [
    "Concentration effect",
    "Second gas effect",
    "Diffusion hypoxia",
    "Bohr effect"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The second gas effect: rapid, large-volume uptake of the high-concentration 'first gas' (nitrous oxide) into pulmonary blood shrinks alveolar gas volume, which concentrates the remaining 'second gas' (the volatile agent) and augments its inspiratory inflow — speeding its alveolar rise. It is a consequence of the concentration effect (which describes only the first gas itself). Diffusion hypoxia is the reverse process at emergence, and the Bohr effect concerns haemoglobin–oxygen affinity, so B is correct.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Inhalation Anesthetics (pharmacokinetics)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_005",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "At the end of a prolonged nitrous oxide anaesthetic, the patient is at risk of diffusion hypoxia. The most appropriate measure to prevent this is to:",
   "options": [
    "Continue nitrous oxide at a low flow rate",
    "Extubate immediately and leave the patient breathing room air",
    "Administer 100% oxygen for the first few minutes after discontinuing nitrous oxide",
    "Hyperventilate the patient with added carbon dioxide"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "When nitrous oxide is stopped, its large stored volume rapidly diffuses from blood back into the alveoli, diluting alveolar oxygen and CO2 and producing transient dilutional (diffusion) hypoxia. Delivering 100% oxygen for 3–5 minutes offsets this dilution and maintains arterial oxygenation. Continuing N2O or leaving the patient in room air worsens hypoxia, and adding CO2 is not indicated. Hence C is the correct preventive step; this risk is greatest after high-concentration, prolonged N2O use.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics (Nitrous oxide)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PHAR_CNS_ANES_006",
   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Prolonged or repeated exposure to nitrous oxide can cause megaloblastic anaemia and myeloneuropathy. This results from irreversible oxidation of the cobalt atom of vitamin B12, which inactivates:",
   "options": [
    "Methionine synthase",
    "Thymidylate synthase",
    "Dihydrofolate reductase",
    "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Nitrous oxide oxidises the cobalt of cobalamin (Co+ to Co3+), inactivating methionine synthase, the B12-dependent enzyme that converts homocysteine to methionine and regenerates tetrahydrofolate from methyl-THF. The resulting 'folate trap' impairs DNA synthesis, causing megaloblastic bone-marrow changes, and chronic exposure produces subacute combined degeneration of the cord. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase is the other B12 enzyme but is not the mediator here; DHFR and thymidylate synthase are folate enzymes not directly oxidised by N2O — so A is correct.",
   "reference": "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics 14e, Ch. General Anesthetics (Nitrous oxide toxicity)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
