   "subject": "Pharmacology",
   "topic": "General & local anaesthetics, opioids",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Assertion (A): The dose of adrenaline infiltrated into the surgical field should be strictly limited during halothane anaesthesia. Reason (R): Halothane sensitises the myocardium to the arrhythmogenic effects of catecholamines. Select the correct option.",
   "options": [
    "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A",
    "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A",
    "A is true but R is false",
    "A is false but R is true"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Halothane (more than other halogenated agents) sensitises the myocardium to catecholamines, so exogenous adrenaline can precipitate multifocal ventricular ectopics or ventricular tachycardia — this is exactly why the infiltrated adrenaline dose is capped during halothane. Both statements are true and the Reason directly and correctly explains the Assertion, so the answer is A. Halothane itself is a myocardial depressant, not a beta-agonist. Isoflurane and sevoflurane sensitise far less and are preferred when adrenaline is required.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics (Halothane, adverse effects)",
