   "topic": "Pharmacokinetics/dynamics, receptors",
   "part": "A",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A drug has an apparent volume of distribution of 40 L and an elimination rate constant (kel) of 0.15 per hour. What is its total body clearance?",
   "options": [
    "0.15 L/hr",
    "2.67 L/hr",
    "6 L/hr",
    "267 L/hr"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Clearance = kel x Vd = 0.15/hr x 40 L = 6 L/hr. Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time and links the first-order rate constant to the distribution volume. 2.67 L/hr wrongly divides Vd by kel; 267 L/hr wrongly divides kel into Vd (40/0.15); 0.15 L/hr confuses the rate constant with clearance. The correct product gives 6 L/hr.",
   "reference": "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology 15e, Ch. Pharmacokinetics (Clearance)",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
