   "topic": "Biostatistics: tests & distributions",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In a regression of blood pressure on age, the Pearson correlation coefficient is r = 0.6. What proportion of the variation in blood pressure is explained by age?",
   "options": [
    "60%, because r directly gives the explained variation",
    "80%, because it equals 1 minus r",
    "40%, because unexplained variation equals r",
    "36%, because the coefficient of determination r-squared = 0.36"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The coefficient of determination is r-squared, the fraction of the variance in the outcome explained by the predictor: r-squared = 0.6 x 0.6 = 0.36, i.e., 36% of the variation in blood pressure is explained by age (the remaining 64% is due to other factors and error). r itself (0.6) measures the strength and direction of the linear association but overstates 'explained variation'; the '1 minus r' and 'r as unexplained variation' options misuse the relationship. Squaring makes r-squared <= |r| when |r| < 1.",
   "reference": "Mahajan's Methods in Biostatistics 9e, Ch. coefficient of determination (r-squared) and regression",
   "verified": "Y",
