   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A population age-sex pyramid with a very broad base that tapers steeply towards the apex is MOST characteristic of which type of population?",
   "options": [
    "A constrictive (contracting) population with a low birth rate",
    "An expansive population with a high birth rate and a young age structure",
    "A stationary population where births roughly equal deaths",
    "An ageing population with a high proportion of the elderly"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The shape of a population pyramid encodes fertility and mortality history. A broad-based, steeply tapering (triangular) pyramid signals an expansive population: a high birth rate feeds large young cohorts at the base while higher mortality trims the older bars — typical of developing/high-fertility settings. A constrictive pyramid narrows at the base (declining births), and a stationary pyramid has near-vertical sides with a rounded top. Recognising the young base is key to understanding the demographic dividend, in which a bulge of working-age adults can boost the economy if productively employed.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
