    "Only the deaths in the 15–49 year age group are compared between the two populations"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Standardization removes the confounding effect of differing age structures so two populations' mortality can be fairly compared. In the DIRECT method you need the age-specific death rates of each study population; these observed rates are applied to a single 'standard' population's age distribution, generating the number of deaths that WOULD occur if both populations had that same structure, and hence directly comparable standardized rates. The INDIRECT method (option B, giving the Standardized Mortality Ratio) is used instead when age-specific rates of the study population are unavailable but the age distribution is known. Option A is the correct description of the direct method.",
