   "stem": "In India, the sex ratio is conventionally defined as the number of females per 1000 males. According to the 2011 Census, the overall sex ratio was approximately:",
   "options": [
    "1000 females per 1000 males",
    "1020 females per 1000 males",
    "943 females per 1000 males (Census 2011 — verify latest)",
    "870 females per 1000 males"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "In India the sex ratio is expressed as the number of females per 1000 males (the reciprocal of the convention used in some other countries). The 2011 Census recorded an overall sex ratio of about 943, while the child sex ratio (0–6 years) was lower at around 919, reflecting son-preference and sex-selective practices that the PC-PNDT Act seeks to curb. These are change-prone census-linked figures (the next full census will revise them) — always flag the census year and verify the latest available value before quoting.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_DEMOG_FP_016",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Life expectancy at birth in India is currently approximately (flag as change-prone — verify latest SRS):",
   "options": [
    "About 50 years",
    "About 55 years",
    "About 62 years",
    "About 69–70 years, with females generally living a few years longer than males"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Expectation of life at birth is the average number of years a newborn would live if current age-specific mortality persisted; it is derived from a life table and is a sensitive summary index of a population's overall mortality and development. In India it has risen dramatically from around 32 years at independence to roughly 69–70 years in recent SRS estimates, with female life expectancy typically a few years higher than male. This is a change-prone figure updated with each SRS abridged life-table release — verify the exact latest value rather than relying on a fixed number.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_DEMOG_FP_017",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A woman is counselled about the copper intrauterine device Cu-T 380A for spacing. What is its approved effective lifespan?",
   "options": [
    "Up to 10 years",
    "6 months",
    "1 year",
    "25 years"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The Cu-T 380A is a long-acting reversible copper-bearing intrauterine device approved for use for up to 10 years, making it one of the most cost-effective spacing methods. Copper IUDs act mainly by preventing FERTILIZATION: released copper ions are toxic to sperm and impair sperm motility/viability, and the device induces a sterile inflammatory endometrial reaction hostile to sperm and ova — they are NOT primarily abortifacient. Contraindications include pregnancy, active pelvic infection/PID, unexplained vaginal bleeding and distorted uterine cavity. Copper IUDs can also serve as emergency contraception if inserted within 5 days of unprotected intercourse.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_DEMOG_FP_018",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A 34-year-old woman requests combined oral contraceptive pills. Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to combined (oestrogen-containing) OCPs?",
   "options": [
    "Mild iron-deficiency anaemia",
    "Past history of confirmed venous thromboembolism (deep vein thrombosis / pulmonary embolism)",
    "A regular 28-day menstrual cycle",
    "History of a single uncomplicated urinary tract infection last year"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Combined OCPs work chiefly by suppressing the mid-cycle LH/FSH surge and thus inhibiting ovulation, plus thickening cervical mucus and altering the endometrium. Their oestrogen component is prothrombotic, so a past or current venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE), history of stroke or ischaemic heart disease, known thrombogenic mutations, active liver disease, current breast cancer, uncontrolled hypertension, migraine with aura, and smoking in a woman over 35 are recognised absolute contraindications (WHO MEC category 4). In such women progestogen-only methods or non-hormonal options such as the copper IUD are preferred. The other options are not contraindications.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_DEMOG_FP_019",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A woman opts for the injectable contraceptive DMPA (depot medroxyprogesterone acetate). At what interval is it administered?",
   "options": [
    "Once every week",
    "Once every month",
    "Once every 3 months (12 weeks)",
    "Once every 12 months"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "DMPA (depot medroxyprogesterone acetate) is a progestogen-only injectable given 150 mg intramuscularly once every 3 months (12 weeks); a subcutaneous 104 mg formulation exists. It acts mainly by inhibiting ovulation, thickening cervical mucus and thinning the endometrium. Being oestrogen-free it can be used in many women for whom combined pills are contraindicated and during lactation. Notable considerations include menstrual disturbance/amenorrhoea, a reversible but sometimes delayed return of fertility after stopping, and a reversible decrease in bone mineral density with long-term use. NET-EN is another injectable given every 2 months.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
