   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "For the Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) to be relied upon as an effective contraceptive, ALL of the following conditions must be met EXCEPT which combination is required?",
   "options": [
    "The mother is menstruating regularly again",
    "The infant is older than 12 months",
    "The mother has resumed mixed/complementary feeding of the infant",
    "The mother is fully or nearly fully breastfeeding, is amenorrhoeic, AND the infant is under 6 months of age"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "LAM relies on lactation-induced suppression of ovulation and is up to about 98% effective only when THREE conditions hold simultaneously: (1) the mother is exclusively or nearly exclusively breastfeeding (frequent day and night feeds), (2) she remains amenorrhoeic (no return of menstruation), and (3) the infant is under 6 months old. If ANY one fails — menses return, breastfeeding is reduced/complemented, or the baby reaches 6 months — effectiveness falls and another method should be started. Options A, B and C each describe a situation in which LAM is NO LONGER reliable, so the required combination is that stated in D.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_DEMOG_FP_021",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A woman presents after an episode of unprotected intercourse and requests emergency contraception with levonorgestrel pills. These are most effective when taken within what time window?",
   "options": [
    "Within 6 hours only",
    "Within 72 hours (3 days) of unprotected intercourse, the sooner the better",
    "Only after the next expected period",
    "Any time up to 3 weeks later"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Levonorgestrel-based emergency contraception (1.5 mg as a single dose or 0.75 mg × 2) is licensed for use within 72 hours (3 days) of unprotected intercourse, with efficacy being greatest the earlier it is taken. It works chiefly by delaying or inhibiting ovulation and is NOT effective once implantation has occurred (it does not disrupt an established pregnancy). The copper IUD is the most effective emergency method and can be inserted up to 5 days after intercourse. Emergency contraception is a back-up, not a routine method; counsel on ongoing regular contraception afterwards.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
