   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A man undergoes vasectomy for terminal contraception. What advice regarding contraception must be given immediately afterwards?",
   "options": [
    "He is not immediately sterile; an alternative contraceptive must be used until azoospermia is confirmed (about 3 months or roughly 20–30 ejaculations)",
    "He is completely sterile from the moment the procedure is finished",
    "Sterility is guaranteed within 24 hours of the operation",
    "He will never regain fertility and no follow-up semen check is ever needed"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Vasectomy is a permanent (terminal) method achieved by ligating/occluding the vas deferens, but it is NOT effective immediately: viable sperm persist in the distal reproductive tract for some time. The couple must therefore use an additional contraceptive method until azoospermia is documented, usually after about 3 months or roughly 20–30 ejaculations, confirmed by semen examination. Vasectomy is simpler, safer and cheaper than female tubectomy. It does not affect libido, erection or ejaculate volume appreciably. Tubectomy (female sterilization) can be done as interval, post-partum or concurrent-with-caesarean procedures.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
