   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Demography, family planning, MCH indicators",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Couple Protection Rate (CPR) is defined as:",
   "options": [
    "The proportion of the total population using any health service in a year",
    "The number of live births prevented per 1000 eligible couples",
    "The percentage of eligible couples effectively protected against childbirth by one or another approved family-planning method at a given time",
    "The percentage of pregnant women who complete at least four antenatal visits"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The Couple Protection Rate is an outcome indicator of a family-planning programme: it is the percentage of eligible couples (typically the currently married couple with the wife of reproductive age, conventionally 15–45 years) who are effectively protected from childbirth by an approved contraceptive method — sterilization, IUD, condom, oral pills, injectables — at a given point in time. A CPR of around 60% is regarded as the level needed to bring the net reproduction rate to 1 (population stabilization). It measures programme reach, unlike unmet need which measures the gap between desired and actual contraceptive use.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Demography and Family Planning",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
