   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Screening: sensitivity/specificity/PPV",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In a community of 2000 people the prevalence of a disease is 10%. A screening test with sensitivity 90% and specificity 80% is applied to everyone. What is the positive predictive value (PPV)?",
   "options": [
    "90%",
    "33.3%",
    "50%",
    "18%"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Prevalence 10% of 2000 = 200 diseased. Sensitivity 90% gives TP = 180 and FN = 20. Non-diseased = 1800; specificity 80% gives TN = 1440 and FP = 360. PPV = TP / (TP + FP) = 180 / (180 + 360) = 180 / 540 = 0.333 = 33.3%. Low prevalence keeps PPV low despite good sensitivity and specificity, so the answer is B.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Screening for Disease",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
