   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_EPI_SCREEN_022",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Screening: sensitivity/specificity/PPV",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Screening appears to improve survival simply because disease is diagnosed EARLIER, adding the interval between early detection and the usual clinical diagnosis to the measured survival time without postponing death. This spurious effect is called:",
   "options": [
    "Length-time bias",
    "Lead-time bias",
    "Volunteer (selection) bias",
    "Overdiagnosis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Lead-time bias occurs when earlier detection lengthens the measured time from diagnosis to death without actually changing the date of death, falsely inflating survival credited to screening. Length-time bias (A) is the over-representation of indolent cases, C is selection bias from healthy volunteers, and D is overdiagnosis, so the answer is B.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Screening for Disease",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSM_EPI_SCREEN_023",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Screening: sensitivity/specificity/PPV",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Screening programmes preferentially detect slowly progressive, less aggressive cases that spend longer in the detectable preclinical phase, making screen-detected disease appear to have a better prognosis. This is known as:",
   "options": [
    "Lead-time bias",
    "Recall bias",
    "Length-time bias",
    "Confounding"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Length-time bias arises because slowly growing tumours have a longer sojourn (detectable preclinical) phase and are therefore over-sampled at any screening round, making screen-detected disease look more benign than it truly is. Lead-time bias (A) is about earlier diagnosis, and overdiagnosis is its extreme form, so the answer is C.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Screening for Disease",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
