   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "topic": "Schizophrenia & psychosis",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 24-year-old man has had prominent auditory hallucinations and grossly disorganized speech for the past 6 weeks. To satisfy DSM-5 Criterion A for schizophrenia, the minimum symptom requirement is:",
   "options": [
    "Any one symptom present for at least 1 week",
    "At least two symptoms for a significant portion of 1 month, with at least one being delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech",
    "At least three symptoms, one of which must be a negative symptom",
    "At least two symptoms present continuously for 6 months"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "DSM-5 Criterion A requires two or more of five symptoms — delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized/catatonic behaviour, and negative symptoms — each present for a significant portion of a 1-month period, and at least one must be one of the first three 'core' positive symptoms. Unlike DSM-IV, a single bizarre delusion or Schneiderian voice no longer suffices (A). Negative symptoms are not mandatory (C), and the 6-month figure (D) is total illness duration, not the active phase.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Schizophrenia, DSM-5 Criterion A",
