  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_PSYCH_SCHIZO_004",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "topic": "Schizophrenia & psychosis",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "While waiting at a crossing, the instant the traffic light turned red a patient 'knew with total certainty' that he had been chosen by God to redeem mankind. This two-stage phenomenon — a real perception acquiring a fixed, self-referential meaning — is termed:",
   "options": [
    "Delusional mood (Wahnstimmung)",
    "Delusion of reference",
    "Delusional perception",
    "Autochthonous (primary) delusion"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Delusional perception is a Schneiderian first-rank symptom: a normal perception (the red light) is invested, in two linked stages, with a highly personal and unprovoked delusional significance. It differs from delusional mood (A), a vague pre-delusional atmosphere that 'something is going on', and from an autochthonous/primary delusion (D), which arises fully formed without any preceding perception. A delusion of reference (B) is the broader belief that ordinary events refer to oneself but lacks the specific single-perception-to-fixed-meaning leap that defines delusional perception.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. first-rank symptoms; disorders of thought content",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
