   "id": "Q_GEN_PSYCH_SCHIZO_008",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "topic": "Schizophrenia & psychosis",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A woman is unshakeably convinced that tiny insects are crawling and burrowing under her skin; she repeatedly digs at the skin, rejects all reassurance, and has entirely normal dermatological findings. The core symptom is best described as:",
   "options": [
    "Obsessional thought",
    "Overvalued idea",
    "Somatic (tactile) delusion of infestation",
    "Hypochondriacal preoccupation"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "This is a somatic/tactile delusion of infestation (delusional parasitosis, Ekbom syndrome): a fixed, false belief held with delusional conviction and no insight, unmoved by reassurance or contrary evidence. It differs from an obsession (A), which the patient recognises as their own senseless intrusive thought and resists, and from an overvalued idea (B) or hypochondriacal preoccupation (D), where the belief is held less rigidly, insight is at least partial, and reassurance is actively sought rather than rejected. Fixity plus absent insight defines the delusion.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. delusional disorder, somatic type; disorders of thought content",
