   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "topic": "Schizophrenia & psychosis",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In a patient presenting with psychosis, the prominence of which type of hallucination should MOST strongly prompt a search for an underlying organic or neurological cause rather than schizophrenia?",
   "options": [
    "Third-person auditory hallucinations",
    "Prominent visual and olfactory hallucinations",
    "Running-commentary auditory hallucinations",
    "Hearing one's own thoughts spoken aloud"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Auditory hallucinations — especially third-person voices, running commentary, and thought echo — are typical of primary schizophrenia. Prominent visual, olfactory, gustatory, or tactile hallucinations are comparatively unusual in schizophrenia and should raise suspicion of an organic cause: delirium, substance intoxication or withdrawal, temporal-lobe epilepsy (olfactory/gustatory auras), dementia, or a space-occupying lesion — mandating medical work-up. Options A, C, and D are all auditory first-rank-type phenomena that point toward a functional psychosis.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. A Short Textbook of Psychiatry, hallucinations and organic causes",
