   "id": "Q_GEN_RADIO_SAFETY_003",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "topic": "Radiation safety & principles",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The continuous spectrum of X-rays emitted from a diagnostic X-ray tube is produced by:",
   "options": [
    "Bremsstrahlung (braking radiation) as electrons decelerate near target nuclei",
    "Characteristic radiation from inner-shell electron transitions",
    "Compton scattering within the anode target",
    "Pair production in the tungsten target"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Bremsstrahlung (braking radiation) forms the continuous X-ray spectrum: high-speed electrons decelerate in the electric field of target nuclei, losing kinetic energy as photons of a continuous range up to the peak kVp. Characteristic radiation, by contrast, gives discrete energy lines from inner-shell electron transitions and superimposes spikes on the continuous curve. Pair production requires photon energies far above the diagnostic range.",
   "reference": "Bushberg, Ch. The Essential Physics of Medical Imaging: X-ray Production",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
