  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_RADIO_SAFETY_005",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "topic": "Radiation safety & principles",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In radiography, increasing the kVp (kilovoltage peak) while keeping other factors constant primarily:",
   "options": [
    "Increases only the number (quantity) of photons",
    "Has no effect on the X-ray beam",
    "Increases the energy and penetrating power (quality) of the beam, whereas mAs governs quantity",
    "Reduces the maximum photon energy produced"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "kVp (kilovoltage peak) sets the maximum photon energy and therefore the penetrating power or quality of the beam; raising kVp increases penetration and scatter and lowers subject contrast. mAs (milliampere-seconds) governs the quantity or number of photons and is the main determinant of patient dose for a given technique. Optimising both quantities is central to dose reduction under the ALARA principle.",
   "reference": "Christensen's Physics of Diagnostic Radiology, Ch. Exposure Factors",
