   "id": "Q_GEN_RADIO_SAFETY_006",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "topic": "Radiation safety & principles",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Which statement about the photoelectric effect in diagnostic imaging is correct?",
   "options": [
    "It predominates at high photon energies and is independent of atomic number",
    "It mainly generates image-degrading scatter that irradiates staff",
    "It produces the continuous energy spectrum from the target",
    "Its probability rises steeply with atomic number (about proportional to Z-cubed) and falls with photon energy, making it the basis of radiographic contrast"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "In the photoelectric effect an incident photon is totally absorbed, ejecting an inner-shell electron. Its probability is approximately proportional to the cube of the absorber's atomic number (Z-cubed) and inversely to about the cube of the photon energy, so it dominates at low energies and with high-Z materials such as bone, iodine and barium; this differential absorption is the physical basis of radiographic contrast. Compton scattering, not photoelectric, is the main source of scatter to staff.",
