   "subject": "Radiology",
   "topic": "Radiation safety & principles",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Assertion (A): Doubling the distance from a point radiation source reduces the exposure rate to one-quarter. Reason (R): The intensity of radiation from a point source is inversely proportional to the square of the distance. Select the correct option:",
   "options": [
    "Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A",
    "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A",
    "A is true but R is false",
    "A is false but R is true"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Radiation intensity from a point source obeys the inverse-square law: intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance (I proportional to 1/d-squared). Doubling the distance therefore reduces the exposure rate to one-quarter and tripling it to one-ninth, so the reason correctly explains the assertion. This makes increasing distance one of the most effective and cheapest occupational protection measures during fluoroscopy and mobile radiography.",
   "reference": "Dowd & Tilson, Ch. Practical Radiation Protection and Applied Radiobiology",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_GEN_RADIO_SAFETY_010",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "topic": "Radiation safety & principles",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The dose rate 1 m from a point source is 16 mGy/h. Applying the inverse-square law, the approximate dose rate at 2 m from the same source is:",
   "options": [
    "4 mGy/h",
    "8 mGy/h",
    "32 mGy/h",
    "64 mGy/h"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Using the inverse-square relationship I1 x d1-squared = I2 x d2-squared, a dose rate of 16 mGy/h at 1 m becomes 16 x (1/2)-squared = 16 x 0.25 = 4 mGy/h at 2 m. Because intensity falls with the square of the distance, doubling the separation cuts the rate to one-quarter, not one-half. This simple calculation underpins why stepping back is a powerful, no-cost protection strategy for staff.",
   "reference": "Christensen's Physics of Diagnostic Radiology, Ch. Radiation Protection Calculations",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
