   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "After total thyroidectomy and radioiodine ablation for differentiated (papillary/follicular) thyroid carcinoma, which serum marker is used to monitor for recurrence?",
   "options": [
    "Serum calcitonin",
    "Serum thyroglobulin, which should become undetectable and, if it rises, indicates recurrent or residual tumour",
    "Serum parathyroid hormone",
    "Serum alpha-fetoprotein"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Once all normal and malignant thyroid tissue has been removed by total thyroidectomy and radioiodine ablation, differentiated (papillary and follicular) carcinoma is followed with serum thyroglobulin, which is produced only by thyroid follicular tissue; it should fall to undetectable, and any subsequent rise signals residual or recurrent disease (anti-thyroglobulin antibodies must also be checked as they interfere with the assay). Calcitonin and CEA (option A) are the markers for medullary carcinoma, parathyroid hormone (option C) reflects the parathyroids, and alpha-fetoprotein (option D) is unrelated to thyroid cancer.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Follow-up of Thyroid Cancer",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
