    "A tumour larger than 4 cm, or with extrathyroidal extension, bilateral/multifocal disease, nodal or distant metastases, or a history of neck irradiation"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Total (or near-total) thyroidectomy is indicated in differentiated thyroid cancer when the tumour is large (generally more than 4 cm), shows extrathyroidal extension, is bilateral or multifocal, has lymph-node or distant metastases, or arises after previous neck irradiation, because it enables radioiodine ablation, thyroglobulin follow-up and TSH-suppressive thyroxine. Small unifocal low-risk cancers (option A) may be managed by lobectomy, while a benign hot nodule (option B) and a benign colloid nodule (option C) are not cancers requiring total thyroidectomy at all.",
   "reference": "SRB's Manual of Surgery 6e, Ch. Management of Thyroid Carcinoma",
