    "Hyponatremia",
    "Hypocalcemia",
    "Hyperkalemia"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Stored blood is anticoagulated with citrate, which chelates ionised calcium, so rapid transfusion causes hypocalcaemia (citrate toxicity) with perioral tingling, tetany and QT prolongation. Hyperkalaemia from potassium leaking out of stored cells is the other classic change, but citrate-induced hypocalcaemia is the characteristic answer for rapid transfusion.",
