   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A 35-year-old man has had hypercalcaemia for 18 months with a suppressed parathyroid hormone level, a raised angiotensin-converting enzyme and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the most likely cause?",
   "options": [
    "Malignancy",
    "Sarcoidosis",
    "Parathyroid hyperplasia",
    "CKD"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Chronic, stable hypercalcaemia with a normal PTH is PTH-independent; in a young man this fits sarcoidosis, where granuloma macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase and make excess calcitriol. Parathyroid hyperplasia would raise PTH and CKD tends to lower calcium. NOTE: malignancy is the commonest PTH-independent cause overall, but its hypercalcaemia is usually acute with weight loss, so the chronic stable course here favours sarcoidosis.",
   "reference": "FMGE July 2025 Part 1 (real exam recall); Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Hyperkalemia",
