   "topic": "Study designs; incidence vs prevalence",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A long-term study found that individuals who had high blood pressure (BP) during childhood continued to have high BP in adulthood, and those with low BP in childhood tended to maintain lower levels later in life. Which epidemiological concept does this pattern best represent?",
   "options": [
    "Rule of halves",
    "Regression to the mean",
    "Tracking of blood pressure",
    "Cohort effect"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Tracking describes the tendency of an individual to keep their relative rank within a population distribution over time, so a child at the high end of BP stays high as an adult. It differs from regression to the mean (extreme values drifting toward the average on re-measurement) and the cohort effect (differences between people born in different periods).",
   "reference": "FMGE July 2025 Part 1 (real exam recall); Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Epidemiology",
