   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A 10-year-old child weighs 40 kg, has short stature and intellectual disability, and the upper segment to lower segment ratio is 1.1:1. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
   "options": [
    "Cretinism (congenital hypothyroidism)",
    "Rickets",
    "Achondroplasia",
    "Constitutional short stature"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The upper to lower segment ratio falls with age, from about 1.7 at birth to roughly 1.0 by 10 years and 0.9 in adults. In untreated congenital hypothyroidism limb growth lags behind trunk growth, so the ratio stays infantile and is high for age; the same child shows short stature, weight gain and intellectual disability from thyroid hormone deficiency during brain development. Rickets causes bowing and metaphyseal changes without intellectual disability, and achondroplasia gives a far more extreme disproportion with normal intelligence.",
   "reference": "FMGE July 2025 Part 2 (real exam recall); OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Growth and Development",
