   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "topic": "Study designs; incidence vs prevalence",
   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "On 1 January 2024, an area of 10,000 people had 100 TB cases. During the year 30 were cured, 10 died, 20 new cases occurred, and 5 of the new cases were cured by 31 December. What are the incidence and prevalence of TB (per 1,000 and %, respectively)?",
   "options": [
    "2 and 1.2",
    "1.2 and 10",
    "1.2 and 12",
    "2 and 10"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Incidence counts only new cases among those at risk: 20/(10,000-100) x 1,000 = about 2 per 1,000. Period prevalence counts all cases during the interval: (100 existing + 20 new)/10,000 = 1.2%. NOTE: the source's route (75/10,000 = 0.75% ~ 1.2%) is arithmetically wrong; 1.2% is correctly the period prevalence, and the option mixes units (per-1,000 for incidence, % for prevalence).",
   "reference": "FMGE July 2025 Part 2 (real exam recall); Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Epidemiology",
