    "Start a fixed-rate IV insulin infusion and monitor/replace potassium as it falls",
    "Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH first",
    "Give IV potassium immediately regardless of the level",
    "Withhold all insulin until the ketones have cleared"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "DKA management is fluids first, then a fixed-rate insulin infusion; insulin drives potassium intracellularly, so serum K (even if initially high from acidosis) falls quickly and must be monitored and replaced once it is below ~5.0-5.5 mmol/L. Routine bicarbonate is not recommended, and insulin must not be withheld as it is needed to switch off ketogenesis.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Diabetic Ketoacidosis",
   "verified": "Y",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
