   "part": "B",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "During measles-control planning, the basic reproduction number (R0) of measles is taken as 15. Using the herd-immunity-threshold formula (1 minus 1/R0), what approximate proportion of the population must be immune to interrupt sustained transmission?",
   "options": [
    "About 50%",
    "About 75%",
    "About 93%",
    "About 15%"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The herd immunity threshold equals 1 minus 1/R0. For measles with an R0 of about 15 that is 1 minus 1/15 = 0.933, i.e. roughly 93% of the population must be immune to stop sustained transmission - which is why measles needs very high vaccination coverage. Diseases with a lower R0 need a lower threshold.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Principles of Epidemiology / Immunising Agents",
   "verified": "Y",
