   "topic": "Poisoning & envenomation: emergency management",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The end point of atropinisation in organophosphate poisoning is:",
   "options": [
    "Drying of secretions with clear chest and heart rate above 80",
    "Loss of consciousness",
    "Complete absence of fasciculations",
    "Fully dilated pupils"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Atropine is titrated to drying of bronchial secretions, a clear chest and adequate heart rate - not to pupil size, which is an unreliable end point. Under-atropinisation from fear of toxicity is a common fatal error.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Cholinergic Drugs",
