   "topic": "Poisoning & envenomation: emergency management",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Cherry-red skin with a normal arterial oxygen saturation on pulse oximetry despite severe hypoxia suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Organophosphate poisoning",
    "Carbon monoxide poisoning",
    "Methaemoglobinaemia",
    "Cyanide poisoning"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Carboxyhaemoglobin is misread as oxyhaemoglobin by pulse oximetry, so saturation appears normal; co-oximetry is needed. Treatment is high-flow or hyperbaric oxygen. Methaemoglobinaemia instead causes chocolate-brown blood and cyanosis unresponsive to oxygen.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Poisoning and Drug Overdose",
   "tags": [
