  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_MED_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical disorders in pregnancy: anaemia, diabetes, heart disease",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest fetal complication of poorly controlled gestational diabetes is:",
   "options": [
    "Macrosomia with shoulder dystocia risk",
    "Intrauterine growth restriction",
    "Oligohydramnios",
    "Post-term pregnancy"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Fetal hyperinsulinaemia from maternal hyperglycaemia drives macrosomia with the risk of shoulder dystocia and birth trauma, along with neonatal hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, polycythaemia and respiratory distress. Pre-gestational diabetes additionally causes congenital malformations.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Diabetes in Pregnancy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_MED_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical disorders in pregnancy: anaemia, diabetes, heart disease",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The oral hypoglycaemic agent most accepted in pregnancy when insulin is refused is:",
   "options": [
    "Metformin",
    "Pioglitazone",
    "Glibenclamide only in the first trimester",
    "Sitagliptin"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Insulin remains the standard, but metformin is increasingly accepted, does not cause maternal hypoglycaemia and crosses the placenta without evidence of teratogenicity. Thiazolidinediones and gliptins are avoided.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Insulin and Oral Hypoglycaemics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_MED_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical disorders in pregnancy: anaemia, diabetes, heart disease",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Peripartum cardiomyopathy is defined as new-onset heart failure occurring:",
   "options": [
    "At any time in a woman with prior heart disease",
    "Only after twin delivery",
    "In the last month of pregnancy or within five months of delivery with no other cause",
    "Only in the first trimester"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Peripartum cardiomyopathy presents with left ventricular systolic dysfunction in the last month of pregnancy or up to five months postpartum in the absence of another cause, and recurs in subsequent pregnancies, especially if ejection fraction does not normalise.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Cardiovascular Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
