   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Preterm labour is defined as labour occurring before:",
   "options": [
    "37 completed weeks of gestation",
    "40 weeks",
    "36 weeks",
    "38 weeks"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Preterm birth is delivery before 37 completed weeks; it is subdivided into extremely preterm below 28 weeks, very preterm 28 to 32 and moderate to late preterm 32 to 37 weeks, and is the leading cause of neonatal mortality.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Preterm Labour",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PTL_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Antenatal corticosteroids in threatened preterm labour are given primarily to:",
   "options": [
    "Increase fetal weight",
    "Accelerate fetal lung maturity and reduce respiratory distress, intraventricular haemorrhage and necrotising enterocolitis",
    "Arrest labour",
    "Treat maternal infection"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "A single course of betamethasone or dexamethasone between 24 and 34 weeks reduces neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular haemorrhage, necrotising enterocolitis and mortality, with maximal benefit 24 hours to 7 days after administration.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Preterm Birth",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PTL_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Magnesium sulphate given in preterm labour before 32 weeks provides:",
   "options": [
    "Fetal lung maturation",
    "Fetal neuroprotection reducing cerebral palsy",
    "Prevention of infection",
    "Definitive tocolysis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Magnesium sulphate is given for fetal neuroprotection to reduce cerebral palsy in births before 32 weeks. Its tocolytic effect is weak, and nifedipine or atosiban are preferred if tocolysis is required.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Preterm Birth",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PTL_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The most reliable confirmation of prelabour rupture of membranes is:",
   "options": [
    "Digital vaginal examination",
    "Ultrasound alone",
    "Maternal history alone",
    "Direct visualisation of amniotic fluid pooling in the posterior vaginal fornix on sterile speculum examination"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Sterile speculum examination showing pooling is the standard; digital examination is avoided as it introduces infection. Nitrazine and fern tests and placental alpha-microglobulin-1 assays help in equivocal cases.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. PROM",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PTL_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In preterm prelabour rupture of membranes at 30 weeks with no infection, management is:",
   "options": [
    "Immediate delivery",
    "Amnioinfusion",
    "Expectant management with antibiotics and corticosteroids",
    "Tocolysis for four weeks"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Before 34 weeks with no chorioamnionitis or fetal compromise, expectant management with latency antibiotics such as erythromycin and a corticosteroid course improves outcome. Delivery is indicated for infection, abruption or fetal distress.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Preterm Birth",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PTL_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Preterm labour, PROM & post-term pregnancy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most feared maternal complication of prolonged rupture of membranes is:",
   "options": [
    "Postpartum haemorrhage",
    "Amniotic fluid embolism",
    "Uterine rupture",
    "Chorioamnionitis"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Ascending infection causes chorioamnionitis with maternal fever, tachycardia, uterine tenderness and foul discharge with fetal tachycardia, and mandates delivery with broad-spectrum antibiotics irrespective of gestation.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. PROM",
   "tags": [
