   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Third-stage complications, retained placenta & uterine inversion",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Acute uterine inversion most commonly results from:",
   "options": [
    "Twin pregnancy",
    "Mismanaged third stage with cord traction on an uncontracted uterus and fundal pressure",
    "Pre-eclampsia",
    "Prolonged second stage"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Traction on the cord with a relaxed uterus, often with fundal pressure, inverts the fundus, causing profound shock out of proportion to blood loss from vagal stimulation. Immediate replacement by the Johnson manoeuvre before the cervix contracts is essential.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Inversion of Uterus",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
