   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Colostrum differs from mature milk in having:",
   "options": [
    "More fat and lactose",
    "More protein, immunoglobulin A and vitamin A but less fat and lactose",
    "Less protein",
    "No immunoglobulins"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Colostrum, secreted for the first 3 to 4 days, is rich in protein, secretory IgA, vitamin A and minerals with less fat and lactose, and its laxative effect helps clear meconium.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Breastfeeding",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PUE_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puerperium, lactation & puerperal sepsis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Exclusive breastfeeding as defined by WHO means giving:",
   "options": [
    "Only breast milk with no other food or drink, not even water, for six months",
    "Breast milk with water",
    "Breast milk plus formula",
    "Breast milk for three months"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Exclusive breastfeeding permits only breast milk, allowing medicines and vitamins but no water or other foods, for the first six months, followed by complementary feeding with continued breastfeeding up to two years.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Breastfeeding",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PUE_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puerperium, lactation & puerperal sepsis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Lactational amenorrhoea provides effective contraception only if:",
   "options": [
    "The infant is under six months, exclusively breastfed and the mother is amenorrhoeic",
    "Any breastfeeding occurs",
    "The mother has resumed menses",
    "The infant is under two years"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "All three conditions must hold for the lactational amenorrhoea method to give about 98% protection; failure of any one requires an alternative method, and progestogen-only pills are safe during lactation.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Family Planning",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PUE_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puerperium, lactation & puerperal sepsis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Puerperal pyrexia is defined as a temperature of 38 degrees Celsius or more on any two of the first:",
   "options": [
    "Ten days postpartum, excluding the first 24 hours",
    "Three days",
    "Twenty days",
    "Two days"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Puerperal pyrexia is fever of 38 degrees or more occurring on any two of the first ten days postpartum excluding the first 24 hours. Genital tract sepsis, urinary infection, breast infection, wound infection and venous thrombosis must be sought.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Puerperal Sepsis",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_PUE_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puerperium, lactation & puerperal sepsis",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The commonest organism in puerperal sepsis is:",
   "options": [
    "Escherichia coli alone",
    "Candida",
    "Staphylococcus aureus alone",
    "Anaerobic streptococci, often as part of a polymicrobial infection"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Puerperal genital tract sepsis is usually polymicrobial with anaerobic streptococci predominating, alongside Bacteroides, E. coli and group A streptococcus which can cause fulminant disease. Broad-spectrum antibiotics covering anaerobes are required.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Puerperal Sepsis",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The Bishop score assesses the favourability of the cervix using all EXCEPT:",
   "options": [
    "Cervical consistency",
    "Station of the head",
    "Cervical dilatation",
    "Fetal weight"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The Bishop score grades dilatation, effacement, consistency, position of the cervix and station of the head. A score of 6 or more is favourable; below that, cervical ripening with prostaglandin is used before oxytocin.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Induction of Labour",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "reviewed against a cross-topic duplicate candidate; answer, topic and reference confirmed"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The agent of choice for cervical ripening in an unfavourable cervix at term is:",
   "options": [
    "Magnesium sulphate",
    "Oxytocin infusion",
    "Ergometrine",
    "Prostaglandin E2 gel or misoprostol"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Prostaglandins soften and efface the cervix, unlike oxytocin which stimulates contractions without ripening. Misoprostol is cheap and effective but must be used cautiously after previous caesarean because of rupture risk.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Oxytocics and Prostaglandins",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Induction of labour is absolutely contraindicated in:",
   "options": [
    "Mild pre-eclampsia",
    "Gestational diabetes",
    "Post-term pregnancy",
    "Placenta praevia and classical caesarean scar"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Absolute contraindications include placenta praevia, vasa praevia, transverse lie, active genital herpes, cord prolapse, previous classical caesarean or myomectomy entering the cavity, and invasive cervical carcinoma.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Induction of Labour",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The prerequisite that must be satisfied before applying outlet forceps is:",
   "options": [
    "Fully dilated cervix with an engaged head and known position",
    "Cervix 6 cm dilated",
    "Unengaged head",
    "Breech presentation"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Forceps require full dilatation, ruptured membranes, an engaged head with known position, adequate pelvis, empty bladder, anaesthesia and consent. Applying forceps before full dilatation causes cervical tears and fetal injury.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Forceps Delivery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The commonest indication for primary caesarean section is:",
   "options": [
    "Cord prolapse",
    "Placenta praevia",
    "Failure to progress or cephalopelvic disproportion",
    "Twin pregnancy"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Failure to progress with cephalopelvic disproportion is the commonest primary indication, while previous caesarean is the commonest overall indication once repeat operations are counted. Fetal distress and malpresentation follow.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Caesarean Section",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The lower segment transverse (Kerr) incision is preferred over the classical incision because it:",
   "options": [
    "Has a much lower risk of scar rupture in a subsequent pregnancy",
    "Gives better access to a transverse lie",
    "Is quicker",
    "Causes less bleeding in praevia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The lower segment is thin, less vascular and heals with a strong scar, so rupture risk in a later pregnancy is about 0.5% versus 4 to 9% for a classical upper segment incision, which is why vaginal birth after caesarean is possible only with the former.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Caesarean Section",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A woman with one previous lower segment caesarean requesting vaginal birth requires:",
   "options": [
    "Routine induction with misoprostol",
    "Prolonged second stage to avoid surgery",
    "Elective forceps",
    "A facility with continuous monitoring and immediate access to caesarean and blood"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Trial of labour after caesarean succeeds in about 70% but carries around 0.5% scar rupture risk, so it needs continuous fetal monitoring in a facility able to perform immediate caesarean. Misoprostol induction is avoided.",
   "reference": "Williams Obstetrics 26e, Ch. Prior Caesarean Delivery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_IND_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Induction, augmentation & operative obstetrics (forceps, vacuum, LSCS)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Amniotomy accelerates labour but carries the risk of:",
   "options": [
    "Uterine inversion",
    "Postpartum psychosis",
    "Cord prolapse and ascending infection",
    "Placenta accreta"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Artificial rupture of membranes shortens labour and allows assessment of liquor colour, but risks cord prolapse, especially with a high or unengaged head or malpresentation, and ascending infection with prolonged labour.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Induction of Labour",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AUB_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puberty, amenorrhoea & abnormal uterine bleeding",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Primary amenorrhoea is defined as absence of menstruation by age:",
   "options": [
    "15 years with secondary sexual characteristics, or 13 years without them",
    "10 years",
    "18 years",
    "20 years"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Evaluation begins at 15 years if secondary sexual characteristics are present, or at 13 if they are absent, or 3 years after thelarche. Earlier evaluation is warranted with clinical clues such as short stature or virilisation.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Amenorrhoea",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AUB_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puberty, amenorrhoea & abnormal uterine bleeding",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of primary amenorrhoea is:",
   "options": [
    "Imperforate hymen",
    "Mullerian agenesis",
    "Prolactinoma",
    "Gonadal dysgenesis, most often Turner syndrome"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Gonadal dysgenesis, typically 45,XO Turner syndrome with streak ovaries, high FSH and short stature, is the commonest cause. Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky) is second and presents with normal secondary characteristics and absent uterus.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Amenorrhoea",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AUB_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puberty, amenorrhoea & abnormal uterine bleeding",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of secondary amenorrhoea in a sexually active woman is:",
   "options": [
    "Polycystic ovary syndrome",
    "Premature ovarian insufficiency",
    "Pregnancy",
    "Asherman syndrome"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Pregnancy must always be excluded first with a urine or serum hCG before any other investigation. Thereafter polycystic ovary syndrome, hyperprolactinaemia, thyroid disease and hypothalamic causes are considered.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Amenorrhoea",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AUB_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Puberty, amenorrhoea & abnormal uterine bleeding",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The most effective medical treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding in a woman wanting to retain her uterus is:",
   "options": [
    "Cyclical progestogen for 10 days",
    "Ethamsylate",
    "Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system",
    "Combined oral contraceptive for 3 months only"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The levonorgestrel intrauterine system reduces menstrual blood loss by 80 to 95% and is the most effective medical option, often avoiding hysterectomy. Tranexamic acid and NSAIDs are effective non-hormonal alternatives.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Abnormal Uterine Bleeding",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
