  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_INFT_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Infertility & assisted reproduction",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The single commonest identifiable cause of female infertility in India is:",
   "options": [
    "Cervical factor",
    "Tubal factor, largely from genital tuberculosis and pelvic inflammatory disease",
    "Uterine anomaly",
    "Endometriosis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Tubal disease from genital tuberculosis and pelvic inflammatory disease is the leading female cause in India, followed by ovulatory dysfunction. Male factor contributes to about 30 to 40% of couples.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Infertility",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_INFT_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Infertility & assisted reproduction",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The first-line investigation of the male partner in an infertile couple is:",
   "options": [
    "Semen analysis",
    "Serum testosterone",
    "Scrotal Doppler",
    "Testicular biopsy"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Semen analysis is the essential first test and should be repeated after 2 to 3 months if abnormal. WHO reference values include volume 1.5 mL or more, concentration 15 million per mL, total motility 40% and normal forms 4%.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Infertility",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_INFT_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Infertility & assisted reproduction",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Tubal patency is most commonly assessed by:",
   "options": [
    "Serum progesterone",
    "Ultrasound alone",
    "Endometrial biopsy",
    "Hysterosalpingography or saline infusion sonohysterography"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Hysterosalpingography demonstrates tubal patency and uterine cavity contour and has a mild therapeutic flushing effect; laparoscopy with chromopertubation remains the gold standard and allows treatment of adhesions and endometriosis.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Infertility",
   "tags": [
