   "topic": "Gestational trophoblastic disease & vulvo-vaginal lesions",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which feature most strongly suggests choriocarcinoma rather than a persisting mole?",
   "options": [
    "Falling hCG",
    "Uterine size reduction",
    "Rising hCG with distant metastases, commonly pulmonary",
    "Absence of bleeding"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Choriocarcinoma is a highly vascular malignancy with early haematogenous spread, most often to the lungs producing a cannonball or snowstorm pattern, then vagina, brain and liver. It is remarkably chemosensitive with high cure rates.",
