  {
   "id": "Q_PED_NB_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Newborn assessment, prematurity & low birth weight",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of neonatal hypothermia in a delivery room is:",
   "options": [
    "Evaporative heat loss from a wet newborn",
    "Radiant gain",
    "Conduction through the cot",
    "Fever"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A wet newborn loses heat rapidly by evaporation, so immediate drying and removal of wet linen is the single most effective preventive step, along with a warm delivery room, skin-to-skin contact and delayed bathing.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Thermoregulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_NB_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Newborn assessment, prematurity & low birth weight",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A term baby weighing 1800 g at birth is classified as:",
   "options": [
    "Preterm",
    "Appropriate for gestational age",
    "Large for gestational age",
    "Small for gestational age and low birth weight"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Birth weight below the tenth centile for gestational age defines small for gestational age; a term baby of 1800 g is both SGA and low birth weight. Such babies are at risk of hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and polycythaemia.",
   "reference": "OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Low Birth Weight",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_NB_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Newborn assessment, prematurity & low birth weight",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Retinopathy of prematurity is most strongly associated with:",
   "options": [
    "Hyperbilirubinaemia",
    "Maternal diabetes",
    "Hypocalcaemia",
    "Prematurity with unmonitored supplemental oxygen exposure"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Immature retinal vasculature exposed to fluctuating high oxygen tension undergoes vaso-obliteration then pathological neovascularisation. Screening of babies below 34 weeks or 1750 g is mandatory, with laser or anti-VEGF treatment for threshold disease.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Retinopathy of Prematurity",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_NB_010",
   "part": "B",
