   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest congenital heart disease in children is:",
   "options": [
    "Atrial septal defect",
    "Patent ductus arteriosus",
    "Tetralogy of Fallot",
    "Ventricular septal defect"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Ventricular septal defect is the commonest congenital cardiac lesion; small defects often close spontaneously while large ones cause left-to-right shunting, failure to thrive and eventually pulmonary hypertension.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Ventricular Septal Defect",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The four components of tetralogy of Fallot are:",
   "options": [
    "ASD, VSD, PDA and coarctation",
    "Pulmonary atresia, TAPVC, VSD and PDA",
    "Mitral stenosis, ASD, PDA and PS",
    "Pulmonary stenosis, VSD, right ventricular hypertrophy and overriding aorta"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Tetralogy comprises pulmonary outflow obstruction, a large ventricular septal defect, right ventricular hypertrophy and an aorta overriding the septum. Severity of pulmonary stenosis determines the degree of cyanosis and gives a boot-shaped heart.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Tetralogy of Fallot",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "During a cyanotic (tet) spell, the child should be placed in:",
   "options": [
    "Supine flat position",
    "The knee-chest position with oxygen, morphine and fluids",
    "Sitting upright with legs dependent",
    "Prone with head down"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The knee-chest or squatting position raises systemic vascular resistance, reducing right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect. Morphine calms hyperpnoea, and beta blockers relieve infundibular spasm.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Tetralogy of Fallot",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in the neonatal period is:",
   "options": [
    "Truncus arteriosus",
    "Transposition of the great arteries",
    "Tetralogy of Fallot",
    "Tricuspid atresia"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Transposition presents with severe cyanosis in the first days as the ductus closes, with an egg-on-side cardiac silhouette. Prostaglandin E1 maintains ductal patency and balloon atrial septostomy allows mixing before arterial switch.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Transposition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Eisenmenger syndrome refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Reversible pulmonary hypertension",
    "Isolated pulmonary stenosis",
    "Irreversible pulmonary hypertension with shunt reversal in an untreated left-to-right shunt",
    "A right-to-left shunt from birth"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Prolonged left-to-right shunting causes fixed pulmonary vascular disease, so the shunt reverses and the patient becomes cyanosed with clubbing and polycythaemia. Surgical closure is then contraindicated and transplantation is the only option.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Pulmonary Hypertension",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The murmur of a small ventricular septal defect is:",
   "options": [
    "An ejection systolic murmur in the aortic area",
    "A harsh pansystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border with a thrill",
    "A continuous machinery murmur",
    "A mid-diastolic murmur at the apex"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "A small defect generates a loud harsh pansystolic murmur because of the high pressure gradient; paradoxically, a very large defect equalises pressures and produces a softer murmur - loudness does not indicate size.",
   "reference": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 21e, Ch. Ventricular Septal Defect",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_CHD_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Congenital & rheumatic heart disease in children",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The most common cause of acquired heart disease in Indian children is:",
   "options": [
    "Infective endocarditis",
    "Kawasaki disease",
    "Viral myocarditis",
    "Rheumatic heart disease"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Rheumatic heart disease following group A streptococcal pharyngitis remains the leading acquired cardiac disease in Indian children, with mitral valve involvement most frequent, and is preventable by prompt treatment of sore throat.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Rheumatic Heart Disease",
   "tags": [
