   "topic": "Childhood tuberculosis & paediatric HIV",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Anti-tuberculosis drug doses in children are higher per kilogram than in adults because children:",
   "options": [
    "Absorb less from the gut",
    "Are more likely to have resistant organisms",
    "Metabolise and clear the drugs faster, achieving lower serum levels at adult doses",
    "Have larger livers"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Faster clearance means weight-band dosing uses higher mg/kg - isoniazid 10, rifampicin 15, pyrazinamide 35 and ethambutol 20 mg/kg - to reach therapeutic concentrations. Doses are recalculated as the child gains weight.",
   "reference": "National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme Guidelines for Paediatric Tuberculosis 2022; OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Tuberculosis",
