  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "An extradural haematoma most commonly results from injury to the:",
   "options": [
    "Bridging veins",
    "Vertebral artery",
    "Middle meningeal artery with an associated temporal bone fracture",
    "Superior sagittal sinus"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Rupture of the middle meningeal artery beneath a temporal fracture produces an arterial extradural collection, classically with a lucid interval, and appears biconvex (lens-shaped) on CT because it cannot cross suture lines.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Head Injury",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A crescent-shaped hyperdense collection crossing suture lines on CT indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Subarachnoid haemorrhage",
    "Extradural haematoma",
    "Intracerebral haematoma",
    "Acute subdural haematoma from torn bridging veins"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Subdural blood from torn bridging veins spreads across the whole hemisphere crossing sutures but not the midline, appearing crescentic. It is common in the elderly and alcoholics with cerebral atrophy and carries worse prognosis than extradural bleeds.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Head Injury",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#image-based"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The lucid interval classically described in head injury refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Amnesia only",
    "A period of normal consciousness between the initial concussion and subsequent deterioration",
    "Permanent recovery",
    "Coma from the outset"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The lucid interval is characteristic of extradural haematoma, as arterial blood slowly strips the dura before the mass effect causes deterioration and uncal herniation. Its presence demands urgent CT and evacuation.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Head Injury",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A Glasgow Coma Scale score of 8 or less indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Severe head injury requiring intubation for airway protection",
    "Mild injury",
    "Moderate injury",
    "Normal consciousness"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "GCS 13 to 15 is mild, 9 to 12 moderate and 8 or below severe. A score of 8 or less means the patient cannot protect the airway and requires definitive airway management with neurosurgical referral.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Head Injury",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Battle sign and raccoon eyes in a head-injured patient indicate:",
   "options": [
    "An orbital blowout fracture",
    "A depressed vault fracture",
    "A base of skull fracture",
    "A cervical spine injury"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Mastoid ecchymosis (Battle sign) and periorbital bruising (raccoon eyes) with CSF otorrhoea or rhinorrhoea and haemotympanum indicate basal skull fracture. Nasogastric tubes are avoided because of the risk of intracranial passage.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Head Injury",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Monro-Kellie doctrine explains why:",
   "options": [
    "The brain can expand indefinitely",
    "CSF production stops after injury",
    "Blood pressure falls with rising ICP",
    "Intracranial pressure rises steeply once compensatory displacement of CSF and venous blood is exhausted"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The cranium is a rigid box containing brain, blood and CSF; an expanding mass is first buffered by displacing CSF and venous blood, but once this reserve is exhausted, small further increases in volume cause steep rises in pressure.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Intracranial Pressure",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A head-injured patient develops rising blood pressure with bradycardia and irregular breathing. What is this response and what does it signify?",
   "options": [
    "The Cushing reflex, signifying critically raised intracranial pressure and impending herniation",
    "Neurogenic shock from cervical cord injury",
    "A normal stress response to pain",
    "Vasovagal syncope"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "It is a late and ominous sign rather than an early warning, so it demands immediate measures to lower intracranial pressure and urgent imaging with neurosurgical referral rather than treatment of the hypertension itself.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Neurosurgery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#formula"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "duplicate of Q_SUR_HED_014, which gives the same cerebral perfusion pressure formula together with its target. Rewritten to the Cushing reflex"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_HED_008",
