   "topic": "Head injury & neurosurgical emergencies",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of spontaneous subarachnoid haemorrhage is:",
   "options": [
    "Rupture of a berry (saccular) aneurysm of the circle of Willis",
    "Arteriovenous malformation",
    "Hypertensive bleed",
    "Tumour"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "About 85% of spontaneous subarachnoid haemorrhage is from a ruptured saccular aneurysm, presenting with sudden thunderclap headache, neck stiffness and photophobia. CT then lumbar puncture for xanthochromia confirms it; nimodipine prevents vasospasm.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Subarachnoid Haemorrhage",
