   "topic": "Burns & basics of plastic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A deep partial thickness (deep dermal) burn is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Erythema only",
    "Painless, dry, leathery eschar",
    "Intact sensation with blistering and brisk refill",
    "Reduced sensation with a mottled appearance and sluggish capillary refill, healing slowly with scarring"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Superficial dermal burns are painful, blistered and blanch briskly, healing in two weeks. Deep dermal burns have diminished sensation and sluggish refill, heal slowly with scarring and often need grafting; full thickness burns are painless and leathery.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Burns",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_BRN_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Burns & basics of plastic surgery",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which finding suggests inhalational injury requiring early intubation?",
   "options": [
    "Burns to the lower limbs",
    "Facial burns with singed nasal hair, soot in the oropharynx and hoarseness",
    "Circumferential limb burn",
    "A small scald on the hand"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Airway oedema develops rapidly and unpredictably, so early elective intubation is safer than emergency intubation later. Carboxyhaemoglobin should be measured and 100% oxygen given, as pulse oximetry is falsely reassuring.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Burns",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_BRN_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Burns & basics of plastic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Escharotomy is indicated for:",
   "options": [
    "Superficial burns",
    "Facial burns",
    "A circumferential full-thickness burn causing distal ischaemia or restricting chest expansion",
    "Any partial thickness burn"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Inelastic circumferential eschar acts as a tourniquet as oedema develops, compromising limb perfusion or ventilation. Escharotomy incisions through the eschar down to fat relieve this and are performed at the bedside.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Burns",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_BRN_006",
