   "topic": "Burns & basics of plastic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A skin graft fails to take most commonly because of:",
   "options": [
    "Cold weather",
    "Haematoma or seroma beneath the graft preventing plasmatic imbibition and revascularisation",
    "Excessive graft thickness only",
    "Systemic antibiotics"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Graft take depends on plasmatic imbibition then inosculation and revascularisation, all of which fail if a collection, shear movement or infection separates graft from bed. Meticulous haemostasis and immobilisation are therefore essential.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Plastic Surgery",
