   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Subclavian steal syndrome results from:",
   "options": [
    "Proximal subclavian stenosis causing retrograde vertebral artery flow with arm exercise",
    "Carotid stenosis",
    "Aortic coarctation",
    "Venous thrombosis"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Stenosis proximal to the vertebral origin causes blood to be siphoned retrogradely down the vertebral artery to supply the arm during exercise, producing posterior circulation symptoms - dizziness, diplopia, syncope - with a blood pressure difference between arms.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Arterial Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_VAS_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
