   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Vascular surgery, varicose veins & peripheral arterial disease",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most important investigation before surgery for varicose veins is:",
   "options": [
    "CT angiography",
    "Duplex ultrasound to map incompetence and confirm deep venous patency",
    "Plain radiograph",
    "Venography in all cases"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Duplex ultrasound identifies the sites of reflux and confirms the deep system is patent, since stripping superficial veins in a patient with deep venous obstruction would be harmful.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Venous Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Prolonged vomiting from pyloric obstruction produces:",
   "options": [
    "Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria",
    "Respiratory alkalosis",
    "Metabolic acidosis",
    "Hyperchloraemic acidosis"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Loss of gastric acid depletes hydrogen and chloride; the kidney retains sodium and bicarbonate and excretes hydrogen and potassium, producing paradoxical aciduria. Correction is with normal saline and potassium.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Large-volume resuscitation with 0.9% sodium chloride can produce:",
   "options": [
    "Hypernatraemia only",
    "Metabolic alkalosis",
    "Respiratory acidosis",
    "Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Normal saline has a chloride concentration of 154 mmol/L, well above plasma, so large volumes cause a dilutional hyperchloraemic normal anion gap acidosis. Balanced crystalloids such as Ringer lactate avoid this.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The daily maintenance requirement of sodium in an adult is approximately:",
   "options": [
    "1 to 2 mmol per kg per day",
    "10 mmol per kg",
    "0.1 mmol per kg",
    "5 mmol per kg"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Adults need about 1 to 2 mmol/kg/day of sodium, 1 mmol/kg/day of potassium and 25 to 30 mL/kg/day of water, adjusted for losses from drains, fistulae, fever and third-space sequestration.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most serious complication of central venous total parenteral nutrition is:",
   "options": [
    "Hyperglycaemia",
    "Catheter-related bloodstream infection",
    "Hypertension",
    "Constipation"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Line sepsis is the most serious complication, requiring strict aseptic technique and a dedicated lumen. Metabolic complications include hyperglycaemia, electrolyte disturbance, hepatic steatosis and refeeding syndrome.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Third-space loss in a surgical patient refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Insensible skin loss",
    "Sequestration of extracellular fluid into the bowel lumen, peritoneum or interstitium where it is non-functional",
    "Blood loss into a drain",
    "Urinary losses"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Fluid shifted into the interstitium, gut lumen or peritoneal cavity after surgery, peritonitis or obstruction is lost from the circulation despite remaining in the body, so it must be replaced with isotonic crystalloid.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The best simple bedside indicator of adequate resuscitation in a surgical patient is:",
   "options": [
    "Respiratory rate",
    "Skin turgor",
    "Urine output of at least 0.5 mL/kg/hour",
    "Blood pressure alone"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Urine output reflects renal perfusion and is a practical continuous marker, supported by heart rate, capillary refill, mental state and lactate clearance. Blood pressure is maintained until late by compensation.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Refeeding syndrome in a malnourished surgical patient is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Hypophosphataemia with hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia after reintroduction of feeding",
    "Hypernatraemia",
    "Metabolic acidosis alone",
    "Hyperphosphataemia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Carbohydrate-driven insulin release shifts phosphate, potassium and magnesium intracellularly, risking arrhythmia and cardiac failure. Feeding is started at reduced calories with thiamine and electrolyte monitoring.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_FLU_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid, electrolyte & nutritional support in surgery",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Severe hyperkalaemia with ECG changes is treated first with:",
   "options": [
    "Oral resonium",
    "Haemodialysis",
    "Insulin with dextrose",
    "Intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilise the myocardium"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Calcium does not lower potassium but immediately antagonises its membrane effect and prevents arrhythmia, buying time for insulin-dextrose, salbutamol and bicarbonate to shift potassium intracellularly, and for dialysis or resins to remove it.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Fluid and Electrolytes; KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Drugs Affecting Calcium Balance",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
