  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A flail chest is defined as:",
   "options": [
    "Fracture of the sternum",
    "Fracture of three or more consecutive ribs in two or more places producing paradoxical movement",
    "Fracture of the clavicle",
    "A single rib fracture"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The flail segment moves paradoxically inward on inspiration. The main problem is the underlying pulmonary contusion rather than the mechanics, so management centres on analgesia, physiotherapy and ventilatory support if hypoxia develops.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Trauma",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "An open (sucking) chest wound is initially managed by:",
   "options": [
    "Observation",
    "Immediate suturing",
    "A four-sided airtight dressing",
    "A three-sided occlusive dressing acting as a flutter valve, then an intercostal drain"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A dressing taped on three sides lets air escape on expiration but prevents entry on inspiration, avoiding conversion to a tension pneumothorax which a fully sealed dressing would cause. Definitive drainage and closure follow.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Trauma",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest site of aortic injury in blunt deceleration trauma is:",
   "options": [
    "The aortic isthmus just distal to the left subclavian artery",
    "The abdominal aorta",
    "The aortic root",
    "The arch at the innominate origin"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The isthmus is relatively fixed by the ligamentum arteriosum while the arch is mobile, so shearing forces concentrate here. A widened mediastinum on chest radiograph prompts CT angiography.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Trauma",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Cardiac tamponade following penetrating chest trauma is definitively managed by:",
   "options": [
    "Emergency thoracotomy or sternotomy with pericardial decompression and repair",
    "Pericardiocentesis alone",
    "Diuretics",
    "Fluid restriction"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Pericardiocentesis is only a temporising measure in traumatic tamponade because the blood clots; definitive surgical decompression and repair of the cardiac wound is required. Beck triad and a positive FAST scan support the diagnosis.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Trauma",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The site for intercostal drain insertion in the safe triangle is:",
   "options": [
    "Directly over the rib",
    "Second space midclavicular below the rib",
    "Eighth space posteriorly",
    "Fifth intercostal space just anterior to the midaxillary line, above the rib"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The safe triangle is bounded by the lateral border of pectoralis major, the anterior border of latissimus dorsi and a line above the fifth intercostal space. The drain passes over the upper border of the rib to avoid the neurovascular bundle in the subcostal groove.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Surgery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
