   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of a solitary pulmonary nodule in India is:",
   "options": [
    "Tuberculoma",
    "Bronchogenic carcinoma",
    "Metastasis",
    "Hamartoma"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "In a tuberculosis-endemic country a tuberculoma is the commonest cause, though malignancy must be excluded. Features suggesting benignity include central or laminated calcification, small size, smooth margins and stability over two years.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Surgery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour classically produces:",
   "options": [
    "Superior vena caval obstruction alone",
    "Horner syndrome with shoulder and ulnar arm pain from brachial plexus involvement",
    "Hoarseness only",
    "Dysphagia"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "An apical tumour invades the sympathetic chain causing ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis, and the lower brachial plexus roots causing pain along the ulnar border and small muscle wasting; rib and vertebral erosion may occur.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Surgery",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_SUR_CHT_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Surgery",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Chest trauma & thoracic surgery",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Surgical emphysema after chest trauma indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Fat embolism",
    "Infection with gas-forming organisms only",
    "Air leaking into subcutaneous tissue, usually from a pneumothorax or airway injury",
    "Haemothorax"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Crepitus in the chest wall and neck follows air tracking from a pleural or airway breach; extensive rapidly progressive emphysema suggests major bronchial injury requiring bronchoscopy. An intercostal drain usually resolves it.",
   "reference": "Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery 28e, Ch. Thoracic Trauma",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_HDN_001",
