   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Haemodynamics: oedema, thrombosis, embolism, infarction, shock",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation in shock is best described as:",
   "options": [
    "Isolated thrombosis",
    "Isolated bleeding tendency",
    "Widespread microthrombi with consumption of platelets and clotting factors causing bleeding",
    "A primary marrow disorder"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Systemic activation of coagulation forms microthrombi causing ischaemic organ damage and microangiopathic haemolysis, while simultaneous consumption of platelets and factors with secondary fibrinolysis produces bleeding - hence the paradox.",
   "reference": "Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 10e, Ch. Haemodynamic Disorders",
   "tags": [
