  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_003",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Fanconi anaemia is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Normal chromosomes",
    "Acquired aplasia only",
    "Isolated red cell aplasia",
    "Inherited marrow failure with chromosomal fragility, short stature and radial ray anomalies"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Defective DNA crosslink repair causes progressive pancytopenia from about 5 to 10 years, with cafe-au-lait spots, absent or hypoplastic thumbs, renal anomalies and a high risk of AML and squamous carcinoma. Increased chromosomal breakage with diepoxybutane confirms it.",
   "reference": "Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 10e, Ch. Red Blood Cell Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Pure red cell aplasia with a normal white cell and platelet count following parvovirus B19 infection occurs typically in patients with:",
   "options": [
    "Thrombocytopenia",
    "Normal red cell lifespan",
    "Iron deficiency",
    "Chronic haemolytic anaemia such as hereditary spherocytosis or sickle cell disease"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors via the P antigen, halting erythropoiesis for about a week. This is inconsequential with a normal 120-day red cell lifespan, but precipitates aplastic crisis when the lifespan is already short.",
   "reference": "Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 10e, Ch. Red Blood Cell Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_005",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of aplastic anaemia is:",
   "options": [
    "Drugs",
    "Idiopathic, presumed immune-mediated",
    "Radiation",
    "Viral hepatitis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Most cases are idiopathic and respond to immunosuppression with antithymocyte globulin and ciclosporin, or to stem cell transplantation in the young. Identified causes include chloramphenicol, benzene, radiation, hepatitis and pregnancy.",
   "reference": "Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 10e, Ch. Red Blood Cell Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Packed red cells are stored at 2 to 6 degrees Celsius for a maximum of:",
   "options": [
    "35 to 42 days depending on the anticoagulant preservative",
    "24 hours",
    "5 days",
    "6 months"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "CPDA-1 allows 35 days and additive solutions such as SAGM up to 42 days. Platelets are stored at 20 to 24 degrees with agitation for only 5 days, and fresh frozen plasma at minus 30 degrees for a year.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blood Transfusion Services",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Leucodepletion of blood products principally reduces:",
   "options": [
    "Volume overload",
    "Bacterial contamination",
    "Febrile non-haemolytic reactions, HLA alloimmunisation and CMV transmission",
    "Iron overload"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Removing donor leucocytes prevents cytokine-mediated febrile reactions, reduces HLA sensitisation in patients needing repeated transfusion, and lowers transmission of leucocyte-associated viruses such as CMV.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blood Transfusion Services",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Irradiation of cellular blood products is performed to prevent:",
   "options": [
    "Febrile reactions",
    "Bacterial growth",
    "Haemolysis",
    "Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Gamma irradiation inactivates donor lymphocytes, preventing transfusion-associated GVHD in immunocompromised recipients, neonates and recipients of directed donations from relatives, in whom it is almost invariably fatal.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blood Transfusion Services",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PAT_MDS_009",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Pathology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Myelodysplasia, marrow failure & transfusion medicine",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The mandatory screening tests for donated blood in India include all EXCEPT:",
   "options": [
    "HIV 1 and 2",
    "Hepatitis B and C",
    "Malaria and syphilis",
    "Cytomegalovirus"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Indian regulations mandate screening for HIV, hepatitis B and C, syphilis and malaria. CMV screening is not routine and is reserved for selected immunocompromised recipients, for whom leucodepleted products are usually adequate.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blood Transfusion Services",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
