  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) acts by:",
   "options": [
    "Stimulating acetylcholine release",
    "Blocking acetylcholine release",
    "Blocking release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters glycine and GABA from Renshaw cells",
    "Blocking sodium channels"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely up motor axons to the spinal cord, cleaving synaptobrevin and preventing inhibitory neurotransmitter release, so unopposed motor activity causes trismus, risus sardonicus and opisthotonus.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Botulinum toxin causes paralysis by:",
   "options": [
    "Destroying the motor neuron",
    "Blocking GABA release",
    "Preventing release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction",
    "Blocking the acetylcholine receptor"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins blocking acetylcholine exocytosis, causing descending flaccid paralysis with diplopia, dysphagia and respiratory failure - the mirror image of tetanus which blocks inhibition.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_003",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Infant botulism differs from foodborne botulism in that it results from:",
   "options": [
    "Wound contamination",
    "Ingestion of spores, often in honey, which germinate in the immature gut",
    "Preformed toxin in canned food",
    "Person-to-person spread"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The infant colon lacks a protective flora, so ingested spores germinate and produce toxin in situ, causing a floppy infant with constipation, poor feeding and ptosis. Honey should not be given under one year.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Gas gangrene is most commonly caused by:",
   "options": [
    "Clostridium tetani",
    "Clostridium difficile",
    "Clostridium botulinum",
    "Clostridium perfringens type A with its alpha toxin (lecithinase)"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Alpha toxin is a lecithinase that destroys cell membranes causing myonecrosis, crepitus from gas production and profound toxaemia. The Nagler reaction demonstrates lecithinase activity on egg yolk agar.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_005",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Management of established gas gangrene centres on:",
   "options": [
    "Antibiotics alone",
    "Observation",
    "Urgent radical surgical debridement with high-dose penicillin and supportive care",
    "Hyperbaric oxygen alone"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Extensive debridement or amputation of all necrotic tissue is life-saving because antibiotics cannot penetrate dead tissue; penicillin with clindamycin, which suppresses toxin synthesis, and hyperbaric oxygen are adjuncts.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Prophylaxis for tetanus in a contaminated wound in an incompletely immunised patient requires:",
   "options": [
    "Human tetanus immunoglobulin plus tetanus toxoid at separate sites",
    "Immunoglobulin alone",
    "Toxoid alone",
    "Antibiotics alone"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Passive immunity from immunoglobulin gives immediate protection while toxoid induces active immunity; they must be given with separate syringes at separate sites. Thorough wound toilet remains essential.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_CLO_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Clostridia & anaerobes (tetanus, gas gangrene, botulism)",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Clostridioides difficile causes pseudomembranous colitis chiefly after:",
   "options": [
    "Broad-spectrum antibiotic use disrupting the normal colonic flora",
    "Dietary change",
    "Surgery alone",
    "Viral infection"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Clindamycin, cephalosporins and fluoroquinolones are classic precipitants. Toxins A and B damage the mucosa producing yellow pseudomembranous plaques; diagnosis is by toxin or GDH antigen detection with PCR.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Clostridium",
   "tags": [
