  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The intravenous induction agent of choice in a haemodynamically unstable trauma patient is:",
   "options": [
    "Midazolam",
    "Propofol",
    "Thiopentone",
    "Ketamine"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Ketamine preserves or increases blood pressure through sympathetic stimulation, unlike propofol and thiopentone which cause vasodilatation and myocardial depression. It also provides analgesia and bronchodilatation.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) is defined as the concentration preventing movement to a standard stimulus in:",
   "options": [
    "Ninety-five percent",
    "One hundred percent",
    "Fifty percent of subjects",
    "Ten percent"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "MAC is a measure of inhalational anaesthetic potency; lower MAC means greater potency. It is reduced by age, hypothermia, opioids and pregnancy, and increased by hyperthermia and chronic alcohol use.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Inhalational Anesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Which inhalational agent has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient and therefore the fastest induction?",
   "options": [
    "Ether",
    "Halothane",
    "Isoflurane",
    "Desflurane"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A low blood-gas solubility means the alveolar partial pressure rises quickly, giving rapid induction and recovery. Halothane and ether are highly soluble and therefore slow.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Inhalational Anesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by succinylcholine and volatile agents and is treated with:",
   "options": [
    "Dantrolene",
    "Naloxone",
    "Atropine",
    "Neostigmine"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A ryanodine receptor mutation causes uncontrolled sarcoplasmic calcium release with rigidity, hypercarbia, hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis. Dantrolene blocks calcium release and is life-saving alongside cooling and stopping the trigger.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Propofol is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Increased intracranial pressure",
    "Marked analgesia",
    "Rapid smooth induction and recovery with antiemetic properties but no analgesia",
    "Slow recovery"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Propofol gives clear-headed recovery with intrinsic antiemetic effect, but causes pain on injection, apnoea and hypotension, and provides no analgesia. Propofol infusion syndrome limits prolonged high-dose sedation.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Nitrous oxide is limited as a sole anaesthetic because:",
   "options": [
    "Its MAC exceeds 100 percent, so it cannot produce surgical anaesthesia alone",
    "It is too potent",
    "It causes malignant hyperthermia",
    "It is highly soluble"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Nitrous oxide has excellent analgesia but a MAC of 104%, so it is used as an adjunct to reduce the requirement of other agents. It diffuses into air-filled cavities, contraindicating it in pneumothorax and bowel obstruction.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Emergence delirium with vivid unpleasant dreams is characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Thiopentone",
    "Ketamine",
    "Propofol",
    "Sevoflurane"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Ketamine produces dissociative anaesthesia with emergence phenomena, reduced by premedication with a benzodiazepine and quiet recovery. It also raises intracranial and intraocular pressure and increases secretions.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Halothane hepatitis is thought to result from:",
   "options": [
    "An immune reaction to oxidative trifluoroacetyl metabolites binding hepatic proteins",
    "Biliary obstruction",
    "Hypoxia alone",
    "Direct dose-related toxicity"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Repeated exposure, obesity, middle age and female sex increase risk of this rare but often fatal massive hepatic necrosis, which is why halothane has been largely replaced by newer agents.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The second gas effect refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Slowed uptake",
    "Rebreathing",
    "Acceleration of uptake of a second anaesthetic gas by high-volume uptake of nitrous oxide",
    "Diffusion hypoxia"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Rapid absorption of large volumes of nitrous oxide concentrates the remaining alveolar gases, speeding uptake of the accompanying volatile agent. Conversely, diffusion hypoxia occurs at the end when nitrous oxide floods back into alveoli.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Inhalational Anesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_GA_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "General anaesthetics: IV induction & inhalational agents",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Thiopentone is contraindicated in:",
   "options": [
    "Diabetes",
    "Asthma treated with steroids",
    "Hypertension",
    "Acute intermittent porphyria"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Barbiturates induce ALA synthase, precipitating acute porphyric crises. Thiopentone is also avoided in airway obstruction and severe hypovolaemia because of apnoea and profound hypotension.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. General Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_MR_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Muscle relaxants, monitoring, anaesthesia machine & breathing circuits",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Succinylcholine is a:",
   "options": [
    "Depolarising neuromuscular blocker producing fasciculations followed by flaccid paralysis",
    "Non-depolarising blocker",
    "Anticholinesterase",
    "Competitive antagonist"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Succinylcholine binds and persistently depolarises the motor end plate, giving the fastest onset and shortest duration, ideal for rapid sequence induction. Its block is not reversed by neostigmine.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Skeletal Muscle Relaxants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_MR_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Muscle relaxants, monitoring, anaesthesia machine & breathing circuits",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Succinylcholine is contraindicated in burns and spinal cord injury after 24 to 48 hours because of:",
   "options": [
    "Extrajunctional receptor proliferation causing life-threatening hyperkalaemia",
    "Bradycardia",
    "Malignant hyperthermia only",
    "Prolonged apnoea"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Upregulated extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors release massive potassium on depolarisation, causing cardiac arrest. Other contraindications include hyperkalaemia, myopathies and a history of malignant hyperthermia.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Skeletal Muscle Relaxants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_MR_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Muscle relaxants, monitoring, anaesthesia machine & breathing circuits",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Prolonged apnoea after succinylcholine suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Atypical (deficient) pseudocholinesterase",
    "Hepatic failure",
    "Hyperkalaemia",
    "Renal failure"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Succinylcholine is hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase; genetic variants prolong block for hours, assessed by the dibucaine number. Management is continued ventilation until spontaneous recovery.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Skeletal Muscle Relaxants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_MR_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Muscle relaxants, monitoring, anaesthesia machine & breathing circuits",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade is reversed by:",
   "options": [
    "Naloxone",
    "Neostigmine with glycopyrrolate, or sugammadex for aminosteroid agents",
    "Succinylcholine",
    "Atropine alone"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Neostigmine raises synaptic acetylcholine to outcompete the blocker, and an antimuscarinic is co-administered to prevent bradycardia and secretions. Sugammadex directly encapsulates rocuronium and vecuronium.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Skeletal Muscle Relaxants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_MR_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Muscle relaxants, monitoring, anaesthesia machine & breathing circuits",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The neuromuscular blocker of choice in renal and hepatic failure is:",
   "options": [
    "Vecuronium",
    "Rocuronium",
    "Atracurium or cisatracurium, eliminated by Hofmann degradation",
    "Pancuronium"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Hofmann elimination is spontaneous degradation at body pH and temperature, independent of renal or hepatic function, making atracurium ideal in organ failure. Laudanosine accumulation is a theoretical concern.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Skeletal Muscle Relaxants",
   "tags": [
