   "topic": "Regional, spinal & epidural anaesthesia; postoperative care, pain & complications",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Epidural anaesthesia differs from spinal anaesthesia in that it:",
   "options": [
    "Has faster onset",
    "Cannot be continued postoperatively",
    "Uses smaller volumes",
    "Has slower onset, uses larger drug volumes and allows continuous catheter techniques"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The epidural space requires 10 to 20 mL of local anaesthetic acting on nerve roots, giving slower segmental onset but permitting catheter top-ups for labour and postoperative analgesia.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Epidural Anesthesia",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_RA_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Regional, spinal & epidural anaesthesia; postoperative care, pain & complications",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Absolute contraindications to central neuraxial block include:",
   "options": [
    "Obesity",
    "Patient refusal, coagulopathy, raised intracranial pressure and infection at the site",
    "Diabetes",
    "Hypertension"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Coagulopathy risks epidural haematoma with permanent paraplegia, and dural puncture with raised intracranial pressure risks coning. Local sepsis risks meningitis, and refusal is always absolute.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Regional Anesthesia",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_RA_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Local anaesthetics (max doses, toxicity)",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity presents initially with:",
   "options": [
    "Perioral tingling, tinnitus and metallic taste before seizures and cardiac arrest",
    "Rash only",
    "Immediate cardiac arrest",
    "Hypertension"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Central nervous system excitation precedes cardiovascular collapse, with bupivacaine notably cardiotoxic and resistant to resuscitation. Intravenous lipid emulsion is the specific rescue therapy alongside standard resuscitation.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Local Anaesthetics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "misfiled: the early features of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity belong with local anaesthetic dosing and toxicity, not with neuraxial technique. Its own reference already pointed to the local anaesthetics chapter"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_AN_RA_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Anaesthesia",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Regional, spinal & epidural anaesthesia; postoperative care, pain & complications",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Horner syndrome after an interscalene brachial plexus block indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Vascular injection",
    "Phrenic nerve palsy",
    "Total spinal",
    "Spread of local anaesthetic to the stellate ganglion"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Stellate ganglion involvement gives ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis; ipsilateral phrenic nerve palsy occurs in nearly all interscalene blocks, so it is avoided in significant respiratory disease. Both are self-limiting.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Peripheral Nerve Blocks",
   "tags": [
