   "topic": "Regional, spinal & epidural anaesthesia; postoperative care, pain & complications",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Patient-controlled analgesia is considered safe because:",
   "options": [
    "It has no lockout",
    "It uses non-opioids",
    "The dose is fixed",
    "Sedation precedes respiratory depression, so an oversedated patient stops pressing the button"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The inherent negative feedback requires that only the patient operates the device; proxy administration by relatives removes this safeguard and is the commonest cause of PCA-related respiratory depression.",
   "reference": "Morgan & Mikhail's Clinical Anesthesiology 6e, Ch. Postoperative Pain",
   "tags": [
