  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The first-line drug treatment for attention deficit hyperactivity disorder in a school-age child is:",
   "options": [
    "Haloperidol",
    "Methylphenidate",
    "Fluoxetine",
    "Sodium valproate"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Stimulants increase synaptic dopamine and noradrenaline in prefrontal circuits and are highly effective. Growth and appetite are monitored; atomoxetine is a non-stimulant alternative where misuse or tics are a concern.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. CNS Stimulants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Electroconvulsive therapy is most strongly indicated in:",
   "options": [
    "Generalised anxiety",
    "Severe depression with stupor, refusal to eat or high suicidal risk",
    "Mild depression",
    "Personality disorder"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "ECT acts faster than any antidepressant, making it the treatment of choice when delay is dangerous, and is also used in catatonia, puerperal psychosis and neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Memory disturbance is the main adverse effect.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Brain Stimulation Therapies",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The only absolute contraindication to electroconvulsive therapy is:",
   "options": [
    "Pregnancy",
    "Pacemaker",
    "Raised intracranial pressure",
    "Old age"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Raised intracranial pressure risks herniation. Pregnancy, old age and cardiac devices are relative considerations, and ECT is in fact often preferred in pregnancy to avoid teratogenic drugs.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Brain Stimulation Therapies",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Lithium requires regular monitoring because:",
   "options": [
    "It is hepatotoxic",
    "It has a narrow therapeutic index with renal and thyroid toxicity",
    "It causes retinopathy",
    "It causes agranulocytosis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Serum levels are kept at 0.6 to 1.0 mmol/L; toxicity causes coarse tremor, ataxia, vomiting, confusion and seizures. Thiazides, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and dehydration raise levels dangerously.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Drugs for Bipolar Disorder",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Clozapine is reserved for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and requires monitoring for:",
   "options": [
    "Renal failure",
    "Agranulocytosis by regular white cell counts",
    "Hearing loss",
    "Retinal damage"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Clozapine is the most effective antipsychotic in resistant illness and reduces suicidality, but causes agranulocytosis in about 1%, plus myocarditis, seizures, weight gain and hypersalivation, mandating registry-based blood monitoring.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Antipsychotic Drugs",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Hyperthermia, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability and raised creatine kinase",
    "Diarrhoea and hyperreflexia",
    "Isolated confusion",
    "Flaccidity and hypothermia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "NMS follows dopamine blockade, developing over days, and is treated by stopping the antipsychotic with cooling, dantrolene and bromocriptine. Serotonin syndrome differs in having hyperreflexia, clonus and diarrhoea with rapid onset.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Antipsychotic Drugs",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Tardive dyskinesia is:",
   "options": [
    "A late-onset involuntary orofacial movement disorder from prolonged dopamine blockade",
    "Reversible in all cases",
    "A withdrawal effect",
    "An acute dystonia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Tardive dyskinesia emerges after months to years of antipsychotic use and may be irreversible, so the lowest effective dose is used. Acute dystonia occurring within days responds promptly to anticholinergics.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Antipsychotic Drugs",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Cognitive behaviour therapy works on the principle that:",
   "options": [
    "Behaviour alone matters",
    "Insight is unnecessary",
    "Unconscious conflict must be uncovered",
    "Maladaptive thoughts drive emotions and behaviour, and can be identified and modified"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "CBT is structured, time-limited and problem-focused with homework, and has the strongest evidence base in depression, anxiety disorders and OCD. Psychoanalysis instead explores unconscious conflict over a long period.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Psychotherapies",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Systematic desensitisation for phobia involves:",
   "options": [
    "Graded exposure to a hierarchy of feared stimuli paired with relaxation",
    "Avoidance training",
    "Aversive conditioning",
    "Sudden maximal exposure"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Reciprocal inhibition pairs relaxation with progressively more anxiety-provoking stimuli. Flooding instead uses immediate maximal exposure, and both work by preventing avoidance so anxiety extinguishes naturally.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Psychotherapies",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Enuresis in a child older than 5 years is best managed initially with:",
   "options": [
    "Long-term imipramine",
    "Antibiotics",
    "Behavioural measures including an enuresis alarm and motivational therapy",
    "Fluid loading at night"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Alarms have the best long-term cure rates with no drug exposure. Desmopressin is useful for short-term cover such as school trips, and imipramine is now rarely used because of cardiotoxicity in overdose.",
   "reference": "Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry 12e, Ch. Elimination Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A sunburst periosteal reaction with Codman triangle in the metaphysis of a long bone suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Osteomyelitis",
    "Osteosarcoma",
    "Giant cell tumour",
    "Ewing sarcoma"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Aggressive periosteal elevation produces the Codman triangle, and spiculated new bone perpendicular to the cortex gives the sunburst appearance, typical of osteosarcoma around the knee in adolescents.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#image-based"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "An onion-peel (lamellated) periosteal reaction in the diaphysis of a long bone in a child suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Osteochondroma",
    "Enchondroma",
    "Ewing sarcoma",
    "Osteosarcoma"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Successive layers of periosteal new bone give the onion-skin pattern, characteristic of Ewing sarcoma, which is diaphyseal and may mimic osteomyelitis with fever and raised inflammatory markers.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A soap-bubble appearance in the epiphysis of the distal femur in a young adult indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Osteoid osteoma",
    "Giant cell tumour",
    "Simple bone cyst",
    "Osteosarcoma"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Giant cell tumour is an eccentric expansile lytic epiphyseal lesion with a narrow zone of transition abutting subchondral bone, giving a multiloculated soap-bubble look. It is locally aggressive with high recurrence.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Multiple punched-out lytic skull lesions without marginal sclerosis are characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Multiple myeloma",
    "Paget disease",
    "Osteopetrosis",
    "Fibrous dysplasia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Myeloma is purely lytic with no osteoblastic reaction, so lesions have no sclerotic rim and bone scans are typically negative - hence skeletal survey rather than scintigraphy is used for staging.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A sequestrum on radiograph in chronic osteomyelitis represents:",
   "options": [
    "Dead avascular bone within an involucrum of new bone",
    "A fracture callus",
    "A tumour",
    "New bone formation"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The sequestrum is dense necrotic bone acting as a nidus for persistent infection, surrounded by the involucrum of reactive new bone with cloacae draining pus. Surgical removal is required for cure.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Looser zones (pseudofractures) are characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Multiple myeloma",
    "Osteomalacia",
    "Osteoporosis",
    "Paget disease"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Looser zones are transverse translucent bands with sclerotic margins at sites of stress - pubic rami, femoral neck, ribs and scapula - reflecting unmineralised osteoid, and are pathognomonic of osteomalacia.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Bamboo spine on a lumbar radiograph indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Spinal tuberculosis",
    "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "Ankylosing spondylitis with syndesmophytes and ligamentous ossification",
    "Osteoarthritis"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Vertical syndesmophytes bridging vertebral bodies with ossification of spinal ligaments fuse the spine into a rigid bamboo-like column, preceded by bilateral symmetrical sacroiliitis which is the earliest sign.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A rat-bite or pencil-in-cup deformity of the digits suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Osteoarthritis",
    "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "Psoriatic arthritis",
    "Gout"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Psoriatic arthritis causes both erosion and proliferation, producing pencil-in-cup deformity and arthritis mutilans with distal interphalangeal involvement, unlike rheumatoid arthritis which spares the DIP joints.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A hair-on-end appearance of the skull vault is characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Paget disease",
    "Meningioma",
    "Thalassaemia major and other chronic haemolytic anaemias",
    "Osteosarcoma"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Marrow hyperplasia widens the diploic space and thins the outer table, with vertical trabeculae producing the hair-on-end pattern, along with maxillary overgrowth giving chipmunk facies.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_SKL_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Skeletal radiology & classic bone signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Subperiosteal bone resorption of the radial aspect of the middle phalanges is pathognomonic of:",
   "options": [
    "Scurvy",
    "Hyperparathyroidism",
    "Osteoporosis",
    "Rickets"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Subperiosteal resorption, best seen on the radial border of the middle phalanges of the index and middle fingers, is the most specific radiological sign of hyperparathyroidism, with salt-and-pepper skull and brown tumours.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Musculoskeletal",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The initial investigation of choice in suspected acute stroke is:",
   "options": [
    "Carotid Doppler",
    "MRI with contrast",
    "Non-contrast CT of the head to exclude haemorrhage",
    "Cerebral angiography"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Non-contrast CT rapidly distinguishes haemorrhagic from ischaemic stroke, which determines whether thrombolysis can be given. Early ischaemic changes may be subtle, while blood appears immediately hyperdense.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A biconvex (lentiform) hyperdense collection that does not cross suture lines indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Intracerebral haemorrhage",
    "Extradural haematoma",
    "Subdural haematoma",
    "Subarachnoid haemorrhage"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Extradural blood is limited by firm dural attachment at sutures, producing a lens shape, whereas subdural blood spreads freely across sutures in a crescent but is limited by dural reflections at the midline.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#image-based"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Blood in the basal cisterns on non-contrast CT indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Cerebral infarct",
    "Extradural haematoma",
    "Hydrocephalus",
    "Subarachnoid haemorrhage"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage fills the basal cisterns and sylvian fissures. If CT is negative but suspicion is high, lumbar puncture at 12 hours for xanthochromia is required, followed by CT angiography.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "MRI is superior to CT for imaging:",
   "options": [
    "Calcification",
    "Acute haemorrhage",
    "Posterior fossa, brainstem and early ischaemia by diffusion-weighted imaging",
    "Skull fractures"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "MRI avoids beam-hardening artefact in the posterior fossa and detects cytotoxic oedema within minutes on diffusion-weighted sequences. CT remains superior for acute blood, bone and calcification, and is faster.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Multiple ring-enhancing lesions in an HIV-positive patient most likely represent:",
   "options": [
    "Cerebral toxoplasmosis",
    "Cerebral infarct",
    "Meningioma",
    "Glioblastoma"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Toxoplasmosis typically gives multiple lesions in the basal ganglia and at the grey-white junction. Primary CNS lymphoma is usually solitary and periventricular; failure to respond to empirical anti-toxoplasma therapy prompts biopsy.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A butterfly lesion crossing the corpus callosum with irregular ring enhancement suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Metastasis",
    "Glioblastoma multiforme",
    "Abscess",
    "Meningioma"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Glioblastoma spreads across the corpus callosum giving the butterfly pattern, with central necrosis, irregular enhancement and marked vasogenic oedema. It is the commonest primary malignant brain tumour in adults.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "An extra-axial dural-based mass with homogeneous enhancement and a dural tail is characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Meningioma",
    "Abscess",
    "Metastasis",
    "Glioma"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Meningiomas arise from arachnoid cap cells, are extra-axial with a broad dural base, enhance avidly and homogeneously, may calcify and cause hyperostosis of adjacent bone.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Restricted diffusion on DWI with low ADC within a ring-enhancing lesion favours:",
   "options": [
    "Meningioma",
    "Pyogenic brain abscess over necrotic tumour",
    "Infarct only",
    "Haemorrhage"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Viscous pus restricts water diffusion, whereas necrotic tumour does not, making DWI the key discriminator between abscess and cystic or necrotic neoplasm when enhancement patterns overlap.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Periventricular calcification in a neonate suggests congenital infection with:",
   "options": [
    "Toxoplasma",
    "Syphilis",
    "Cytomegalovirus",
    "Rubella"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "CMV causes periventricular calcification with microcephaly, while toxoplasmosis causes scattered diffuse intracranial calcification with hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis - a useful distinguishing pattern.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_CNS_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "CNS imaging: CT/MRI in stroke, trauma & space-occupying lesions",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The hyperdense middle cerebral artery sign on non-contrast CT indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Normal finding",
    "Calcification",
    "Haemorrhage",
    "Acute thrombus within the vessel, an early sign of infarction"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A hyperdense artery represents fresh intraluminal thrombus and is one of the earliest CT signs of ischaemic stroke, appearing before parenchymal changes such as loss of grey-white differentiation and insular ribbon sign.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Neuroradiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Free gas under the diaphragm on an erect chest radiograph indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Hollow viscus perforation",
    "Paralytic ileus",
    "Ascites",
    "Intestinal obstruction"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Pneumoperitoneum is best seen on an erect chest film or left lateral decubitus, and in a patient with an acute abdomen indicates perforation, most often of a duodenal ulcer, requiring urgent surgery.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#image-based"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Multiple air-fluid levels with dilated central small bowel loops showing valvulae conniventes indicate:",
   "options": [
    "Small bowel obstruction",
    "Large bowel obstruction",
    "Paralytic ileus",
    "Volvulus"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Valvulae conniventes cross the full bowel width and the loops lie centrally, unlike colonic haustra which cross only part of the lumen with peripheral loops. More than three air-fluid levels suggests obstruction.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The investigation of choice for suspected acute appendicitis in a young adult male is:",
   "options": [
    "Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen",
    "Barium enema",
    "Plain radiograph",
    "MRI"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "CT has high sensitivity and specificity showing a thickened appendix with periappendiceal fat stranding. Ultrasound is preferred first in children and pregnancy to avoid radiation, and MRI in pregnancy if ultrasound is inconclusive.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The imaging investigation of choice for gallstones is:",
   "options": [
    "CT",
    "MRI",
    "Plain radiograph",
    "Ultrasound of the abdomen"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Ultrasound detects over 95% of gallstones as mobile echogenic foci with posterior acoustic shadowing, and also shows wall thickening and duct dilatation. Only about 10 to 15% of gallstones are radio-opaque on plain film.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A coffee-bean sign on abdominal radiograph indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Sigmoid volvulus",
    "Perforation",
    "Appendicitis",
    "Intussusception"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A massively dilated closed loop arising from the pelvis with apposed medial walls forms the coffee-bean or omega sign. Caecal volvulus instead displaces the dilated loop towards the left upper quadrant.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A string sign of Kantor on barium study of the terminal ileum suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Coeliac disease",
    "Crohn disease",
    "Ulcerative colitis",
    "Tuberculosis only"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Narrowing of the terminal ileal lumen from oedema and fibrosis produces a thin barium stream. Cobblestoning, skip lesions and fistulae are other Crohn features; ileocaecal tuberculosis is the main differential in India.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The target or doughnut sign on abdominal ultrasound in an infant indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Pyloric stenosis",
    "Hirschsprung disease",
    "Appendicitis",
    "Intussusception"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Concentric rings of invaginated bowel produce a target sign in transverse and pseudokidney in longitudinal section. Ultrasound is the investigation of choice, avoiding radiation, and guides pneumatic reduction.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "MRCP is preferred over ERCP for biliary imaging because it:",
   "options": [
    "Is cheaper always",
    "Is non-invasive and avoids the risk of post-procedure pancreatitis",
    "Is therapeutic",
    "Uses ionising radiation"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "MRCP is purely diagnostic with no risk of pancreatitis, bleeding or perforation, so ERCP is now reserved for cases needing intervention such as stone extraction, stenting or sphincterotomy.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Rigler sign on a supine abdominal radiograph refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Bowel wall thickening",
    "Gas in the biliary tree",
    "Calcified gallstones",
    "Visualisation of both sides of the bowel wall due to free intraperitoneal gas"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Air on both the luminal and peritoneal surfaces outlines the wall, indicating pneumoperitoneum on a supine film where free gas under the diaphragm cannot be assessed. The football sign is a large midline gas collection.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_ABD_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abdominal & GIT imaging; acute abdomen signs",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Gas in the biliary tree (pneumobilia) with small bowel obstruction and an ectopic gallstone constitutes:",
   "options": [
    "Virchow triad",
    "Charcot triad",
    "Beck triad",
    "Rigler triad of gallstone ileus"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A large gallstone erodes through a cholecystoduodenal fistula and impacts at the terminal ileum. Rigler triad of pneumobilia, obstruction and an ectopic calcified stone is diagnostic.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Abdominal Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The most accurate ultrasound parameter for dating a pregnancy in the first trimester is:",
   "options": [
    "Femur length",
    "Biparietal diameter",
    "Crown-rump length",
    "Abdominal circumference"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Crown-rump length between 7 and 13 weeks dates pregnancy to within about 5 days because early growth is highly uniform; accuracy declines progressively with advancing gestation.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Obstetric Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The anomaly scan in pregnancy is optimally performed at:",
   "options": [
    "18 to 20 weeks",
    "8 weeks",
    "28 weeks",
    "36 weeks"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "By 18 to 20 weeks organogenesis is complete and fetal size permits detailed structural assessment, while still allowing time for further investigation and decision-making within legal limits for termination.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Obstetric Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The mammographic finding most suspicious of malignancy is:",
   "options": [
    "A well-defined round opacity",
    "Diffuse symmetrical density",
    "Clustered pleomorphic branching microcalcification with a spiculated mass",
    "Coarse popcorn calcification"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Fine pleomorphic linear branching microcalcifications suggest ductal carcinoma in situ, and a spiculated mass with architectural distortion suggests invasive carcinoma. Popcorn calcification indicates an involuting fibroadenoma.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Breast Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#image-based"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The imaging modality of choice for a breast lump in a woman under 35 years is:",
   "options": [
    "Mammography",
    "CT",
    "MRI",
    "Ultrasound"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Dense fibroglandular tissue in younger women limits mammographic sensitivity, so ultrasound is preferred and reliably distinguishes cystic from solid lesions. Mammography is the screening tool above 40.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Breast Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The BI-RADS classification is used to:",
   "options": [
    "Assess response to chemotherapy",
    "Grade tumours histologically",
    "Standardise reporting and management recommendations for breast imaging",
    "Stage breast cancer"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "BI-RADS categories run from 0 (incomplete) through 1 and 2 (normal or benign) to 5 (highly suggestive of malignancy) and 6 (biopsy-proven), each with a defined management recommendation.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Breast Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A snowstorm appearance of the uterine cavity with no fetal parts indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Twin pregnancy",
    "Fibroid uterus",
    "Complete hydatidiform mole",
    "Missed abortion"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Hydropic villi produce multiple small cystic spaces giving the snowstorm or cluster-of-grapes pattern, usually with a uterus large for dates, very high hCG and bilateral theca lutein cysts.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Obstetric Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Transvaginal ultrasound is preferred over transabdominal in early pregnancy because it:",
   "options": [
    "Uses lower frequency",
    "Requires a full bladder",
    "Gives higher resolution allowing earlier detection of the gestational sac and cardiac activity",
    "Is safer"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The higher frequency probe placed close to the pelvic organs improves resolution, detecting an intrauterine sac from about 4.5 to 5 weeks and cardiac activity from 6 weeks - crucial in suspected ectopic pregnancy.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Obstetric Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The most reliable ultrasound sign of ectopic pregnancy is:",
   "options": [
    "A thickened endometrium",
    "An adnexal mass with an extrauterine gestational sac containing a yolk sac or embryo",
    "An empty uterus alone",
    "Free fluid alone"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Definitive diagnosis requires visualising an extrauterine sac, though this is uncommon; more often an empty uterus with beta-hCG above the discriminatory zone, an adnexal mass and free fluid raise suspicion.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Obstetric Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In the PC-PNDT Act context, ultrasound in pregnancy must not be used to:",
   "options": [
    "Detect anomalies",
    "Confirm viability",
    "Determine or disclose the sex of the fetus",
    "Assess growth"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques Act prohibits sex determination and disclosure to combat female foeticide; registration of clinics, record maintenance and prominent display of notices are mandatory.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. PC-PNDT Act",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_OBS_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Obstetric, breast & pelvic imaging",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Hysterosalpingography is best performed:",
   "options": [
    "During menstruation",
    "Just before menstruation",
    "In the luteal phase",
    "In the follicular phase, days 7 to 10, after menstruation has ceased"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Performing it after bleeding stops but before ovulation avoids disturbing an early pregnancy and gives a clean cavity for assessment. It is contraindicated in active pelvic infection and pregnancy.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Gynaecological Imaging",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The radionuclide most widely used in diagnostic nuclear medicine is:",
   "options": [
    "Technetium-99m",
    "Iodine-131",
    "Cobalt-60",
    "Radium-226"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Technetium-99m has a 6-hour half-life and emits pure gamma radiation at 140 keV, ideal for imaging with minimal patient dose, and is available from a portable molybdenum-99 generator.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Nuclear Medicine",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A bone scan using technetium-99m MDP shows increased uptake in all EXCEPT:",
   "options": [
    "Healing fracture",
    "Osteomyelitis",
    "Osteoblastic metastases",
    "Multiple myeloma"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Bone scans detect osteoblastic activity, so purely lytic myeloma lesions are typically cold or normal, which is why skeletal survey or whole-body MRI is preferred for myeloma staging.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Nuclear Medicine",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Iodine-131 is used therapeutically in thyroid disease because it emits:",
   "options": [
    "X-rays",
    "Beta particles causing local tissue destruction, with gamma emission allowing imaging",
    "Only gamma rays",
    "Only alpha particles"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Beta emission destroys thyroid tissue over a short range, treating thyrotoxicosis and thyroid carcinoma, while the gamma component permits scanning. It is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Nuclear Medicine",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "FDG-PET detects tumours by exploiting their:",
   "options": [
    "Reduced blood flow",
    "Increased glucose uptake and glycolysis",
    "Increased calcium content",
    "Reduced oxygen use"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Fluorodeoxyglucose accumulates in metabolically active cells reflecting the Warburg effect, making PET valuable for staging, detecting recurrence and assessing treatment response, usually fused with CT for localisation.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Nuclear Medicine",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Brachytherapy differs from teletherapy in that the radiation source is:",
   "options": [
    "Systemically administered",
    "Not used in cancer",
    "At a distance from the patient",
    "Placed within or adjacent to the tumour"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Brachytherapy delivers a very high local dose with rapid fall-off by the inverse square law, sparing surrounding tissue, and is central to treating carcinoma cervix. Teletherapy uses an external beam at a distance.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Radiotherapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most radiosensitive cells in the body are:",
   "options": [
    "Bone cells",
    "Rapidly dividing cells such as bone marrow, gonads and intestinal mucosa",
    "Muscle cells",
    "Neurons"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "By the law of Bergonie and Tribondeau, radiosensitivity increases with mitotic activity and decreases with differentiation, which explains both radiotherapy toxicity and the effectiveness of fractionation.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Radiotherapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Fractionation in radiotherapy is used to:",
   "options": [
    "Reduce total dose",
    "Shorten treatment",
    "Avoid imaging",
    "Allow normal tissue repair and tumour reoxygenation between doses"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Dividing the total dose exploits differences between tumour and normal tissue in the four Rs - repair, redistribution, repopulation and reoxygenation - improving the therapeutic ratio.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Radiotherapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) is indicated for:",
   "options": [
    "Refractory variceal bleeding or ascites in portal hypertension",
    "Aortic aneurysm",
    "Renal artery stenosis",
    "Acute cholecystitis"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "TIPS creates a low-resistance channel between the portal and hepatic veins, decompressing varices. Its main complication is hepatic encephalopathy from diverting portal blood away from the liver.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Interventional Radiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Uterine artery embolisation is an established alternative to surgery for:",
   "options": [
    "Symptomatic uterine fibroids and intractable postpartum haemorrhage",
    "Cervical carcinoma",
    "Ovarian cancer",
    "Endometriosis"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Embolising both uterine arteries causes fibroid infarction with symptom relief while preserving the uterus, and is life-saving in postpartum haemorrhage where fertility is to be conserved.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Interventional Radiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_RD_NUC_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Radiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nuclear medicine, radiotherapy basics & interventional radiology",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The ALARA principle in radiation protection stands for:",
   "options": [
    "As Low As Reasonably Achievable",
    "Assess Local And Regional Absorption",
    "Average Level Age Related Adjustment",
    "Always Limit All Radiation Access"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "ALARA underpins justification, optimisation and dose limitation - the three pillars of radiation protection - implemented through shielding, distance, time minimisation and avoiding unnecessary investigations.",
   "reference": "Sutton's Textbook of Radiology and Imaging 7e, Ch. Radiation Protection",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MIC_NEI_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Microbiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Neisseria, Haemophilus, Bordetella, Brucella",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Gram-negative intracellular diplococci within neutrophils in a urethral smear from a symptomatic man indicate:",
   "options": [
    "Gonorrhoea, for which the smear is highly sensitive and specific",
    "Candidiasis",
    "Syphilis",
    "Chlamydia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "In symptomatic male urethritis the Gram-stained smear is diagnostic, but sensitivity is poor in women and at extragenital sites, where culture or nucleic acid amplification is required.",
   "reference": "Ananthanarayan and Paniker's Textbook of Microbiology 11e, Ch. Neisseria",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
