  {
   "id": "Q_OP_TRA_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Ocular trauma, orbit, ocular pharmacology & blindness control",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Atropine as a cycloplegic is preferred in children for refraction because it:",
   "options": [
    "Is short-acting",
    "Causes no side effects",
    "Produces complete cycloplegia, essential when accommodation is strong",
    "Constricts the pupil"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Children have powerful accommodation that masks hypermetropia, so full cycloplegia with atropine ointment is used under 5 years. Systemic toxicity causes flushing, fever, dry mouth and delirium.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Ocular Pharmacology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OP_TRA_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Ocular trauma, orbit, ocular pharmacology & blindness control",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Under the National Programme for Control of Blindness, blindness in India is defined as presenting visual acuity in the better eye of less than:",
   "options": [
    "1/60",
    "6/60",
    "3/60",
    "6/18"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "India revised its definition to below 3/60 to align with WHO, from the earlier 6/60 criterion. Cataract remains the leading cause of blindness, followed by refractive error, glaucoma and diabetic retinopathy.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blindness",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OP_TRA_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Ocular trauma, orbit, ocular pharmacology & blindness control",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of blindness in India is:",
   "options": [
    "Glaucoma",
    "Trachoma",
    "Cataract",
    "Vitamin A deficiency"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Cataract accounts for the majority of blindness and is reversible by surgery, which is why the national programme emphasises cataract surgical rate and intraocular lens implantation. Uncorrected refractive error is the leading cause of visual impairment.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blindness",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OP_TRA_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Ophthalmology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Ocular trauma, orbit, ocular pharmacology & blindness control",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Vision 2020: The Right to Sight targets which priority conditions?",
   "options": [
    "Only trauma",
    "Cataract, refractive error, childhood blindness, trachoma, onchocerciasis and glaucoma",
    "Only diabetes",
    "Only cataract"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The global initiative targets avoidable blindness through disease control, human resource development and infrastructure. In India cataract, refractive error, childhood blindness and glaucoma are the operational priorities.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Blindness",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The earliest and most reliable clinical sign of acute compartment syndrome is:",
   "options": [
    "Pallor",
    "Pain out of proportion to injury, worsened by passive stretch of the compartment muscles",
    "Absent distal pulses",
    "Paralysis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Pulselessness and paralysis are late signs occurring after irreversible muscle necrosis. Pain on passive stretch with a tense swollen compartment demands immediate fasciotomy; a normal pulse never excludes the diagnosis.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Volkmann ischaemic contracture most classically follows:",
   "options": [
    "Femoral shaft fracture",
    "Supracondylar fracture of the humerus in a child",
    "Colles fracture",
    "Clavicle fracture"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Brachial artery injury or a tight cast causes forearm compartment ischaemia, and subsequent fibrosis produces flexion contracture of the fingers that relaxes on wrist flexion. Early recognition and fasciotomy prevent it.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Fat embolism syndrome after a long bone fracture typically presents:",
   "options": [
    "After two weeks",
    "24 to 72 hours after injury with hypoxia, confusion and petechiae",
    "Immediately",
    "Only after surgery"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The classic triad of respiratory distress, neurological change and a petechial rash over the axilla, conjunctiva and upper chest appears after a latent interval. Management is supportive with oxygen and early fracture fixation.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Delayed union differs from non-union in that:",
   "options": [
    "Union is slower than expected but the healing process is still ongoing",
    "There is infection",
    "The fracture is comminuted",
    "Healing has permanently ceased"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Non-union shows a sclerosed sealed medullary canal with persistent mobility and no radiological progression over three months, requiring bone grafting and stable fixation; delayed union may still unite with time and protection.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Avascular necrosis is a particular risk after fracture of the:",
   "options": [
    "Radial shaft",
    "Fibula",
    "Clavicle",
    "Femoral neck, scaphoid and talus"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "These bones have a retrograde blood supply entering distally, so a proximal fracture interrupts perfusion of the proximal fragment. This is why undisplaced scaphoid fractures are immobilised even with a normal initial radiograph.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A greenstick fracture is characteristic of children because:",
   "options": [
    "They have thin periosteum",
    "Their bones are brittle",
    "Their bone is soft with a thick periosteum, so it bends and breaks on one cortex only",
    "They heal slowly"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Paediatric bone is more porous and elastic with a thick osteogenic periosteum, giving incomplete fractures, rapid healing and considerable remodelling potential, but also physeal injuries with growth disturbance.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures in Children",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "An open (compound) fracture requires:",
   "options": [
    "Immediate internal fixation only",
    "Urgent wound debridement, antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis with fracture stabilisation",
    "Delayed treatment",
    "Closed reduction alone"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The Gustilo-Anderson classification grades severity. Early antibiotics, thorough debridement and stabilisation reduce infection, which is the principal complication; primary closure is avoided in contaminated wounds.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Open Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The Salter-Harris classification describes:",
   "options": [
    "Adult intra-articular fractures",
    "Physeal (growth plate) injuries in children, with higher types carrying greater growth arrest risk",
    "Pelvic fractures",
    "Spinal injuries"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Type I is through the physis, II extends into metaphysis (commonest), III into epiphysis, IV crosses both, and V is a crush injury with the worst prognosis for growth arrest and deformity.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures in Children",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Compartment pressure measurement supports fasciotomy when the delta pressure (diastolic minus compartment pressure) is:",
   "options": [
    "Less than 30 mmHg",
    "Greater than 60 mmHg",
    "Greater than 100 mmHg",
    "Equal to systolic pressure"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A delta pressure below 30 mmHg indicates inadequate perfusion. Measurement supports but never replaces clinical judgement, and in a convincing clinical picture fasciotomy should not await manometry.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_FRC_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fracture principles, complications & compartment syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Complex regional pain syndrome (Sudeck atrophy) after a fracture presents with:",
   "options": [
    "Disproportionate burning pain, swelling, vasomotor changes and patchy osteopenia",
    "Rapid resolution",
    "Normal radiographs always",
    "Painless stiffness"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Sympathetically mediated pain with allodynia, trophic skin changes and spotty osteoporosis follows apparently minor injury, classically after Colles fracture. Early mobilisation, physiotherapy and analgesia are the mainstay.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
