   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Lower limb & pelvic injuries; hip dislocation",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A fracture of the shaft of the femur in an adult may cause hypovolaemic shock because it can lose:",
   "options": [
    "One to one and a half litres of blood",
    "100 mL",
    "Under 500 mL",
    "No significant blood"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Closed femoral shaft fractures conceal substantial blood loss, and bilateral fractures or associated pelvic injury can cause profound shock. Early splinting reduces bleeding and pain.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Lower Limb Injuries",
   "tags": [
