    "Septic arthritis",
    "A tumour",
    "Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral capital epiphysis in a child of 4 to 8 years"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "It presents with a painless limp and restricted abduction and internal rotation. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis instead affects obese adolescents, giving an externally rotated limb with a positive Trethowan sign.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Paediatric Orthopaedics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Neuropraxia is best described as:",
   "options": [
    "Complete nerve transection",
    "Axonal loss with intact sheath",
    "Permanent injury",
    "A temporary conduction block with intact axons, recovering fully within weeks"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Seddon classified injuries as neuropraxia (conduction block, full recovery), axonotmesis (axonal disruption with intact endoneurium, recovery by regeneration at about 1 mm per day) and neurotmesis (complete division needing repair).",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Peripheral Nerve Injuries",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Tinel sign in a regenerating nerve indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Complete transection",
    "Permanent damage",
    "The advancing front of regenerating axons, moving distally with recovery",
    "Infection"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Percussion over the nerve elicits paraesthesia at the level reached by regenerating fibres; a Tinel sign that advances distally on serial examination indicates ongoing recovery, whereas a static sign suggests a block.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Peripheral Nerve Injuries",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Erb palsy from upper brachial plexus injury (C5-C6) produces:",
   "options": [
    "Claw hand",
    "Wrist drop",
    "The waiter's tip position with arm adducted, internally rotated and forearm pronated",
    "Winged scapula"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Loss of deltoid, biceps, brachialis and supraspinatus gives this posture, seen after shoulder dystocia or a fall on the shoulder. Klumpke palsy of C8-T1 causes claw hand and may include Horner syndrome.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Brachial Plexus Injuries",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Winging of the scapula results from paralysis of serratus anterior due to injury of:",
   "options": [
    "The suprascapular nerve",
    "The axillary nerve",
    "The long thoracic nerve",
    "The thoracodorsal nerve"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The long thoracic nerve from C5, C6 and C7 is vulnerable during axillary surgery and from carrying heavy loads on the shoulder. Winging becomes obvious on pushing against a wall.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Peripheral Nerve Injuries",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A plaster cast applied for an acute fracture should be:",
   "options": [
    "Applied only after swelling settles",
    "Complete and tight",
    "Left for six weeks without review",
    "Split or a slab used initially to accommodate swelling"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "A tight circumferential cast over a swelling limb causes compartment syndrome and pressure sores. Patients are warned about increasing pain, numbness and colour change, and the limb is elevated with active finger or toe movement.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fractures",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "External fixation is particularly indicated in:",
   "options": [
    "All closed fractures",
    "Undisplaced fractures",
    "Grossly contaminated open fractures and damage control in polytrauma",
    "Intracapsular fractures"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "External fixation stabilises bone away from the zone of injury, avoiding implanting metal into contaminated tissue, and is quick in an unstable polytrauma patient, allowing definitive fixation once the patient stabilises.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fracture Management",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The principle of intramedullary nailing is that it provides:",
   "options": [
    "Fixation for intra-articular fractures",
    "Absolute rigid fixation",
    "Load-sharing fixation allowing early weight-bearing in diaphyseal fractures",
    "No stability"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "A nail acts as an internal splint sharing load with bone, promoting secondary bone healing with callus and permitting early mobilisation. Plates provide load-bearing rigid fixation with primary bone healing.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Fracture Management",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In leprosy, the most important measure to prevent deformity is:",
   "options": [
    "Prolonged bed rest",
    "Amputation",
    "Splinting alone",
    "Early detection and treatment of neuritis, with care of the anaesthetic limb"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Deformity results from nerve damage causing anaesthesia, paralysis and autonomic loss, so prompt steroids for acute neuritis, protective footwear, daily self-examination and care of insensitive hands and feet prevent disability.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Leprosy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A foot drop splint is prescribed principally to:",
   "options": [
    "Cure the nerve lesion",
    "Prevent equinus contracture while awaiting nerve recovery",
    "Reduce pain only",
    "Restore power"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Passive stretching and an ankle-foot orthosis maintain the joint in a functional position so that when reinnervation occurs the limb is supple and useful; contracture would otherwise negate neurological recovery.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Rehabilitation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OR_NRV_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Orthopedics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nerve injuries, implants, splints & rehabilitation",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most important principle of rehabilitation after a musculoskeletal injury is:",
   "options": [
    "Bed rest for six weeks",
    "Prolonged complete immobilisation",
    "Early controlled mobilisation to prevent stiffness, muscle wasting and osteopenia",
    "Avoiding physiotherapy"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Prolonged immobilisation causes joint stiffness, muscle atrophy, disuse osteopenia and thromboembolism, so modern fracture care aims for stable fixation permitting early movement - the fracture disease concept.",
   "reference": "Maheshwari Essential Orthopaedics 6e, Ch. Rehabilitation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A macule is defined as:",
   "options": [
    "A raised lesion",
    "A depression",
    "A fluid-filled lesion",
    "A flat circumscribed area of colour change less than 1 cm, without elevation"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Precise terminology guides diagnosis: a patch is a macule over 1 cm, a papule is raised under 1 cm, a plaque over 1 cm, a nodule is deeper, a vesicle contains clear fluid under 1 cm and a bulla over 1 cm.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Basic Dermatology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The layer of epidermis responsible for the barrier function of skin is:",
   "options": [
    "Stratum basale",
    "Stratum spinosum",
    "Stratum granulosum",
    "Stratum corneum"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The corneocytes embedded in intercellular lipid form a brick-and-mortar barrier limiting water loss and entry of irritants. Its disruption underlies atopic dermatitis and explains the value of emollients.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Structure of Skin",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Koebner phenomenon refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Healing of lesions",
    "Appearance of characteristic lesions at sites of trauma",
    "Pigment change",
    "Spread of infection"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Koebnerisation occurs in psoriasis, lichen planus, vitiligo and viral warts, so surgery and trauma to affected skin should be minimised during active disease.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Basic Dermatology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The Auspitz sign, pinpoint bleeding on removing a scale, is characteristic of:",
   "options": [
    "Eczema",
    "Pemphigus",
    "Lichen planus",
    "Psoriasis"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Removing the silvery scale exposes thinned suprapapillary epidermis over dilated dermal capillaries. Auspitz sign, the candle grease sign and Koebner phenomenon together strongly suggest psoriasis.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Papulosquamous Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Nikolsky sign, in which the epidermis shears off with lateral pressure, is positive in:",
   "options": [
    "Bullous pemphigoid",
    "Pemphigus vulgaris and toxic epidermal necrolysis",
    "Lichen planus",
    "Psoriasis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Loss of epidermal cohesion from acantholysis or full-thickness necrosis produces a positive sign, indicating a fragile intraepidermal or subepidermal split. Pemphigoid has a firm subepidermal blister with a negative sign.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Vesiculobullous Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Wood lamp examination is useful for diagnosing:",
   "options": [
    "Fungal infections, vitiligo, erythrasma and pigment disorders",
    "Urticaria",
    "Eczema",
    "Psoriasis"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Ultraviolet A at 365 nm shows coral-red fluorescence in erythrasma, green in some tinea capitis, and accentuates depigmented vitiligo patches especially in fair skin.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Investigations in Dermatology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A KOH mount of skin scrapings is used to demonstrate:",
   "options": [
    "Mites only",
    "Fungal hyphae and spores",
    "Viral inclusions",
    "Bacteria"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Potassium hydroxide dissolves keratin leaving fungal elements visible, giving a rapid bedside diagnosis of dermatophytosis. Skin scrapings for scabies mites are examined in mineral oil rather than KOH.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Investigations in Dermatology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Dermatitis is described as spongiotic when histology shows:",
   "options": [
    "Subepidermal blister",
    "Acanthosis with parakeratosis",
    "Basal cell degeneration",
    "Intercellular oedema of the epidermis, characteristic of eczema"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Spongiosis separating keratinocytes is the histological hallmark of eczematous dermatitis at all stages. Psoriasis shows parakeratosis with Munro microabscesses, and lichen planus a lichenoid interface infiltrate.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Eczema",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The potency of a topical corticosteroid should be chosen so that:",
   "options": [
    "Potency does not matter",
    "The strongest is always used",
    "Only oral steroids are used",
    "Low potency is used on the face, flexures and in children, and higher potency on palms and soles"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Absorption varies over a hundredfold by site, so potent steroids on the face and flexures cause atrophy, telangiectasia and striae, while thick palmar and plantar skin needs potent preparations to be effective.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Topical Therapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_STR_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Structure of skin & approach to dermatological diagnosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The rule of wet-to-wet and dry-to-dry in dermatological therapy means:",
   "options": [
    "Always use lotions",
    "Always use ointments",
    "Use wet preparations for weeping lesions and greasy ointments for dry scaly lesions",
    "Vehicle is irrelevant"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Acute weeping dermatitis is treated with wet compresses and lotions, subacute lesions with creams, and chronic dry lichenified lesions with ointments, which are more occlusive and enhance penetration.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Topical Therapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Honey-coloured crusted lesions around the nose and mouth of a child indicate:",
   "options": [
    "Eczema",
    "Impetigo contagiosa",
    "Herpes simplex",
    "Tinea faciei"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Non-bullous impetigo is caused by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes, is highly contagious and responds to topical mupirocin, with oral antibiotics for extensive disease. Streptococcal impetigo may be followed by acute glomerulonephritis.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Bacterial Infections",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Erysipelas differs from cellulitis in that it:",
   "options": [
    "Is deeper",
    "Involves the upper dermis and lymphatics with a sharply demarcated raised border",
    "Has an indistinct margin",
    "Is non-infective"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Erysipelas is a superficial streptococcal infection with a clearly defined advancing edge, commonly on the face or leg, while cellulitis involves deeper subcutaneous tissue with a diffuse poorly defined margin.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Bacterial Infections",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "An annular scaly plaque with central clearing and an active raised scaly border indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Lichen planus",
    "Discoid eczema",
    "Tinea corporis",
    "Psoriasis"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Dermatophyte infection spreads centrifugally with an active edge where scrapings for KOH should be taken. Prior topical steroid use produces tinea incognito with an atypical, less scaly appearance.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Fungal Infections",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Pityriasis versicolor is caused by:",
   "options": [
    "Candida",
    "Microsporum",
    "Trichophyton",
    "Malassezia furfur, showing a spaghetti and meatballs pattern on KOH"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Hypopigmented or hyperpigmented scaly macules on the trunk show fine scale on stretching, and KOH reveals short hyphae with spores. Topical ketoconazole or selenium sulfide is used; pigment takes months to normalise.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Fungal Infections",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Nocturnal itching with burrows in finger webs affecting several family members indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Urticaria",
    "Eczema",
    "Pediculosis",
    "Scabies"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Sarcoptes scabiei burrows in the stratum corneum, causing intense night-time itch from a delayed hypersensitivity response. Permethrin 5% applied to the whole body below the neck, with simultaneous treatment of all contacts, is standard.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Infestations",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_INF_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Bacterial, fungal & viral skin infections; scabies, pediculosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Crusted (Norwegian) scabies is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Massive mite infestation with thick crusts in immunosuppressed patients, and high infectivity",
    "Few mites",
    "Occurrence only in children",
    "Absence of scale"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Impaired immunity permits enormous mite numbers with hyperkeratotic crusts, often with little itch, making these patients highly contagious and a source of institutional outbreaks. Oral ivermectin with topical therapy is needed.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Infestations",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
