   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Atopic dermatitis in an infant characteristically affects:",
   "options": [
    "Palms only",
    "The face and extensor surfaces, moving to flexures in older children",
    "Scalp only",
    "Flexures from birth"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Infantile eczema involves cheeks and extensors, whereas from about 2 years it becomes flexural at antecubital and popliteal fossae. It is part of the atopic triad with asthma and allergic rhinitis.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Eczema",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The cornerstone of long-term management of atopic dermatitis is:",
   "options": [
    "Continuous potent topical steroids",
    "Regular liberal emollients with avoidance of irritants",
    "Antibiotics",
    "Systemic steroids"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Barrier repair with emollients reduces flares and steroid requirement. Topical corticosteroids are used in short bursts for flares, with calcineurin inhibitors as steroid-sparing agents on the face and flexures.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Eczema",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Allergic contact dermatitis is mediated by:",
   "options": [
    "Type IV delayed cell-mediated hypersensitivity, confirmed by patch testing",
    "Type III immune complexes",
    "Type II cytotoxic reaction",
    "Type I IgE hypersensitivity"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Sensitisation requires prior exposure, and reaction appears 24 to 72 hours later, so patch tests are read at 48 and 96 hours. Irritant contact dermatitis instead needs no sensitisation and occurs on first exposure.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Eczema",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
