  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Acute urticaria is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Transient itchy weals lasting less than 24 hours at any one site, with no residual mark",
    "Scarring",
    "Painful lesions",
    "Weals lasting a week"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Individual weals resolving within 24 hours without bruising is the defining feature. Lesions persisting longer with purpura or pigmentation suggest urticarial vasculitis and warrant biopsy.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Urticaria",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The first-line treatment of chronic urticaria is:",
   "options": [
    "Systemic corticosteroids",
    "Antibiotics",
    "Adrenaline",
    "Second-generation non-sedating H1 antihistamines, up-titrated as needed"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Non-sedating antihistamines can be increased up to fourfold; omalizumab and ciclosporin are used in refractory cases. Long-term systemic steroids are avoided because of cumulative toxicity.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Antihistamines",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Dermographism is best described as:",
   "options": [
    "Pigmentation after scratching",
    "Scarring",
    "A weal and flare produced by stroking the skin, a form of physical urticaria",
    "Blistering"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Simple dermographism occurs in about 5% of normal people; symptomatic dermographism causes itch and responds to antihistamines. It is the commonest physical urticaria, alongside cholinergic, cold and pressure types.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Urticaria",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ECZ_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Eczema, atopic & contact dermatitis, urticaria",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Lichen simplex chronicus results from:",
   "options": [
    "A primary infection",
    "An autoimmune process",
    "A drug reaction",
    "Chronic rubbing and scratching producing lichenified thickened plaques"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The itch-scratch cycle thickens skin with accentuated skin markings, typically on the nape, shins and ankles. Breaking the cycle with potent topical steroids under occlusion and addressing the underlying itch is essential.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Eczema",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
