   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Acne, rosacea, hair & nail disorders",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The primary lesion of acne vulgaris is:",
   "options": [
    "The pustule",
    "The scar",
    "The nodule",
    "The comedone"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Follicular hyperkeratinisation forming the microcomedone is the initiating lesion, followed by sebum overproduction under androgen drive, Cutibacterium acnes proliferation and inflammation.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Acne",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ACN_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Acne, rosacea, hair & nail disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Topical retinoids are central to acne treatment because they:",
   "options": [
    "Reduce sebum only",
    "Normalise follicular keratinisation and are comedolytic",
    "Are anti-inflammatory only",
    "Kill bacteria"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Retinoids target the microcomedone, the precursor of all acne lesions, so they treat and prevent. They cause irritation and photosensitivity, are applied at night, and are contraindicated in pregnancy.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Drugs for Acne",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ACN_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Acne, rosacea, hair & nail disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Oral isotretinoin is indicated for:",
   "options": [
    "Comedonal acne only",
    "Severe nodulocystic or scarring acne unresponsive to conventional therapy",
    "First-line treatment in everyone",
    "All mild acne"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Isotretinoin addresses all four pathogenic factors and can give lasting remission, but is highly teratogenic, requiring strict contraception and pregnancy testing, with monitoring of lipids and transaminases.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Drugs for Acne",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_ACN_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Acne, rosacea, hair & nail disorders",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Rosacea is distinguished from acne by:",
   "options": [
    "Absence of comedones, with centrofacial erythema, telangiectasia and flushing",
    "Presence of comedones",
    "Occurrence in adolescents",
    "Scarring"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Rosacea affects middle-aged adults with flushing triggered by heat, alcohol and spicy food, progressing to papulopustular and phymatous stages. Ocular rosacea with blepharitis is common and often overlooked.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Rosacea",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
