   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The mainstay of medical treatment for limited stable vitiligo is:",
   "options": [
    "Antihistamines",
    "Antibiotics",
    "Antifungals",
    "Topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors with phototherapy"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Repigmentation arises from follicular melanocyte reservoirs, so hairy areas respond better than acral skin and lips. Narrowband UVB is used for extensive disease and surgical grafting for stable resistant patches.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Pigmentary Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Albinism results from:",
   "options": [
    "Absence of melanocytes",
    "A defect in melanin synthesis with a normal number of melanocytes",
    "Autoimmune destruction",
    "Excess melanin"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Oculocutaneous albinism, often from tyrosinase deficiency, causes white skin and hair with photophobia, nystagmus and reduced acuity. Rigorous sun protection is essential given the high risk of skin cancer.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Pigmentary Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Ichthyosis vulgaris is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Scaling of flexures",
    "Fine fish-like scaling with hyperlinear palms, sparing the flexures",
    "Pustules",
    "Blistering"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Filaggrin mutations impair barrier formation, causing dry scaly skin with prominent palmar markings and strong association with atopic dermatitis. Flexural sparing distinguishes it from other ichthyoses.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Genodermatoses",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Cafe-au-lait macules numbering six or more, over 15 mm in an adult, suggest:",
   "options": [
    "Melasma",
    "Vitiligo",
    "Tuberous sclerosis",
    "Neurofibromatosis type 1"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "NF1 diagnostic criteria also include axillary or inguinal freckling, two or more neurofibromas, optic glioma, Lisch nodules, a distinctive bony lesion and an affected first-degree relative.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Genodermatoses",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Ash-leaf macules and adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas) with seizures indicate:",
   "options": [
    "Sturge-Weber syndrome",
    "Neurofibromatosis",
    "Incontinentia pigmenti",
    "Tuberous sclerosis"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Tuberous sclerosis shows hypomelanotic ash-leaf macules best seen under Wood lamp, shagreen patch, periungual fibromas, cortical tubers with epilepsy, and renal angiomyolipomas and cardiac rhabdomyomas.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Genodermatoses",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Epidermolysis bullosa is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Blistering at sites of minor trauma from inherited defects in structural adhesion proteins",
    "Autoimmune blistering",
    "Infective blistering",
    "Drug-induced blistering"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Simplex, junctional and dystrophic forms are defined by the level of split - intraepidermal, lamina lucida and sublamina densa respectively. Management is supportive with meticulous wound care and avoidance of friction.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Genodermatoses",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_PIG_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Pigmentary disorders (vitiligo, melasma) & genodermatoses",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation is particularly troublesome in Indian skin because:",
   "options": [
    "There is less melanin",
    "Higher constitutive melanin means inflammation readily provokes lasting pigment change",
    "It never occurs",
    "It resolves in days"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Fitzpatrick types IV to VI pigment readily after acne, eczema or any dermatitis, so treating the underlying inflammation early and rigorous photoprotection matter more than depigmenting agents alone.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Pigmentary Disorders",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are distinguished by:",
   "options": [
    "The percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment - under 10 percent versus over 30 percent",
    "The causative drug",
    "Presence of fever",
    "Mucosal involvement"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "SJS involves under 10%, overlap 10 to 30% and TEN over 30% detachment. Both are severe cutaneous adverse reactions with mucosal involvement, and mortality is predicted by the SCORTEN score.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Drug Reactions",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The drugs most commonly implicated in Stevens-Johnson syndrome and TEN include:",
   "options": [
    "Sulphonamides, anticonvulsants, allopurinol and NSAIDs",
    "Penicillins only",
    "Antifungals",
    "Antihypertensives"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Cotrimoxazole, carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine, nevirapine, allopurinol and oxicam NSAIDs are the classic culprits. HLA-B*1502 predicts carbamazepine-induced SJS in South Asian populations.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Drug Reactions",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The single most important step in managing toxic epidermal necrolysis is:",
   "options": [
    "Antibiotics for all",
    "Systemic steroids",
    "Immediate withdrawal of the offending drug with supportive burns-unit care",
    "Topical steroids"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Early withdrawal improves survival. Management mirrors burns care - fluid balance, temperature control, nutrition, wound care, ophthalmology input and infection surveillance. The role of steroids and IVIG remains debated.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Drug Reactions",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A fixed drug eruption is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Blistering only",
    "Changing sites",
    "A well-defined dusky plaque recurring at exactly the same site on re-exposure",
    "Widespread rash"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Fixed drug eruption recurs at identical sites, often lips, genitalia or hands, leaving post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation. Cotrimoxazole, NSAIDs, tetracyclines and paracetamol are common causes.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Drug Reactions",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "DRESS syndrome is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Immediate onset",
    "Rash with fever, eosinophilia, lymphadenopathy and internal organ involvement, appearing 2 to 6 weeks after the drug",
    "Absence of eosinophilia",
    "No systemic features"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The long latency distinguishes DRESS from other drug eruptions, and hepatitis, nephritis and myocarditis may persist after drug withdrawal. Anticonvulsants, allopurinol and dapsone are typical causes.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Drug Reactions",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The ABCDE criteria for suspecting melanoma stand for:",
   "options": [
    "Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Colour variation, Diameter over 6 mm and Evolution",
    "Age, Bleeding, Colour, Diameter, Elevation",
    "Area, Bleeding, Crust, Depth, Erythema",
    "Asymmetry, Bleeding, Crust, Diameter, Erythema"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Any changing pigmented lesion warrants excision biopsy with a narrow margin for histology, followed by wide local excision guided by Breslow thickness. Incisional or shave biopsy is avoided.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Skin Tumours",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Basal cell carcinoma most characteristically presents as:",
   "options": [
    "A pearly nodule with rolled edges and telangiectasia on sun-exposed skin",
    "An ulcer with everted edges",
    "A pigmented macule",
    "A scaly plaque on the trunk"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "BCC arises on the face above a line from the angle of the mouth to the ear lobe, is locally invasive as a rodent ulcer but essentially never metastasises. Squamous carcinoma has everted edges and does metastasise.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Skin Tumours",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Acral lentiginous melanoma is important in Indian patients because it:",
   "options": [
    "Occurs on palms, soles and nail beds where it is easily missed and presents late",
    "Is the commonest type worldwide",
    "Never metastasises",
    "Is always pigmented"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "It is the commonest melanoma subtype in darker-skinned and Asian populations. Longitudinal melanonychia with Hutchinson sign of periungual pigment spread is an important warning sign.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Skin Tumours",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The quantity of topical steroid needed is estimated using:",
   "options": [
    "The fingertip unit, approximately 0.5 g covering two adult palm areas",
    "Tablespoons",
    "Body weight",
    "Grams per kilogram"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "One fingertip unit is the amount from the distal crease to the fingertip of an adult index finger. This standardises prescribing, prevents both underuse from steroid phobia and overuse causing atrophy.",
   "reference": "IADVL Textbook of Dermatology 5e, Ch. Topical Therapy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_DE_DRG_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Dermatology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Drug reactions (SJS/TEN), skin tumours & melanoma; topical therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Topical calcineurin inhibitors such as tacrolimus are valuable because they:",
   "options": [
    "Provide steroid-sparing anti-inflammatory action without causing skin atrophy",
    "Are antibacterial",
    "Cause no irritation",
    "Are more potent than steroids"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "They are particularly useful on the face, eyelids and flexures where prolonged steroid use causes atrophy and telangiectasia. Transient burning on application is the commonest side effect.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Immunosuppressants",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The most reliable method of establishing personal identity is:",
   "options": [
    "Clothing",
    "Height",
    "Tattoo marks",
    "Fingerprints (dactylography), as they are unique and unchanging"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Dermal ridge patterns form by the fourth month of fetal life, remain unchanged throughout life and are destroyed only by deep dermal damage. Loops are commonest, followed by whorls, arches and composites.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "DNA fingerprinting for identity is best performed on:",
   "options": [
    "Urine only",
    "Sweat only",
    "Any nucleated cell source such as blood, hair root, bone or tissue",
    "Mature red blood cells"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Mature erythrocytes lack nuclei, so DNA comes from leucocytes in blood. Mitochondrial DNA is maternally inherited and useful for degraded remains and hair shafts without roots.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Stature can be estimated from long bones using:",
   "options": [
    "Skull circumference",
    "Rib length",
    "Regression formulae such as those of Trotter and Gleser, applied to the femur or tibia",
    "Vertebral count"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Long bones of the lower limb correlate best with height. Formulae are population and sex specific, so applying Western formulae to Indian remains introduces error - hence Indian-specific tables are used.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Corpus delicti in forensic medicine refers to:",
   "options": [
    "The crime scene",
    "Only the dead body",
    "The murder weapon",
    "The essential facts constituting the crime, including identification of the body"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Establishing identity of the deceased is a fundamental part of the corpus delicti, without which a charge of homicide cannot proceed - which is why identification is a core forensic responsibility.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Superimposition technique for identification involves:",
   "options": [
    "Dental casting",
    "DNA analysis",
    "Fingerprint matching",
    "Photographic or video comparison of a skull with an antemortem photograph of the suspected individual"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Anatomical landmarks of the skull are superimposed on a life-size photograph. It is chiefly a method of exclusion rather than positive identification, and is used when other methods are unavailable.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_ID_010",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Identification: age, sex, dactylography, DNA, anthropometry",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Bertillon system of identification was based on:",
   "options": [
    "Fingerprints",
    "DNA",
    "Anthropometric body measurements, now superseded by fingerprinting",
    "Dental records"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Bertillonage used a series of body measurements but was abandoned because measurements change with age and can coincide between individuals, unlike the unique and permanent fingerprint.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Identification",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_JUR_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical jurisprudence: negligence, consumer protection, PC-PNDT, MTP & transplantation acts",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The four essential elements to establish medical negligence are:",
   "options": [
    "Duty of care, breach of duty, causation and damage",
    "Only a bad outcome",
    "Intent, motive, act and harm",
    "Consent, fee, treatment and outcome"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "All four elements must be proved; a poor outcome alone is not negligence. The standard applied is that of a reasonably competent practitioner of the same standing - the Bolam test.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Medical Jurisprudence",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_JUR_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical jurisprudence: negligence, consumer protection, PC-PNDT, MTP & transplantation acts",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Res ipsa loquitur, meaning the thing speaks for itself, applies when:",
   "options": [
    "There is any complication",
    "The injury could not have occurred without negligence, such as a swab left in the abdomen",
    "Consent was verbal",
    "The patient is dissatisfied"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The burden of proof shifts to the doctor in such cases - a retained instrument, wrong-site surgery or a burn from a diathermy pad, where negligence is self-evident and expert evidence is unnecessary.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Medical Jurisprudence",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_JUR_003",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Medical jurisprudence: negligence, consumer protection, PC-PNDT, MTP & transplantation acts",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Under the Consumer Protection Act, medical services are covered except when:",
   "options": [
    "Treatment is in a government hospital",
    "The patient is poor",
    "Surgery is involved",
    "The service is rendered entirely free of charge to all patients"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Following Indian Medical Association v V P Shantha, paid medical services fall within the Act. Services free to everyone are excluded, but if some patients pay, even those treated free are covered.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Medical Jurisprudence",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
