   "topic": "Thermal, electrical & lightning injuries; burns",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In a death from a house fire, the finding that proves the victim was alive during the fire is:",
   "options": [
    "Charring of skin",
    "Fractured bones",
    "Soot in the trachea and bronchi with raised carboxyhaemoglobin",
    "Contracted muscles"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Inhaled soot below the vocal cords and blood carboxyhaemoglobin above about 10% prove respiration during the fire. Their absence suggests death occurred before the fire started.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Thermal Deaths",
   "tags": [
