  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Testamentary capacity requires that the person:",
   "options": [
    "Is physically fit",
    "Understands the nature of the act, the extent of property and the claims of those excluded",
    "Is over 60",
    "Has no mental illness ever"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "A person with mental illness may still make a valid will during a lucid interval provided these three elements are satisfied at the time of execution, which is why contemporaneous medical documentation matters.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Forensic Psychiatry",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_003",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Lucid interval in forensic psychiatry refers to:",
   "options": [
    "A period of normal mental functioning between episodes of mental illness",
    "The interval in extradural haematoma",
    "A period of amnesia",
    "A period of intoxication"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The same phrase means something different in head injury, where it describes the period of consciousness between concussion and deterioration in extradural haemorrhage - a classic source of confusion.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Forensic Psychiatry",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The universal antidote in poisoning has been replaced in modern practice by:",
   "options": [
    "Potassium permanganate",
    "Activated charcoal",
    "Egg white",
    "Milk"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Activated charcoal 1 g/kg given within one hour adsorbs most poisons, but not iron, lithium, alcohols, corrosives or hydrocarbons. Gastric lavage is now rarely indicated and is contraindicated with corrosives.",
   "reference": "KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology 8e, Ch. Poisoning",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_005",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The viscera to be preserved in a suspected poisoning autopsy include:",
   "options": [
    "Brain only",
    "Stomach with contents, upper small intestine, liver, kidney and blood",
    "Lungs only",
    "Skin only"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Saturated sodium chloride is the routine preservative, except for suspected alcohol where sodium fluoride is used. Rectified spirit is never used when alcohol or organophosphate poisoning is suspected.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Autopsy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In a medico-legal autopsy, the incision of choice for opening the body is:",
   "options": [
    "No incision",
    "Any incision",
    "A small incision",
    "The I-shaped or Y-shaped incision, avoiding the neck where strangulation is suspected until bloodless dissection"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "In suspected neck compression, the head and brain are removed first to drain blood, then layer-by-layer bloodless neck dissection is performed to avoid artefactual haemorrhage that could mimic true injury.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Autopsy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A medico-legal autopsy should be performed:",
   "options": [
    "Any time",
    "At night preferably",
    "Only in daylight or equivalent illumination, as artificial light alters colour perception",
    "Only after 24 hours"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Colour changes such as the cherry-red of carbon monoxide, jaundice and bruise colour are misjudged under artificial light. The autopsy must not be delayed unnecessarily as putrefaction destroys evidence.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Autopsy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
