   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Negative autopsy refers to one in which:",
   "options": [
    "No cause of death is found despite gross, microscopic, toxicological and laboratory examination",
    "Only gross examination is negative",
    "The autopsy was not done",
    "The cause is obvious"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Negative autopsies constitute 2 to 5% of cases; possible mechanisms include vagal inhibition, cardiac arrhythmia, epilepsy and anaphylaxis, which leave no morphological trace.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Autopsy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_009",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Forensic Medicine (FMT)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Forensic psychiatry, general toxicology & autopsy technique",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Vagal inhibition causing sudden death is characteristically associated with:",
   "options": [
    "Massive haemorrhage",
    "Prolonged illness",
    "Severe head injury",
    "Minor trauma to sensitive areas such as the neck, larynx, epigastrium or genitalia"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Reflex cardiac arrest from parasympathetic overactivity produces a negative autopsy, so the diagnosis is one of exclusion based on circumstances. Alcohol and a full stomach are predisposing factors.",
   "reference": "Reddy's The Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology 34e, Ch. Autopsy",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_FM_PSY_010",
   "part": "A",
