  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The most important stimulus to respiration under normal conditions is:",
   "options": [
    "Blood pH alone",
    "Oxygen tension",
    "Lung stretch",
    "Arterial carbon dioxide tension acting on central chemoreceptors"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond to hydrogen ions generated from carbon dioxide crossing the blood-brain barrier. Hypoxic drive via peripheral chemoreceptors becomes significant only below about 60 mmHg.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Acetazolamide prevents acute mountain sickness by:",
   "options": [
    "Causing alkalosis",
    "Blocking hypoxic drive",
    "Causing bicarbonate diuresis and metabolic acidosis which stimulates ventilation",
    "Increasing oxygen carriage"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It accelerates the renal compensation that would otherwise take days. Definitive treatment of severe altitude illness including cerebral or pulmonary oedema is immediate descent.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#DoC"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Decompression sickness in divers results from:",
   "options": [
    "Oxygen toxicity",
    "Carbon dioxide retention",
    "Nitrogen bubbles forming as dissolved gas comes out of solution on rapid ascent",
    "Hypothermia"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Bubbles cause joint pain (bends), respiratory distress (chokes) and neurological deficits, with chronic dysbaric osteonecrosis of femoral and humeral heads. Recompression in a hyperbaric chamber is the treatment.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Nitrogen narcosis in deep diving occurs because nitrogen at high partial pressure:",
   "options": [
    "Causes hypoxia",
    "Forms bubbles",
    "Causes hypercapnia",
    "Dissolves in neural lipid membranes producing an anaesthetic effect"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Rapture of the deep begins around 30 metres, impairing judgement. Helium-oxygen mixtures avoid it because helium is far less lipid-soluble, and are used for deep commercial diving.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Cheyne-Stokes respiration is characterised by:",
   "options": [
    "Sustained apnoea",
    "Cyclical crescendo-decrescendo breathing alternating with apnoea",
    "Regular deep breathing",
    "Irregular chaotic breathing"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Prolonged circulation time and increased chemoreceptor gain cause instability, seen in heart failure, at altitude and in bilateral cerebral lesions. Biot breathing is irregular and indicates medullary damage.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_RSP_010",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Respiratory regulation, high altitude & diving",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Haldane effect describes:",
   "options": [
    "Bicarbonate formation only",
    "Increased carbon dioxide carriage by deoxygenated haemoglobin",
    "Chloride shift",
    "Oxygen carriage by haemoglobin"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Deoxygenated haemoglobin binds hydrogen ions and forms carbamino compounds more readily, so venous blood carries more carbon dioxide - the complement to the Bohr effect at the tissues.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Respiration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_REN_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Renal tubular transport, acid-base regulation & micturition",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Aldosterone acts principally on the:",
   "options": [
    "Proximal tubule",
    "Loop of Henle",
    "Late distal tubule and collecting duct, increasing sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion",
    "Glomerulus"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It also promotes hydrogen ion secretion, so hyperaldosteronism causes hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with hypertension, whereas hypoaldosteronism causes hyperkalaemic acidosis as in type 4 renal tubular acidosis.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Renal Physiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_REN_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Renal tubular transport, acid-base regulation & micturition",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap is characteristically caused by:",
   "options": [
    "Diarrhoea and renal tubular acidosis",
    "Diabetic ketoacidosis",
    "Lactic acidosis",
    "Methanol poisoning"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Bicarbonate loss is replaced by chloride, keeping the gap normal - hyperchloraemic acidosis. A raised gap indicates added acid, remembered as MUDPILES. Urinary anion gap distinguishes renal from gut losses.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Renal Physiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_REN_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Renal tubular transport, acid-base regulation & micturition",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In respiratory acidosis, renal compensation involves:",
   "options": [
    "Increased carbon dioxide excretion",
    "Increased bicarbonate reabsorption and acid excretion, taking days to be complete",
    "Reduced bicarbonate",
    "Immediate bicarbonate rise"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Acute compensation raises bicarbonate about 1 mmol/L per 10 mmHg rise in PCO2, and chronic compensation about 4 mmol/L, which is why chronic retainers tolerate high PCO2 with near-normal pH.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Renal Physiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_REN_010",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Renal tubular transport, acid-base regulation & micturition",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The renin-angiotensin system is activated by:",
   "options": [
    "Hyperkalaemia alone",
    "Hypertension",
    "Reduced renal perfusion pressure, reduced distal sodium delivery and sympathetic stimulation",
    "Hypervolaemia"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole preserving GFR when perfusion falls, which is why ACE inhibitors precipitate acute kidney injury in bilateral renal artery stenosis or severe hypovolaemia.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Renal Physiology",
   "tags": [
