   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Coronary blood flow to the left ventricle occurs mainly during:",
   "options": [
    "Isovolumetric contraction",
    "Both equally",
    "Systole",
    "Diastole"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Systolic myocardial compression obstructs left coronary flow, so tachycardia shortening diastole and aortic stenosis reducing perfusion pressure both precipitate ischaemia even with normal coronaries.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_CO_005",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The most potent physiological stimulus increasing cerebral blood flow is:",
   "options": [
    "Hypotension",
    "A fall in carbon dioxide",
    "Hypoxia alone",
    "A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Hypercapnia causes cerebral vasodilatation, which is why hyperventilation is used briefly to lower intracranial pressure. Prolonged hyperventilation risks ischaemia from excessive vasoconstriction.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_CO_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "During exercise, the increase in cardiac output is achieved mainly by:",
   "options": [
    "Increased heart rate, with stroke volume plateauing at moderate intensity",
    "Stroke volume alone",
    "Reduced venous return",
    "Increased peripheral resistance"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Total peripheral resistance falls as skeletal muscle vessels dilate, so mean pressure rises only modestly while systolic pressure and pulse pressure increase substantially.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_CO_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The ejection fraction is calculated as:",
   "options": [
    "End-systolic over end-diastolic volume",
    "Stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, normally 55 to 70 percent",
    "Stroke volume divided by cardiac output",
    "Cardiac output over heart rate"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction below 40% is distinguished from heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, which reflects diastolic dysfunction and requires different management.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_CO_009",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The baroreceptor reflex responds to a fall in blood pressure by:",
   "options": [
    "Increasing vagal tone",
    "Reducing heart rate",
    "Reducing vagal tone and increasing sympathetic outflow, raising rate and resistance",
    "Causing vasodilatation"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Carotid sinus and aortic arch stretch receptors signal via glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves. This buffer reflex resets in chronic hypertension, which is why it does not correct sustained hypertension.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_CO_010",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Cardiac output, regional circulations & exercise",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Central venous pressure principally reflects:",
   "options": [
    "Cardiac contractility",
    "Left ventricular function",
    "Right atrial pressure and therefore right ventricular preload and volume status",
    "Arterial resistance"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It is raised in right heart failure, tamponade, tension pneumothorax and fluid overload, and low in hypovolaemia. Trends in response to fluid challenge are more informative than single readings.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Circulation",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_GIT_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "GIT secretions, motility & absorption",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Gastric acid is secreted by:",
   "options": [
    "Chief cells",
    "Parietal (oxyntic) cells, which also secrete intrinsic factor",
    "G cells",
    "Mucous cells"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, G cells gastrin and D cells somatostatin. Loss of parietal cells in autoimmune atrophic gastritis causes both achlorhydria and pernicious anaemia from intrinsic factor deficiency.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Gastrointestinal Physiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PY_GIT_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Physiology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "GIT secretions, motility & absorption",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The three stimulants of gastric acid secretion acting on the parietal cell are:",
   "options": [
    "Acetylcholine, gastrin and histamine",
    "Insulin, glucagon and gastrin",
    "Somatostatin, GIP and motilin",
    "Secretin, CCK and VIP"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Histamine acting on H2 receptors is the final common amplifier, which is why H2 blockers are effective; proton pump inhibitors act further downstream on the H+/K+ ATPase and are more powerful.",
   "reference": "Ganong's Review of Medical Physiology 26e, Ch. Gastrointestinal Physiology",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
