   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Integration of metabolism: fed/fasting, diabetes, ketosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Cori cycle involves:",
   "options": [
    "Amino acid transamination",
    "Glucose to fat conversion",
    "Transport of muscle lactate to the liver for conversion back to glucose",
    "Ketone transport"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It shifts the metabolic burden of anaerobic glycolysis to the liver at a net ATP cost. The glucose-alanine cycle similarly carries nitrogen from muscle to liver for urea synthesis.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Metabolic Integration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_INT_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Integration of metabolism: fed/fasting, diabetes, ketosis",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "HbA1c reflects glycaemic control over approximately:",
   "options": [
    "One week",
    "One day",
    "The preceding 8 to 12 weeks",
    "One year"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Non-enzymatic glycation is proportional to average glucose over the red cell lifespan. It is falsely low in haemolysis and blood loss and falsely high in iron deficiency, limiting reliability in those settings.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Metabolic Integration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_INT_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Integration of metabolism: fed/fasting, diabetes, ketosis",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Metabolic syndrome centres on insulin resistance, which biochemically causes:",
   "options": [
    "Low triglycerides",
    "Raised triglycerides with low HDL and small dense LDL particles",
    "High HDL",
    "Reduced VLDL"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Failure to suppress lipolysis floods the liver with free fatty acids, driving VLDL overproduction. LDL cholesterol is not itself a diagnostic criterion of the syndrome.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Lipid Metabolism",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_INT_010",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Integration of metabolism: fed/fasting, diabetes, ketosis",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In the fed state, the principal fate of excess dietary glucose is:",
   "options": [
    "Immediate excretion",
    "Glycogen synthesis followed by conversion to fatty acids for storage as triglyceride",
    "Ketone formation",
    "Gluconeogenesis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Insulin activates glycogen synthase and lipogenesis while inhibiting the opposing pathways, ensuring reciprocal regulation so that synthesis and breakdown never run simultaneously as a futile cycle.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Metabolic Integration",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_001",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation relates blood pH to:",
   "options": [
    "The ratio of bicarbonate to carbonic acid (dissolved CO2)",
    "Sodium and potassium",
    "Oxygen and carbon dioxide",
    "Albumin and globulin"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "pH depends on the ratio, not absolute values, which is why compensation restores pH by adjusting the other component. Normal ratio is 20 to 1, giving pH 7.4.",
   "reference": "Vasudevan Textbook of Biochemistry for Medical Students 9e, Ch. Minerals and Acid-Base Balance",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#formula"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Acid-Base\", which is not a chapter in the book cited"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_002",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The anion gap is calculated as:",
   "options": [
    "Bicarbonate minus chloride",
    "Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, normally 8 to 12 mmol/L",
    "Sodium plus potassium",
    "Chloride minus bicarbonate"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "It represents unmeasured anions, principally albumin, so it must be corrected in hypoalbuminaemia by adding 2.5 for every 1 g/dL fall. A raised gap indicates added acid.",
   "reference": "Vasudevan Textbook of Biochemistry for Medical Students 9e, Ch. Minerals and Acid-Base Balance",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Acid-Base\", which is not a chapter in the book cited"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_004",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Iron is absorbed principally in the:",
   "options": [
    "Colon",
    "Ileum",
    "Duodenum, enhanced by vitamin C and the ferrous form",
    "Stomach"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Hepcidin from the liver is the master regulator, blocking ferroportin-mediated release. Its induction by inflammation explains the anaemia of chronic disease with low iron but raised ferritin.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Micronutrients: Vitamins and Minerals",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Minerals\", an incomplete chapter title"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_005",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Copper deficiency causes anaemia because copper is required for:",
   "options": [
    "Globin synthesis",
    "Ceruloplasmin (ferroxidase) activity, needed to mobilise iron",
    "Erythropoietin production",
    "Haem synthesis directly"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Copper deficiency causes a microcytic anaemia unresponsive to iron, with neutropenia and neurological features. Menkes kinky hair disease results from defective copper absorption and transport.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Micronutrients: Vitamins and Minerals",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Minerals\", an incomplete chapter title"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_006",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Iodine deficiency in the fetus causes:",
   "options": [
    "Scurvy",
    "Anaemia",
    "Rickets",
    "Cretinism with intellectual disability and deaf-mutism"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Universal salt iodisation is the principal preventive strategy, and iodine deficiency remains the commonest preventable cause of intellectual disability worldwide. Goitre reflects TSH-driven thyroid hyperplasia.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Micronutrients: Vitamins and Minerals",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
