   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Minerals\", an incomplete chapter title"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_007",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Fluoride in drinking water at optimal levels prevents dental caries, but excess causes:",
   "options": [
    "Goitre",
    "Anaemia",
    "Dental and skeletal fluorosis",
    "Rickets"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The optimal level is about 0.5 to 0.8 mg/L in India. Mottled enamel appears above 1.5 mg/L and crippling skeletal fluorosis with genu valgum above 3 mg/L, a serious problem in several Indian states.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Water",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_008",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Magnesium deficiency should be suspected in refractory:",
   "options": [
    "Hypernatraemia",
    "Hyperkalaemia",
    "Hypokalaemia and hypocalcaemia that do not correct until magnesium is replaced",
    "Acidosis"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Magnesium is required for PTH secretion and action and for renal potassium conservation, so correcting it is a prerequisite for correcting the other electrolytes - a common clinical trap.",
   "reference": "Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry 32e, Ch. Micronutrients: Vitamins and Minerals",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "the chapter was cited as \"Minerals\", an incomplete chapter title"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_BC_MIN_009",
   "part": "A",
   "subject": "Biochemistry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Minerals, trace elements & acid-base biochemistry",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In metabolic acidosis, respiratory compensation is predicted by the Winter formula:",
   "options": [
    "Bicarbonate minus 8",
    "Expected PCO2 equals 1.5 times bicarbonate plus 8, plus or minus 2",
    "Bicarbonate plus 15",
    "Twice bicarbonate"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "If measured PCO2 exceeds the predicted value there is an additional respiratory acidosis, and if lower an additional respiratory alkalosis - allowing detection of mixed disorders.",
   "reference": "Vasudevan Textbook of Biochemistry for Medical Students 9e, Ch. Minerals and Acid-Base Balance",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
