   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Association & causation, bias, confounding, measures of risk",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Confounding is best controlled at the analysis stage by:",
   "options": [
    "Stratification and multivariable regression",
    "Increasing sample size",
    "Blinding",
    "Using a bigger population"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "At the design stage randomisation, restriction and matching are used. Increasing sample size reduces random error but does nothing about confounding, which is a systematic error.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Epidemiology",
   "tags": [
