  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_CHI_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child health programmes, IMNCI & school health",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The most important component of school health services is:",
   "options": [
    "Periodic medical examination with follow-up of detected conditions",
    "Health education alone",
    "Immunisation only",
    "Sanitation only"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Screening without treatment and follow-up achieves nothing, which is the commonest failure of school health programmes. Nutrition through the mid-day meal and a healthful school environment are equally important.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. School Health",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_CHI_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child health programmes, IMNCI & school health",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Anganwadi centres under the ICDS scheme provide all EXCEPT:",
   "options": [
    "Inpatient treatment of severe illness",
    "Supplementary nutrition",
    "Immunisation and health check-up",
    "Non-formal preschool education"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "ICDS delivers six services - supplementary nutrition, immunisation, health check-up, referral, nutrition and health education, and preschool education - to children under six, pregnant and lactating women.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. ICDS",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Kwashiorkor is distinguished from marasmus by:",
   "options": [
    "Normal hair",
    "Absence of oedema",
    "Presence of oedema with relatively preserved subcutaneous fat",
    "Severe wasting only"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Kwashiorkor shows oedema, flaky paint dermatosis, hair changes and fatty liver with a miserable apathetic child, whereas marasmus shows extreme wasting with an alert, ravenously hungry child.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The staple food fortification programme in India includes fortifying salt with:",
   "options": [
    "Zinc",
    "Vitamin A",
    "Calcium",
    "Iodine, and increasingly with both iodine and iron"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Universal salt iodisation is one of the most successful public health interventions in India. Oil is fortified with vitamins A and D, wheat flour and rice with iron, folic acid and B12, and milk with vitamins A and D.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Lathyrism results from prolonged consumption of:",
   "options": [
    "Mustard oil",
    "Lathyrus sativus (khesari dal) containing BOAA neurotoxin",
    "Contaminated maize",
    "Cassava"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "It causes spastic paraparesis in young adults. Epidemic dropsy comes from argemone-contaminated mustard oil and aflatoxicosis from Aspergillus-contaminated groundnuts and maize.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Pasteurisation of milk by the holder method involves heating to:",
   "options": [
    "121 degrees for 15 minutes",
    "100 degrees for 10 minutes",
    "72 degrees for 1 minute",
    "63 degrees Celsius for 30 minutes followed by rapid cooling"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The flash or HTST method uses 72 degrees for 15 seconds. The phosphatase test confirms adequate pasteurisation, as the enzyme is destroyed under the same conditions as pathogens.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Milk Hygiene",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Food poisoning with an incubation period under six hours and prominent vomiting suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Campylobacter",
    "Salmonella",
    "Preformed toxin of Staphylococcus aureus or Bacillus cereus",
    "Clostridium perfringens"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Short incubation indicates preformed toxin, whereas 12 to 48 hours indicates infection requiring bacterial multiplication, as with Salmonella. Botulism has a longer incubation with neurological features.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Food Poisoning",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_NUT_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Nutrition programmes, food fortification & food hygiene",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The first 1000 days of life, critical for nutrition intervention, spans:",
   "options": [
    "Birth to three years",
    "Conception to two years of age",
    "Conception to birth",
    "Birth to one year"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "This window covers rapid brain and physical growth when deficits cause irreversible cognitive and stature deficits, which is why maternal nutrition, exclusive breastfeeding and timely complementary feeding are prioritised.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Nutrition",
   "tags": [
