   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Non-communicable disease epidemiology: CVD, diabetes, cancer, tobacco",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Rose's population strategy of prevention argues that:",
   "options": [
    "Only treatment matters",
    "High-risk targeting is always better",
    "Prevention is ineffective",
    "A small risk reduction across a whole population prevents more disease than targeting high-risk individuals"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Most cases arise from the large number of people at modest risk rather than the few at high risk - the prevention paradox, which explains why population salt reduction outperforms treating hypertensives alone.",
