  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_PLN_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health planning, committees & health policy",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The Mukherjee Committee recommended:",
   "options": [
    "Merging all programmes",
    "Separation of malaria functions from family planning to allow each to be adequately staffed",
    "Abolishing family planning",
    "Privatisation"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "This illustrates the recurring tension between vertical programmes achieving focus and horizontal integration achieving efficiency - a debate that continues in programme design.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Planning",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_PLN_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health planning, committees & health policy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Health being a state subject in India means:",
   "options": [
    "The centre delivers all services",
    "States have primary responsibility for delivering health services, with the centre setting policy and providing funds",
    "States have no role",
    "Only local bodies act"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "This constitutional division explains wide inter-state variation in health outcomes and the use of centrally sponsored schemes with conditional funding to drive national priorities.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Planning",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_001",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana provides health cover of:",
   "options": [
    "Five lakh rupees per family per year for secondary and tertiary hospitalisation",
    "One lakh rupees",
    "Ten lakh rupees",
    "Fifty thousand rupees"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "It targets the poorest 40% of the population identified by deprivation criteria, is cashless and paperless at empanelled hospitals, and is portable across states.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Insurance",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The two components of Ayushman Bharat are:",
   "options": [
    "Health and Wellness Centres for comprehensive primary care and PM-JAY for hospitalisation",
    "Only insurance",
    "Only primary care",
    "Medical education and insurance"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The two pillars address different levels: HWCs deliver 12 packages of comprehensive primary care including NCD screening, while PM-JAY protects against catastrophic hospitalisation expenditure.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. National Health Programmes",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_003",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Catastrophic health expenditure is conventionally defined as out-of-pocket spending exceeding:",
   "options": [
    "One percent",
    "Ten percent of total household consumption expenditure",
    "Fifty percent",
    "Ninety percent"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "India has among the highest out-of-pocket shares globally, pushing millions below the poverty line annually - the impoverishment effect that health financing reform seeks to prevent.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_004",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Cost-effectiveness analysis compares interventions using:",
   "options": [
    "Cost only",
    "Cost per unit of health outcome, such as cost per life year gained",
    "Outcome only",
    "Revenue generated"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Cost-utility analysis uses QALYs or DALYs allowing comparison across diseases, while cost-benefit analysis values outcomes in money terms, which is ethically contentious for health.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The Employees State Insurance Scheme is financed by:",
   "options": [
    "Contributions from employers and employees, making it a social insurance model",
    "General taxation",
    "Voluntary premiums",
    "Donor funding"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Social insurance is compulsory and contributory with risk pooling, distinguishing it from tax-funded national health services and from voluntary private insurance based on individual risk rating.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Social Security",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Moral hazard in health insurance refers to:",
   "options": [
    "Denial of claims",
    "Fraud by providers",
    "Increased use of services because the insured does not bear the full cost",
    "Adverse selection"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Co-payments and gatekeeping mitigate it. Adverse selection is the opposite problem, where those expecting to need care are most likely to insure, which universal enrolment prevents.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The largest share of health expenditure in India has historically been:",
   "options": [
    "Insurance",
    "Central government",
    "State government",
    "Out-of-pocket private expenditure by households"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Although the out-of-pocket share has fallen with expanded insurance and free service delivery, it remains high, which is why financial protection is a central objective of health policy.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A DALY averted is used in health economics to measure:",
   "options": [
    "The health gain from an intervention, combining premature mortality and disability",
    "Cost only",
    "Number of patients treated",
    "Hospital beds"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Interventions are ranked by cost per DALY averted, allowing comparison between, say, immunisation and dialysis. Immunisation and tobacco control are consistently among the most cost-effective.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield",
    "#PYQ"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Strategic purchasing in health financing means:",
   "options": [
    "Actively selecting what services to buy, from whom and how to pay, rather than passive budgeting",
    "Buying the cheapest drugs",
    "Purchasing equipment",
    "Building hospitals"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Provider payment mechanisms - capitation, case-based bundled payment and performance-linked payment - shape provider behaviour, which is why payment design matters as much as the level of funding.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Economics",
   "tags": [
    "#high-yield"
   ],
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ECO_010",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Health economics, insurance, Ayushman Bharat & financing",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana, the predecessor of PM-JAY, differed principally in:",
   "options": [
    "Providing higher cover",
    "Providing a much lower annual cover of thirty thousand rupees per family",
    "Covering only outpatients",
    "Being voluntary"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The low cover proved inadequate for serious illness, and evaluations showed limited reduction in out-of-pocket spending - the evidence that drove the far larger PM-JAY entitlement.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Health Insurance",
   "tags": [
