   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "COPD & asthma",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Non-invasive ventilation in an acute COPD exacerbation is indicated when arterial blood gas shows:",
   "options": [
    "Normal pH with high PaCO2",
    "Isolated hypoxaemia",
    "pH below 7.35 with rising PaCO2 despite medical therapy",
    "Metabolic acidosis"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It is acute respiratory acidosis, not the absolute carbon dioxide level, that identifies decompensation, and NIV in that setting reduces intubation rates and mortality.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 286 (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_CA_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "COPD & asthma",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The stepwise addition of an inhaled corticosteroid to a long-acting beta agonist in COPD is most justified when:",
   "options": [
    "FEV1 is below 50 percent alone",
    "Blood eosinophils are raised with frequent exacerbations",
    "The patient is a current smoker",
    "There is chronic sputum production"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Eosinophilic inflammation predicts corticosteroid responsiveness, whereas in the eosinophil-low patient the pneumonia risk of inhaled steroids outweighs the benefit.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 286 (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_CA_006",
