   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "topic": "Cardiomyopathies & adult congenital heart disease",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 30-year-old man is found to have a harsh grade 4 pansystolic murmur with a thrill at the lower left sternal border. He is acyanotic with normal exercise tolerance and a normal chest radiograph. The most likely lesion is:",
   "options": [
    "A large ventricular septal defect with pulmonary hypertension",
    "An ostium secundum atrial septal defect",
    "A small (restrictive) ventricular septal defect",
    "Severe mitral regurgitation"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "A small restrictive ventricular septal defect generates a large pressure gradient across a tiny orifice, so the murmur is loud and harsh with a thrill, yet the shunt volume is trivial and the patient stays asymptomatic with a normal heart size. Loudness therefore correlates inversely with defect size in ventricular septal defect. A large defect equalises pressures and gives a softer murmur with cardiomegaly and pulmonary hypertension.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 258 (Cardiomyopathy and Myocarditis)",
   "verify_note": "rewritten because the original stem duplicated Q_MED_CMP_006 on fixed splitting of the second sound (Jaccard 0.75); replaced with the inverse murmur-size relationship in ventricular septal defect (plan 6.2)"
  },
