    "It occurs only in type 1 diabetes",
    "Insulin is completely absent",
    "Glucagon is suppressed",
    "Residual insulin suppresses ketogenesis despite severe hyperglycaemia"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Enough insulin remains to block lipolysis but not gluconeogenesis, so glucose climbs above 600 mg/dL with profound dehydration and osmolality above 320 but minimal acidosis.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 403 (Diabetes Mellitus)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_011",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The World Health Organization analgesic ladder recommends that a patient with severe cancer pain be started on:",
   "options": [
    "Paracetamol alone",
    "A strong opioid such as morphine",
    "A weak opioid only",
    "An antidepressant"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Severity, not sequence, determines the step chosen, so severe pain begins at step three with a strong opioid plus adjuvants rather than climbing from the bottom.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_012",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "The commonest adverse effect of chronic opioid therapy that does not develop tolerance is:",
   "options": [
    "Respiratory depression",
    "Nausea",
    "Sedation",
    "Constipation"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Tolerance develops to most opioid effects but not to reduced gut motility, so a stimulant laxative is prescribed prophylactically with every strong opioid.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_013",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A validated first-line screening tool for cognitive impairment in an elderly outpatient is:",
   "options": [
    "Mini-Mental State Examination or Montreal Cognitive Assessment",
    "Glasgow Coma Scale",
    "Barthel index",
    "Geriatric depression scale"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "These test multiple cognitive domains, whereas the Barthel index measures function and the depression scale screens mood, both of which are complementary rather than diagnostic of cognitive loss.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_014",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "An 82-year-old man on eight regular medicines has had two falls this year. Which single prescribing change is most likely to reduce his falls risk?",
   "options": [
    "Adding a calcium and vitamin D supplement",
    "Withdrawing psychotropic drugs such as benzodiazepines and antipsychotics",
    "Switching his statin to a different agent",
    "Adding a proton pump inhibitor for gastric protection"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Psychotropics, especially benzodiazepines, Z-drugs, antipsychotics and sedating antidepressants, carry the strongest and most reversible association with falls in older people, and deprescribing them reduces fall rates in trials. Antihypertensives causing postural hypotension are the other high-yield target. Calcium and vitamin D affect fracture risk rather than fall frequency, and statins and proton pump inhibitors are not fall-related.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verify_note": "rewritten because the original stem duplicated Q_MED_GER_010 on polypharmacy tools (Jaccard 0.75); replaced with a deprescribing question that tests a different decision (plan 6.2)"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "The commonest cause of acute confusion in a hospitalised elderly patient is:",
   "options": [
    "Delirium from an acute medical precipitant",
    "Alzheimer disease",
    "Depression",
    "Schizophrenia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Delirium has an acute onset with fluctuating attention and is usually multifactorial from infection, drugs, pain or retention, so the response is to find and treat the precipitant.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_016",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Falls risk in an older person is most reduced by:",
   "options": [
    "Bed rest",
    "Multifactorial assessment with strength and balance training",
    "Routine hip protectors alone",
    "Sedative prescription"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Exercise programmes plus medication review, vision correction and home hazard modification have the strongest trial evidence, whereas immobility accelerates deconditioning.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_017",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In the last days of life, excessive respiratory secretions are best managed with:",
   "options": [
    "Broad-spectrum antibiotics",
    "Deep suctioning",
    "Intravenous furosemide",
    "Antimuscarinic agents such as glycopyrrolate with repositioning"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "The sound distresses relatives more than the patient; drying secretions and turning the patient are effective, while suction is traumatic and rarely helps.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_018",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Which principle allows giving an opioid dose that relieves pain even if it may hasten death:",
   "options": [
    "Euthanasia",
    "The doctrine of double effect",
    "Physician-assisted suicide",
    "Therapeutic privilege"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The intended effect is relief of suffering and the harmful effect is foreseen but unintended and proportionate, which is ethically and legally distinct from acting in order to cause death.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GER_019",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "topic": "Geriatrics, palliative care & end-of-life issues",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "The family of an 84-year-old woman with advanced dementia and recurrent aspiration pneumonia ask whether a percutaneous gastrostomy tube would help. The best evidence-based advice is that tube feeding:",
   "options": [
    "Reliably prevents further aspiration pneumonia",
    "Has been shown to prolong survival in advanced dementia",
    "Is mandatory once oral intake falls below half of requirements",
    "Does not prevent aspiration or prolong survival, so careful hand feeding with comfort measures is preferred"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Randomised and cohort evidence shows that gastrostomy feeding in advanced dementia does not reduce aspiration pneumonia, pressure ulcers or mortality, and it removes the pleasure of eating while adding restraint and tube complications. Careful hand feeding with modified textures and attention to oral care and positioning is the recommended approach, framed within a goals-of-care discussion.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 471 (Palliative and End-of-Life Care)",
   "verify_note": "rewritten because the original stem was a near-verbatim concept duplicate of Q_MED_GER_004 on frailty (Jaccard 1.0); replaced with an end-of-life feeding decision, which the topic did not yet cover (plan 5.3 and 6.2)"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GN_011",
