   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Glomerular disease & nephrotic syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Frank haematuria occurring within 1 to 2 days of an upper respiratory infection suggests:",
   "options": [
    "Goodpasture syndrome",
    "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis",
    "IgA nephropathy",
    "Thin basement membrane disease"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Synpharyngitic haematuria distinguishes IgA nephropathy from the 1 to 3 week latency of post-streptococcal disease, and mesangial IgA deposits confirm it.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 314 (Glomerular Diseases)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "reviewed against a concept-similarity duplicate candidate; answer, topic and reference confirmed"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_GN_016",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Glomerular disease & nephrotic syndrome",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis is classified into three types by immunofluorescence. What are they?",
   "options": [
    "Linear anti-GBM deposition, granular immune-complex deposition, and pauci-immune ANCA-associated disease",
    "Mesangial IgA, subepithelial spikes, and foot-process effacement",
    "Nodular sclerosis, membranous thickening, and minimal change",
    "All three types show linear staining"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Crescents on light microscopy establish the syndrome but not the cause, so it is the immunofluorescence pattern that separates Goodpasture disease from post-infectious or lupus nephritis and from the vasculitides, and it determines whether plasma exchange is added.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Glomerular Diseases",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "verify_note": "duplicate of Q_MED_GN_008 on anti-PLA2R antibodies in membranous nephropathy. Rewritten to the classification of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"
  },
