   "concept": "The Weil-Felix test detects rickettsial antibodies that cross-react with the OX-19, OX-2 and OX-K somatic antigens of Proteus species, with the OX-K pattern classically associated with scrub typhus. It is cheap but insensitive and non-specific, so an IgM ELISA or PCR is now preferred. The Widal test is for enteric fever, the microscopic agglutination test for leptospirosis, and the Paul-Bunnell test for infectious mononucleosis.",
