   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "After six years of levodopa therapy a Parkinson patient reports that his mobility now deteriorates predictably in the last hour before each dose. The most appropriate first step is:",
   "options": [
    "Stop levodopa and start an anticholinergic",
    "Add a typical antipsychotic",
    "Double each individual levodopa dose",
    "Give the same total daily dose in smaller, more frequent doses or add a COMT or MAO-B inhibitor"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Predictable end-of-dose wearing off reflects the short half-life of levodopa acting on a progressively smaller pool of dopaminergic terminals. Smoothing plasma levels by fractionating the dose, or prolonging levodopa's action with a COMT inhibitor such as entacapone or an MAO-B inhibitor such as rasagiline, is the first response. Doubling individual doses instead provokes peak-dose dyskinesia, and antipsychotics worsen parkinsonism.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 437 (Parkinson's Disease)",
   "verify_note": "rewritten because the original stem duplicated Q_MED_MOV_002 on the most effective symptomatic drug (Jaccard 0.83); replaced with management of motor fluctuations (plan 6.2)"
  },
