    "Results from iodine-induced excess synthesis in a nodular gland",
    "Is a destructive thyroiditis with a low vascularity on Doppler, responding to corticosteroids",
    "Always requires thyroidectomy",
    "Occurs only in iodine-deficient areas"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Type 1 is iodine-driven hyperfunction treated with thionamides, whereas type 2 is drug-induced destruction releasing preformed hormone and responds to steroids; distinguishing them changes management entirely.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 375 (Disorders of the Thyroid Gland)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_TD_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Thyroid disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A solitary thyroid nodule with fine needle aspiration reported as Bethesda category IV, a follicular neoplasm, should be managed by:",
   "options": [
    "Repeat aspiration in six months",
    "Diagnostic hemithyroidectomy or molecular testing",
    "Radioiodine",
    "Suppressive thyroxine"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Cytology cannot distinguish follicular adenoma from carcinoma because that rests on capsular invasion, so lobectomy provides the histology; molecular marker panels can reduce unnecessary surgery.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 375 (Disorders of the Thyroid Gland)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_TD_008",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Thyroid disorders",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Which finding on a thyroid nodule biopsy would indicate medullary carcinoma and prompt screening for phaeochromocytoma?",
   "options": [
    "Papillae with nuclear grooves",
    "Sheets of cells with amyloid stroma and positive calcitonin staining",
    "Hurthle cells",
    "Lymphocytic infiltration"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Medullary carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells and may be part of MEN 2, so RET testing and exclusion of phaeochromocytoma are mandatory before any neck surgery to avoid an intraoperative hypertensive crisis.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 375 (Disorders of the Thyroid Gland)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_TD_009",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Thyroid disorders",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In a critically ill patient, a low free triiodothyronine with a normal or low TSH and a raised reverse triiodothyronine indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Non-thyroidal illness (sick euthyroid) syndrome",
    "Primary hypothyroidism",
    "Thyrotoxicosis",
    "Iodine deficiency"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Illness shifts deiodination from the active to the inactive pathway; treating with thyroxine has not improved outcomes, so thyroid function is best rechecked after recovery rather than acted on during the acute illness.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 375 (Disorders of the Thyroid Gland)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_RTA_011",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which acid-base and potassium pattern characterises type 2 (proximal) renal tubular acidosis?",
   "options": [
    "Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with hypokalaemia and a bicarbonate threshold that is reset lower",
    "High anion gap acidosis with hyperkalaemia",
    "Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalaemia",
    "Normal acid-base status"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Failure of proximal bicarbonate reclamation causes bicarbonate wasting until plasma bicarbonate falls to the new lower threshold, after which the urine can be acidified, unlike distal disease where it cannot.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 315 (Tubulointerstitial Diseases of the Kidney)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_RTA_012",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which drug most characteristically causes chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis with a bland urinary sediment and slowly rising creatinine?",
   "options": [
    "Lithium",
    "Penicillin",
    "Furosemide",
    "Metformin"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Lithium enters principal cells through the epithelial sodium channel and causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with chronic interstitial fibrosis and microcysts, so renal function and concentrating ability need long-term monitoring.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 315 (Tubulointerstitial Diseases of the Kidney)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_RTA_013",
