   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which vascular access provides the best long-term survival for maintenance haemodialysis?",
   "options": [
    "Tunnelled internal jugular catheter",
    "Native arteriovenous fistula",
    "Femoral temporary catheter",
    "Arteriovenous graft"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "A native fistula has the lowest rates of infection and thrombosis and the best patency, which is why forearm veins are preserved from venepuncture in patients approaching end-stage renal disease.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Dialysis in the Treatment of Renal Failure",
