   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A patient develops acute kidney injury with sterile pyuria, eosinophiluria and a rash a week after starting a proton pump inhibitor. The management is:",
   "options": [
    "Increase fluid intake alone",
    "Withdraw the offending drug, with corticosteroids if renal function does not recover",
    "Start antibiotics",
    "Begin urgent dialysis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Acute interstitial nephritis is a hypersensitivity reaction, so removing the trigger is essential; steroids are used when function fails to improve, and delayed diagnosis leads to interstitial fibrosis.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 315 (Tubulointerstitial Diseases of the Kidney)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_RTA_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Which feature suggests that hyperkalaemia is due to type 4 rather than type 1 renal tubular acidosis?",
   "options": [
    "A urine pH above 5.5",
    "Hyperkalaemia with a mild acidosis in a diabetic with hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism",
    "Nephrocalcinosis",
    "Recurrent calcium phosphate stones"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Type 4 reflects aldosterone deficiency or resistance, so potassium is high and the acidosis mild; type 1 causes hypokalaemia with an inappropriately alkaline urine, nephrocalcinosis and stones.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 315 (Tubulointerstitial Diseases of the Kidney)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_MED_RTA_016",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Medicine",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Which complication of long-term haemodialysis is caused by beta-2 microglobulin deposition?",
   "options": [
    "Dialysis-related amyloidosis with carpal tunnel syndrome and destructive arthropathy",
    "Uraemic pericarditis",
    "Aluminium bone disease",
    "Dialysis disequilibrium"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Beta-2 microglobulin is poorly cleared by conventional low-flux membranes, and its amyloid deposits in synovium, bone cysts and the carpal tunnel; high-flux membranes and transplantation reduce the risk.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. Dialysis in the Treatment of Renal Failure",
