   "topic": "Tubulointerstitial disease, RTA & renal replacement therapy",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Which condition causes a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with a positive urinary anion gap and a urine pH above 5.5 despite systemic acidaemia?",
   "options": [
    "Diarrhoeal bicarbonate loss",
    "Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis",
    "Proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis after bicarbonate depletion",
    "Ureteric diversion"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The failure of the alpha-intercalated cell hydrogen ATPase means ammonium excretion is impaired, so the urinary anion gap is positive and the urine cannot be acidified, in contrast to diarrhoea where ammonium excretion rises and the gap is negative.",
   "reference": "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine 21e, Ch. 315 (Tubulointerstitial Diseases of the Kidney)",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_ABL_011",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
