   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A multipara at term has a transverse lie with the fetal back anterior and membranes intact, and the cervix is 2 cm dilated. The correct management is:",
   "options": [
    "Immediate caesarean section",
    "Internal podalic version",
    "Oxytocin augmentation",
    "Await spontaneous correction"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A persistent transverse lie at term cannot deliver vaginally and risks cord prolapse and uterine rupture once membranes rupture, so delivery is by caesarean; internal podalic version is confined to the second twin.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_ABL_012",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "On vaginal examination in labour the anterior fontanelle is felt centrally with the sagittal suture transverse. This indicates:",
   "options": [
    "Occipito-anterior position",
    "Brow presentation",
    "Face presentation",
    "Deep transverse arrest with a deflexed head"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Feeling the anterior fontanelle centrally means the head is deflexed rather than well flexed, and a sagittal suture lying transverse at the level of the ischial spines with no progress defines deep transverse arrest.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
