   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A primigravida at term has a mento-posterior face presentation with no descent after 2 hours of good contractions. The appropriate management is:",
   "options": [
    "Forceps rotation",
    "Await rotation to mento-anterior",
    "Caesarean section",
    "Vacuum extraction"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "A persistent mento-posterior face cannot deliver vaginally because the fetal neck is already maximally extended and cannot extend further to negotiate the pelvic curve, unlike a mento-anterior face which can deliver by flexion.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_ABL_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In a brow presentation at term, which engaging diameter makes vaginal delivery of a normal-sized fetus impossible?",
   "options": [
    "Suboccipito-bregmatic",
    "Submento-bregmatic",
    "Mento-vertical, about 14 cm",
    "Occipito-frontal"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The mento-vertical diameter is the largest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head, so a persistent brow must either convert to a face or a vertex or be delivered by caesarean.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
