   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A woman with a previous caesarean scar in labour develops sudden severe abdominal pain, cessation of contractions, fetal bradycardia and a rising maternal pulse with vaginal bleeding. The diagnosis is:",
   "options": [
    "Placental abruption",
    "Amniotic fluid embolism",
    "Scar dehiscence with uterine rupture",
    "Vasa praevia"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Loss of contractions with an abnormally palpable fetal presenting part, maternal tachycardia and acute fetal distress in a scarred uterus indicates rupture, requiring immediate laparotomy.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
