  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_ABL_019",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "In a locked twin where the first twin is breech and the second cephalic, the heads impact at the pelvic brim. The correct management is:",
   "options": [
    "Oxytocin augmentation",
    "Vigorous traction on the first twin",
    "Destructive operation on the second twin",
    "Immediate caesarean section"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Locking is a rare obstetric emergency in which neither twin can descend; caesarean delivery is the safe modern approach, and vigorous traction risks decapitation and uterine rupture.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_ABL_020",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abnormal labour: malpresentation, obstructed labour, CPD",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A woman with untreated obstructed labour delivers and 7 days later develops continuous urinary leakage with a normal bladder capacity. The most likely lesion is:",
   "options": [
    "Vesicovaginal fistula from pressure necrosis",
    "Stress urinary incontinence",
    "Urethral injury from forceps",
    "Detrusor overactivity"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Prolonged impaction of the head compresses the bladder base against the symphysis, and the resulting ischaemic slough separates around the seventh to tenth day, producing a fistula rather than immediate leakage.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abnormal Labour",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
