   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, recurrent pregnancy loss",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A stable woman has a 2 cm unruptured tubal ectopic pregnancy, a beta-hCG of 1800 IU/L, no fetal cardiac activity and no free fluid. Methotrexate is chosen. Which follow-up finding indicates treatment success?",
   "options": [
    "Any fall in beta-hCG by day 2",
    "A fall in beta-hCG of at least 15 percent between days 4 and 7",
    "Resolution of pain within 24 hours",
    "Disappearance of the mass on day 7 ultrasound"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Beta-hCG characteristically rises between day 1 and day 4 before falling, so the day 4 to day 7 comparison is the accepted measure; separation pain around day 3 is expected and is not failure.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abortion and Ectopic Pregnancy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AEP_004",
