   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, recurrent pregnancy loss",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A woman with three consecutive first-trimester losses has a normal karyotype in both partners and a normal uterine cavity, but the lupus anticoagulant is positive on two occasions 12 weeks apart. The treatment that improves live birth rate is:",
   "options": [
    "Progesterone supplementation alone",
    "Low-dose aspirin with low molecular weight heparin from confirmation of pregnancy",
    "Corticosteroids alone",
    "Intravenous immunoglobulin"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Antiphospholipid syndrome is the only clearly treatable cause of recurrent miscarriage, and combined aspirin and heparin raises live birth rates substantially.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abortion and Ectopic Pregnancy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AEP_006",
