   "topic": "Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, recurrent pregnancy loss",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A woman presents with a mid-trimester loss preceded by painless cervical dilatation and bulging membranes, having had a similar loss previously. The management in a future pregnancy is:",
   "options": [
    "Bed rest alone",
    "History-indicated cervical cerclage at 12 to 14 weeks",
    "Tocolysis from 20 weeks",
    "Elective caesarean at term"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Cervical insufficiency causes painless dilatation without contractions, and a prophylactic cerclage placed early in the next pregnancy, with progesterone if the cervix shortens, is the accepted approach.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abortion and Ectopic Pregnancy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_AEP_007",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, recurrent pregnancy loss",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "Uterine evacuation for a second-trimester missed abortion is complicated by sudden collapse with bleeding from puncture sites. The likely diagnosis is:",
   "options": [
    "Amniotic fluid embolism",
    "Disseminated intravascular coagulation from a retained dead fetus",
    "Anaphylaxis to anaesthetic",
    "Uterine inversion"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Thromboplastins released from a fetus retained beyond about four weeks consume clotting factors, so coagulation should be checked before evacuating a long-standing missed abortion.",
   "reference": "DC Dutta's Textbook of Obstetrics 9e, Ch. Abortion and Ectopic Pregnancy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
