   "topic": "Contraception",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "A woman has a copper intrauterine device in situ and a positive pregnancy test with an intrauterine gestation she wishes to continue. The correct action is:",
   "options": [
    "Terminate the pregnancy",
    "Leave the device and give antibiotics",
    "Remove the device if the threads are visible",
    "Push the device higher into the fundus"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Leaving a device in place raises the risk of second-trimester loss, chorioamnionitis and preterm birth, so removal is advised when the threads are accessible.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Contraception",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_C_002",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Contraception",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which contraceptive method reduces the risk of both endometrial and ovarian carcinoma with increasing duration of use?",
   "options": [
    "Copper intrauterine device",
    "Combined oral contraceptive pill",
    "Male condom",
    "Progestogen-only injectable"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Suppression of ovulation and of endometrial proliferation gives roughly a 50 percent reduction in ovarian and endometrial cancer that persists for years after stopping, a key counselling point balanced against thrombotic risk.",
   "reference": "Shaw's Textbook of Gynaecology 17e, Ch. Contraception",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_OBG_C_003",
