   "topic": "Fluid & electrolyte therapy and diarrhoeal disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In severe dehydration in a child over 12 months, the recommended intravenous regimen is:",
   "options": [
    "Half-strength saline over 24 hours",
    "Normal saline 20 mL per kg once only",
    "Dextrose 5 percent 100 mL per kg over 6 hours",
    "Ringer lactate 100 mL per kg, giving 30 mL per kg in the first 30 minutes then 70 mL per kg over 2.5 hours"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Infants under 12 months receive the same total volume but more slowly, with the first 30 mL per kg over 1 hour, and reassessment guides repetition of the bolus.",
   "reference": "OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Diarrhoeal Diseases and Fluid Therapy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_DIA_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Pediatrics",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Fluid & electrolyte therapy and diarrhoeal disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 2-year-old with acute watery diarrhoea is brought by parents requesting a stool test and an antibiotic. He is alert with no dehydration. The correct advice is:",
   "options": [
    "Withhold feeds for 24 hours to rest the bowel",
    "Send a stool culture and start ciprofloxacin",
    "Give an antimotility agent to reduce stool frequency",
    "No stool test or antibiotic is needed; give oral rehydration solution, zinc and continued feeding"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Most acute watery diarrhoea is viral and self-limiting, routine stool culture changes nothing, antimotility drugs are contraindicated in children, and withholding feeds worsens nutrition and mucosal recovery.",
   "reference": "OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Diarrhoeal Diseases and Fluid Therapy",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PED_DIA_017",
