   "topic": "Fluid & electrolyte therapy and diarrhoeal disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Which fluid is preferred in a child with severe acute malnutrition who is in shock, and why?",
   "options": [
    "Half-strength Darrow solution with dextrose or Ringer lactate with dextrose, given cautiously because of the risk of cardiac failure",
    "Full-volume rapid Ringer lactate bolus as in a normally nourished child",
    "Normal saline 40 mL per kg rapidly",
    "Colloid"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Myocardial function and sodium handling are impaired in severe malnutrition, so slower, smaller boluses with close monitoring for pulmonary oedema are used, and ReSoMal is preferred for oral rehydration.",
