   "verified_on": "2026-09-01",
   "topic": "Fluid & electrolyte therapy and diarrhoeal disease in children",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 2-year-old has had loose stools for 18 days and has lost weight. Which management principle applies?",
   "options": [
    "Reduce lactose load with continued feeding, give micronutrients including zinc and vitamin A, and treat specific infections",
    "Withhold all feeds until the stools become formed",
    "Give a prolonged course of a broad-spectrum antibiotic",
    "Prescribe an antimotility agent to reduce frequency"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Persistent diarrhoea lasting 14 days or more carries high mortality because it is entangled with malnutrition, so nutritional rehabilitation is the treatment rather than antimicrobials. Continued feeding with a reduced lactose load, micronutrient replacement and treating identified pathogens such as Giardia or Shigella is the approach, and antimotility drugs are contraindicated in children.",
   "reference": "OP Ghai Essential Pediatrics 9e, Ch. Diarrhoeal Diseases and Fluid Therapy",
