   "topic": "Association & causation, bias, confounding, measures of risk",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Cases of a disease are recruited from a specialist hospital while controls come from the general community. Which bias is most likely?",
   "options": [
    "Interviewer bias",
    "Recall bias",
    "Selection bias, specifically Berkson bias",
    "Lead-time bias"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "Hospital cases differ systematically from community cases in severity, comorbidity and referral pattern, which distorts the exposure distribution independently of any true association.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Epidemiology",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ACB_005",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Association & causation, bias, confounding, measures of risk",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "In a case-control study of birth defects, mothers of affected infants remember drug exposure more completely than mothers of healthy infants. This is:",
   "options": [
    "Recall bias, a form of information bias",
    "Confounding",
    "Selection bias",
    "Random error"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "Differential accuracy of recall between groups biases the exposure estimate, and it is reduced by using records rather than memory, by blinding interviewers and by using diseased controls.",
   "reference": "Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine 27e, Ch. Epidemiology",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PSM_ACB_006",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "PSM (Community Medicine)",
