   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A bright 8-year-old reads far below her class level and reverses letters, with normal vision, hearing and intelligence. What is the diagnosis and the intervention?",
   "options": [
    "Intellectual disability, managed with special school",
    "Specific learning disorder with impairment in reading, managed with remedial teaching and examination accommodations",
    "Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, managed with a stimulant",
    "Autism spectrum disorder, managed with behaviour therapy"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "The gap between measured ability and academic achievement is the defining feature, and recognition matters because untreated children develop secondary demoralisation, school refusal and conduct problems.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Child and Adolescent Psychiatry",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_014",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 7-year-old refuses school with morning abdominal pain and cries when the mother leaves, but plays normally at home during the day. What is the diagnosis and the management principle?",
   "options": [
    "Conduct disorder, managed by discipline",
    "Separation anxiety disorder, managed by graded early return to school with parental counselling",
    "Truancy, managed by punishment",
    "Depression, managed with an antidepressant alone"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Prolonged absence entrenches the avoidance, so a rapid graded return is the priority, and truancy is distinguished by the child concealing the absence and not being distressed by separation.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Child and Adolescent Psychiatry",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_CHI_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Child psychiatry, psychopharmacology, ECT & psychotherapies",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A 10-year-old has multiple motor tics and involuntary throat clearing and grunting for over a year. What is the diagnosis and which drug class is used if treatment is needed?",
   "options": [
    "Absence epilepsy, treated with valproate",
    "Obsessive-compulsive disorder, treated with an SSRI",
    "Sydenham chorea, treated with penicillin",
    "Tourette syndrome, treated when disabling with an alpha-2 agonist such as clonidine or a low-dose antipsychotic"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Both motor and vocal tics for more than a year are required, many children need only psychoeducation and habit reversal therapy, and comorbid attention deficit and obsessive symptoms are often more disabling than the tics.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Child and Adolescent Psychiatry",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
