   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "A hypertensive diabetic man has cognitive decline that worsened abruptly on two occasions, with brisk reflexes and a gait disorder. What is the diagnosis and the key intervention?",
   "options": [
    "Vascular dementia, in which control of vascular risk factors can slow further decline",
    "Alzheimer disease, treated with donepezil alone",
    "Frontotemporal dementia",
    "Dementia with Lewy bodies"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "A stepwise course with focal neurological signs and imaging evidence of infarcts distinguishes it, and because the mechanism is vascular, blood pressure, glycaemia, lipids and antiplatelet therapy are the treatment.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_012",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "An elderly man has gait apraxia, urinary incontinence and cognitive slowing with dilated ventricles and no significant cortical atrophy. What is the diagnosis and the treatment?",
   "options": [
    "Normal pressure hydrocephalus, potentially reversible by ventriculoperitoneal shunting",
    "Alzheimer disease, treated with a cholinesterase inhibitor",
    "Vascular dementia, treated with an antiplatelet",
    "Parkinson disease dementia"
   ],
   "correct": 0,
   "concept": "The gait disturbance usually appears first and responds best to shunting, and a large-volume lumbar puncture that transiently improves gait is used to select patients likely to benefit.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_013",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "H",
   "stem": "A 62-year-old has dementia progressing over 4 months with startle myoclonus and periodic sharp waves on the electroencephalogram. What is the diagnosis?",
   "options": [
    "Alzheimer disease",
    "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease",
    "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
    "Neurosyphilis"
   ],
   "correct": 1,
   "concept": "Rapid progression over months rather than years with myoclonus is the clue, supported by raised cerebrospinal fluid protein markers and cortical ribboning on diffusion-weighted imaging, and no treatment alters the course.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_014",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Which drug is added for moderate to severe Alzheimer disease and by what mechanism?",
   "options": [
    "Haloperidol, which blocks D2 receptors",
    "Levodopa, which replaces dopamine",
    "Memantine, a low-affinity NMDA receptor antagonist that reduces glutamatergic excitotoxicity",
    "Selegiline, which inhibits monoamine oxidase B"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "It can be combined with a cholinesterase inhibitor, and the benefit of both classes is modest and symptomatic rather than disease-modifying, which should be made clear when counselling families.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_015",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
