   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "M",
   "stem": "Why is hypoactive delirium more often missed than the hyperactive form, and why does this matter?",
   "options": [
    "It resolves without any treatment",
    "It occurs only in children",
    "It has no fluctuation in attention",
    "The patient is quiet and withdrawn rather than agitated so is mistaken for depressed or simply tired, yet the hypoactive form carries the worse prognosis"
   ],
   "correct": 3,
   "concept": "Routine use of a brief attention test such as reciting the months backwards detects it, and the underlying cause of infection, drugs, retention, pain or metabolic derangement must still be found and treated.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_016",
   "part": "B",
   "subject": "Psychiatry",
   "source": "ai",
   "verified": "Y",
   "verified_by": "model-audit",
   "topic": "Delirium, dementia & organic mental disorders",
   "difficulty": "E",
   "stem": "Which pathological findings characterise Alzheimer disease?",
   "options": [
    "Tau-negative ubiquitin inclusions in the frontal lobes",
    "Lewy bodies in the substantia nigra",
    "Extracellular amyloid beta plaques and intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau neurofibrillary tangles, with early medial temporal and hippocampal atrophy",
    "Multiple lacunar infarcts"
   ],
   "correct": 2,
   "concept": "The cholinergic deficit from loss of neurons in the basal forebrain is the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors, and the early hippocampal involvement explains why recent memory fails first.",
   "reference": "Ahuja A Short Textbook of Psychiatry 8e, Ch. Organic Mental Disorders",
   "verified_on": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q_PS_DEL_017",
